window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"f82b690ec586ae16f987e631d5a988bd9d52fb1b9a7665d904c06308914ab463":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.l112.qi2\"><\/a><a id=\"cm-normal-smoothing\"><\/a>\n## Averaging gives normal functionals, including on increasing nets\n\nUse left Haar measure \\(dt\\), and set \\(T_s\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\beta_{s^{-1}}\\). These are linear isometries of \\(P_*\\), with\n\\[\n T_sT_t=T_{st}.\n \\tag{QI6}\n\\]\nIndeed, composition on the right reverses the two inverse parameters. Define, initially for \\(f\\in C_c(G)\\) and \\(\\omega\\in P_*\\),\n\\[\n \\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x)\n   =\\int_G f(t)\\,\\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}(x))\\,dt.\n \\tag{QI7}\n\\]\nQI1 gives scalar measurability, and \\(|\\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}(x))|\\leq\\|\\omega\\|\\|x\\|\\), so this is an absolutely convergent scalar integral and\n\\[\n \\|\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\|\\leq\\|f\\|_1\\|\\omega\\|.\n \\tag{QI8}\n\\]\nWe do not presume that \\(t\\mapsto T_t\\omega\\) is norm measurable or already continuous.\n\n**Lemma QI2.** The functional \\(\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\) belongs to \\(P_*\\). Furthermore\n\\[\n T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega}=\\Omega_{L_sf,\\omega},\n \\qquad L_sf(t)=f(s^{-1}t),\n \\tag{QI9}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\|T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega}-\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\|\n \\leq\\|L_sf-f\\|_1\\|\\omega\\|\\longrightarrow0\n \\quad(s\\to e).\n \\tag{QI10}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.** First take \\(f\\geq0\\) and \\(\\omega\\geq0\\). The integral is positive. For a bounded increasing positive sequence \\(x_n\\uparrow x\\), normality of each \\(\\beta_{t^{-1}}\\) and \\(\\omega\\), followed by scalar monotone convergence, gives\n\\(\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_n)\\uparrow\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x)\\). Scalar monotone convergence is [SC4](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-04).\n\nHere is the additional step for an arbitrary bounded increasing positive net \\(x_i\\uparrow x\\). Normality of the faithful state \\(\\varphi\\) from (QI4) gives \\(\\varphi(x_i)\\uparrow\\varphi(x)\\). Choose indices \\(i_n\\), increasing in the directed set, with\n\\(\\varphi(x-x_{i_n})<1/n\\). This is possible by directedness and the scalar supremum. Let \\(y=\\sup_n x_{i_n}\\). Bounded increasing positive operators have their strong supremum in \\(P\\); it lies below \\(x\\). Normality of \\(\\varphi\\) and the displayed bounds give \\(\\varphi(x-y)=0\\), so faithfulness makes \\(y=x\\). Applying the sequential conclusion yields\n\\[\n \\sup_i\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_i)\n \\geq\\sup_n\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_{i_n})\n =\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x).\n\\]\nPositivity gives the opposite inequality. Thus the functional preserves all bounded increasing positive suprema. [NF1–4](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.4) proves that such a bounded positive functional is ultraweakly continuous. Hence it belongs to \\(P_*\\).\n\nFor general \\(\\omega\\), polarize the square-summable vector series in CP4–6. The identity, with inner products linear in the first variable,\n\\[\n \\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n =\\frac14\\sum_{k=0}^3 i^k\n       \\langle x(\\xi+i^k\\eta),\\xi+i^k\\eta\\rangle\n \\tag{QI11}\n\\]\nexpresses it as a linear combination of four positive normal vector-series functionals: each of the four squared-norm sums is finite. Decompose the real and imaginary parts of \\(f\\) into positive and negative parts, still in \\(C_c(G)\\). Linearity reduces (QI7) to the positive case.\n\nFinally,\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega})(x)\n &=\\int_G f(t)\\,\n       \\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}\\beta_{s^{-1}}(x))\\,dt\\\\\n &=\\int_G f(t)\\,\n       \\omega(\\beta_{(st)^{-1}}(x))\\,dt\n =\\Omega_{L_sf,\\omega}(x).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last substitution is \\(r=st\\), so left invariance introduces no modular factor. Inequality (QI8) gives (QI10), and the full left-translation continuity is proved in [L24 Lemma 3.1](OA-FLOW-L24.md#oa-flow.grp.translations). Thus every averaged functional has a norm-continuous orbit before continuity has been established for general normal functionals. \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>Averaging gives normal functionals, including on increasing nets<\/h2>\n<p>Use left Haar measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dt\\)\">\\(dt\\)<\/span>, and set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_s\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\beta_{s^{-1}}\\)\">\\(T_s\\omega=\\omega\\circ\\beta_{s^{-1}}\\)<\/span>. These are linear isometries of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_*\\)\">\\(P_*\\)<\/span>, with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_sT_t=T_{st}.\n \\tag{QI6}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_sT_t=T_{st}.\n \\tag{QI6}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed, composition on the right reverses the two inverse parameters. Define, initially for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in C_c(G)\\)\">\\(f\\in C_c(G)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in P_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in P_*\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x)\n   =\\int_G f(t)\\,\\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}(x))\\,dt.\n \\tag{QI7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x)\n   =\\int_G f(t)\\,\\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}(x))\\,dt.\n \\tag{QI7}\n\\]<\/span>\nQI1 gives scalar measurability, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}(x))|\\leq\\|\\omega\\|\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(|\\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}(x))|\\leq\\|\\omega\\|\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>, so this is an absolutely convergent scalar integral and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\|\\leq\\|f\\|_1\\|\\omega\\|.\n \\tag{QI8}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\|\\leq\\|f\\|_1\\|\\omega\\|.\n \\tag{QI8}\n\\]<\/span>\nWe do not presume that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto T_t\\omega\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto T_t\\omega\\)<\/span> is norm measurable or already continuous.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma QI2.<\/strong> The functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\)\">\\(\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\)<\/span> belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_*\\)\">\\(P_*\\)<\/span>. Furthermore\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega}=\\Omega_{L_sf,\\omega},\n \\qquad L_sf(t)=f(s^{-1}t),\n \\tag{QI9}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega}=\\Omega_{L_sf,\\omega},\n \\qquad L_sf(t)=f(s^{-1}t),\n \\tag{QI9}\n\\]<\/span>\nand\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega}-\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\|\n \\leq\\|L_sf-f\\|_1\\|\\omega\\|\\longrightarrow0\n \\quad(s\\to e).\n \\tag{QI10}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega}-\\Omega_{f,\\omega}\\|\n \\leq\\|L_sf-f\\|_1\\|\\omega\\|\\longrightarrow0\n \\quad(s\\to e).\n \\tag{QI10}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> First take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\geq0\\)\">\\(f\\geq0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\geq0\\)\">\\(\\omega\\geq0\\)<\/span>. The integral is positive. For a bounded increasing positive sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_n\\uparrow x\\)\">\\(x_n\\uparrow x\\)<\/span>, normality of each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_{t^{-1}}\\)\">\\(\\beta_{t^{-1}}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>, followed by scalar monotone convergence, gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_n)\\uparrow\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x)\\)\">\\(\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_n)\\uparrow\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x)\\)<\/span>. Scalar monotone convergence is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-04\">SC4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>Here is the additional step for an arbitrary bounded increasing positive net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_i\\uparrow x\\)\">\\(x_i\\uparrow x\\)<\/span>. Normality of the faithful state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> from (QI4) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(x_i)\\uparrow\\varphi(x)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(x_i)\\uparrow\\varphi(x)\\)<\/span>. Choose indices <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i_n\\)\">\\(i_n\\)<\/span>, increasing in the directed set, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(x-x_{i_n})&lt;1/n\\)\">\\(\\varphi(x-x_{i_n})&lt;1/n\\)<\/span>. This is possible by directedness and the scalar supremum. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=\\sup_n x_{i_n}\\)\">\\(y=\\sup_n x_{i_n}\\)<\/span>. Bounded increasing positive operators have their strong supremum in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>; it lies below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>. Normality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> and the displayed bounds give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(x-y)=0\\)\">\\(\\varphi(x-y)=0\\)<\/span>, so faithfulness makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=x\\)\">\\(y=x\\)<\/span>. Applying the sequential conclusion yields\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sup_i\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_i)\n \\geq\\sup_n\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_{i_n})\n =\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x).\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sup_i\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_i)\n \\geq\\sup_n\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x_{i_n})\n =\\Omega_{f,\\omega}(x).\n\\]<\/span>\nPositivity gives the opposite inequality. Thus the functional preserves all bounded increasing positive suprema. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.4\">NF1–4<\/a> proves that such a bounded positive functional is ultraweakly continuous. Hence it belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_*\\)\">\\(P_*\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For general <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>, polarize the square-summable vector series in CP4–6. The identity, with inner products linear in the first variable,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n =\\frac14\\sum_{k=0}^3 i^k\n       \\langle x(\\xi+i^k\\eta),\\xi+i^k\\eta\\rangle\n \\tag{QI11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n =\\frac14\\sum_{k=0}^3 i^k\n       \\langle x(\\xi+i^k\\eta),\\xi+i^k\\eta\\rangle\n \\tag{QI11}\n\\]<\/span>\nexpresses it as a linear combination of four positive normal vector-series functionals: each of the four squared-norm sums is finite. Decompose the real and imaginary parts of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> into positive and negative parts, still in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_c(G)\\)\">\\(C_c(G)\\)<\/span>. Linearity reduces (QI7) to the positive case.<\/p>\n<p>Finally,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega})(x)\n &amp;=\\int_G f(t)\\,\n       \\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}\\beta_{s^{-1}}(x))\\,dt\\\\\n &amp;=\\int_G f(t)\\,\n       \\omega(\\beta_{(st)^{-1}}(x))\\,dt\n =\\Omega_{L_sf,\\omega}(x).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (T_s\\Omega_{f,\\omega})(x)\n &amp;=\\int_G f(t)\\,\n       \\omega(\\beta_{t^{-1}}\\beta_{s^{-1}}(x))\\,dt\\\\\n &amp;=\\int_G f(t)\\,\n       \\omega(\\beta_{(st)^{-1}}(x))\\,dt\n =\\Omega_{L_sf,\\omega}(x).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last substitution is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=st\\)\">\\(r=st\\)<\/span>, so left invariance introduces no modular factor. Inequality (QI8) gives (QI10), and the full left-translation continuity is proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L24.html#oa-flow.grp.translations\">L24 Lemma 3.1<\/a>. Thus every averaged functional has a norm-continuous orbit before continuity has been established for general normal functionals. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L112.md","start_offset":11486,"end_offset":15046,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f8204f89d2089295ddb997e5e1e2e50bd56910a9b427083fba61de7c283c5aca":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l118-model\"><\/a>\n## An exact extension rectangle\n\nLet $Y=\\{y_1,y_2\\}$ have masses $1/3,2/3$, and let $X=\\{x_{11},x_{12},x_{21},x_{22}\\}$ with $q(x_{ij})=y_i$. All functions are continuous, and this finite map is open and onto. Every state extension has weights\n\n<a id=\"equation-ae11\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\big(u,\\tfrac13-u,v,\\tfrac23-v\\big),\n \\qquad 0\\le u\\le\\tfrac13,\\quad0\\le v\\le\\tfrac23.\n \\tag{AE11}\n\\]\n\nThe fibre is exactly this rectangle, by positivity and the two fixed marginal sums. Its four vertices are extreme: a proper convex combination equal to a vertex forces equality in each endpoint coordinate for both summands. Any other point is not extreme, since at least one coordinate admits two distinct feasible perturbations. At a vertex precisely one point in each fibre has positive mass. Its GNS space is two-dimensional; $\\mathcal A=\\mathcal B=\\mathbb C^2$ there. The extension on the original $L^2(Y,\\nu)$ simply chooses the corresponding two coordinates of a function on $X$. Its generated algebra stays the original diagonal $\\mathbb C^2$.\n\nAt the balanced point $(u,v)=(1/6,1/3)$, the weights are $(1/6,1/6,1/3,1/3)$. Its own GNS space is $\\mathbb C^4$ with inner product $\\sum_\\ell w_\\ell\\xi_\\ell\\overline{\\eta_\\ell}$. Here $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb C^4$, whereas $\\mathcal B=\\{(a,a,b,b):a,b\\in\\mathbb C\\}\\cong\\mathbb C^2$, and $H_0$ has dimension two. The state-preserving expectation averages within each fibre. Thus\n\n<a id=\"equation-ae12\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n h=(1,-1,0,0),\\quad P(h)=0,\\quad\\|h\\|=1,\n \\qquad\n \\psi_+:(\\tfrac13,0,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13),\\quad\n \\psi_-:(0,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13).\n \\tag{AE12}\n\\]\n\nBoth new states keep the marginals, average to the balanced one, and differ at $h$ by $2/3$. Each has three-point support, so its own generated algebra is $\\mathbb C^3$; these horizontal boundary points must not be labelled as vertices with $\\mathbb C^2$. This example displays the kernel mechanism and the change of GNS space. It supplies no measurable choice of representatives for general fibres.\n\n**Problem 2.** Verify the state difference and the expectation in this model.\n\n**Solution.** On the first two coordinates $P$ sends $(a_1,a_2)$ to $((a_1+a_2)/2,(a_1+a_2)/2)$, and does the same on the last two. Hence $P(h)=0$. The balanced state has $\\psi(h^2)=1/3$, so $(\\psi_+-\\psi_-)(h)=2\\psi(h^2)=2/3$. Multiplying the balanced weights by $1+h$ and $1-h$ gives the two weights in (AE12), which are positive and total one. Their fibre sums are still $1/3,2/3$.\n\n**Problem 3.** Why does the compact-carrier proof not assume that $Y$ is sigma compact?\n\n**Solution.** The increasing compact sets exhaust the mass of one finite Radon measure, not the space. Their complement may be large but is null for this representation. A different state may need different compact carriers. The final family of support corners can be uncountable; (AE9) uses finite subsets and each vector's square-norm tails, not a countable decomposition of the algebra.","html":"<h2>An exact extension rectangle<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y=\\{y_1,y_2\\}\\)\">\\(Y=\\{y_1,y_2\\}\\)<\/span> have masses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/3,2/3\\)\">\\(1/3,2/3\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=\\{x_{11},x_{12},x_{21},x_{22}\\}\\)\">\\(X=\\{x_{11},x_{12},x_{21},x_{22}\\}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(x_{ij})=y_i\\)\">\\(q(x_{ij})=y_i\\)<\/span>. All functions are continuous, and this finite map is open and onto. Every state extension has weights<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\big(u,\\tfrac13-u,v,\\tfrac23-v\\big),\n \\qquad 0\\le u\\le\\tfrac13,\\quad0\\le v\\le\\tfrac23.\n \\tag{AE11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\big(u,\\tfrac13-u,v,\\tfrac23-v\\big),\n \\qquad 0\\le u\\le\\tfrac13,\\quad0\\le v\\le\\tfrac23.\n \\tag{AE11}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The fibre is exactly this rectangle, by positivity and the two fixed marginal sums. Its four vertices are extreme: a proper convex combination equal to a vertex forces equality in each endpoint coordinate for both summands. Any other point is not extreme, since at least one coordinate admits two distinct feasible perturbations. At a vertex precisely one point in each fibre has positive mass. Its GNS space is two-dimensional; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A=\\mathcal B=\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A=\\mathcal B=\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span> there. The extension on the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(Y,\\nu)\\)\">\\(L^2(Y,\\nu)\\)<\/span> simply chooses the corresponding two coordinates of a function on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>. Its generated algebra stays the original diagonal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>At the balanced point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((u,v)=(1/6,1/3)\\)\">\\((u,v)=(1/6,1/3)\\)<\/span>, the weights are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((1/6,1/6,1/3,1/3)\\)\">\\((1/6,1/6,1/3,1/3)\\)<\/span>. Its own GNS space is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^4\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^4\\)<\/span> with inner product <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_\\ell w_\\ell\\xi_\\ell\\overline{\\eta_\\ell}\\)\">\\(\\sum_\\ell w_\\ell\\xi_\\ell\\overline{\\eta_\\ell}\\)<\/span>. Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A=\\mathbb C^4\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A=\\mathbb C^4\\)<\/span>, whereas <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal B=\\{(a,a,b,b):a,b\\in\\mathbb C\\}\\cong\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathcal B=\\{(a,a,b,b):a,b\\in\\mathbb C\\}\\cong\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_0\\)\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span> has dimension two. The state-preserving expectation averages within each fibre. Thus<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n h=(1,-1,0,0),\\quad P(h)=0,\\quad\\|h\\|=1,\n \\qquad\n \\psi_+:(\\tfrac13,0,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13),\\quad\n \\psi_-:(0,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13).\n \\tag{AE12}\n\\]\">\\[\n h=(1,-1,0,0),\\quad P(h)=0,\\quad\\|h\\|=1,\n \\qquad\n \\psi_+:(\\tfrac13,0,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13),\\quad\n \\psi_-:(0,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13).\n \\tag{AE12}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Both new states keep the marginals, average to the balanced one, and differ at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2/3\\)\">\\(2/3\\)<\/span>. Each has three-point support, so its own generated algebra is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^3\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^3\\)<\/span>; these horizontal boundary points must not be labelled as vertices with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>. This example displays the kernel mechanism and the change of GNS space. It supplies no measurable choice of representatives for general fibres.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem 2.<\/strong> Verify the state difference and the expectation in this model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> On the first two coordinates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a_1,a_2)\\)\">\\((a_1,a_2)\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(((a_1+a_2)/2,(a_1+a_2)/2)\\)\">\\(((a_1+a_2)/2,(a_1+a_2)/2)\\)<\/span>, and does the same on the last two. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P(h)=0\\)\">\\(P(h)=0\\)<\/span>. The balanced state has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(h^2)=1/3\\)\">\\(\\psi(h^2)=1/3\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\psi_+-\\psi_-)(h)=2\\psi(h^2)=2/3\\)\">\\((\\psi_+-\\psi_-)(h)=2\\psi(h^2)=2/3\\)<\/span>. Multiplying the balanced weights by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1+h\\)\">\\(1+h\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-h\\)\">\\(1-h\\)<\/span> gives the two weights in (AE12), which are positive and total one. Their fibre sums are still <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/3,2/3\\)\">\\(1/3,2/3\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem 3.<\/strong> Why does the compact-carrier proof not assume that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> is sigma compact?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The increasing compact sets exhaust the mass of one finite Radon measure, not the space. Their complement may be large but is null for this representation. A different state may need different compact carriers. The final family of support corners can be uncountable; (AE9) uses finite subsets and each vector's square-norm tails, not a countable decomposition of the algebra.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L118.md","start_offset":35358,"end_offset":38314,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f805b0337ed59bde19c5af6f6cf044a3e5e24f527c72b71d66199d11b536ba87":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l127-figure\"><\/a><a id=\"oa-flow.l127.figure\"><\/a>\n\n## Exact character normalization on five integer parameters\n\n![Exact scalar phases, character values and normalized diagonal implementers](../assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization.png)\n\nThe model is \\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\), \\(G=\\mathbb Z\\), \\(\\alpha_n=\\operatorname{Ad}(W_n)\\), and \\(W_n=\\operatorname{diag}(1,(-1)^n)\\). Its fixed center \\(D\\) consists of diagonal matrices and the chosen character is \\(\\chi(\\operatorname{diag}(a,b))=a\\). Set \\(\\zeta=e^{i\\pi/4}\\). The raw implementer is \\(v_n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\zeta^{n^2},(-1)^n\\zeta^{n^2})\\). The figure displays the exact values at \\(n=0,1,2,3,4\\), their character values \\(\\zeta^{n^2}\\), the scalar corrections \\(\\overline{\\chi(v_n)}\\), and the resulting \\(w_n=W_n\\).\n\nEvery phase is recorded as an integer exponent modulo eight in units of \\(\\pi/4\\); the circle coordinates show those exact phase directions. Solid blue and dashed orange rays represent the first and second diagonal entries. Distinct ray lengths make coincident phases visible and do not represent different moduli: every entry has modulus one.\n\nThe displayed defect is exact: \\(v_1^2=iI_2\\), \\(v_2=-I_2\\), and \\(v_1^2=(-i)v_2\\), so \\(\\lambda(1,1)=-i\\). In general the exponent calculation gives \\(\\lambda(m,n)=\\zeta^{-2mn}\\). Character evaluation and its inverse phase cancel it, giving \\(w_mw_n=w_{m+n}\\). In this example \\(K=G=\\mathbb Z\\), so the extension is already \\(r_n=W_n\\); its adjoint cancels the original action on all four matrix units. A proper subgroup still needs the complete extension theorem.\n\nThe full proofs are [CN0 the character algebra](#cn0), [CN1 exact normalization and uniqueness](#cn1), [CN2 the topology](#cn2-topology), [CN3 the extension](#cn3), [CT full normal continuity](#ct), [CN4 both kernel inclusions](#cn4), and [CN6 the exact model](#cn6).\n\nThe [renderer](../assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/render_character_normalization.py), [exact data](../assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization-data.json), [editable SVG](../assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization.svg), and [font notice](../assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/FONT-LICENSE.txt) accompany the diagram. The example and all integer phase identities are proved in CN6.","html":"<h2>Exact character normalization on five integer parameters<\/h2>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization.png\" alt=\"Exact scalar phases, character values and normalized diagonal implementers\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The model is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(G=\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_n=\\operatorname{Ad}(W_n)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_n=\\operatorname{Ad}(W_n)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_n=\\operatorname{diag}(1,(-1)^n)\\)\">\\(W_n=\\operatorname{diag}(1,(-1)^n)\\)<\/span>. Its fixed center <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> consists of diagonal matrices and the chosen character is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi(\\operatorname{diag}(a,b))=a\\)\">\\(\\chi(\\operatorname{diag}(a,b))=a\\)<\/span>. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta=e^{i\\pi/4}\\)\">\\(\\zeta=e^{i\\pi/4}\\)<\/span>. The raw implementer is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\zeta^{n^2},(-1)^n\\zeta^{n^2})\\)\">\\(v_n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\zeta^{n^2},(-1)^n\\zeta^{n^2})\\)<\/span>. The figure displays the exact values at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=0,1,2,3,4\\)\">\\(n=0,1,2,3,4\\)<\/span>, their character values <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta^{n^2}\\)\">\\(\\zeta^{n^2}\\)<\/span>, the scalar corrections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline{\\chi(v_n)}\\)\">\\(\\overline{\\chi(v_n)}\\)<\/span>, and the resulting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_n=W_n\\)\">\\(w_n=W_n\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Every phase is recorded as an integer exponent modulo eight in units of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi/4\\)\">\\(\\pi/4\\)<\/span>; the circle coordinates show those exact phase directions. Solid blue and dashed orange rays represent the first and second diagonal entries. Distinct ray lengths make coincident phases visible and do not represent different moduli: every entry has modulus one.<\/p>\n<p>The displayed defect is exact: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_1^2=iI_2\\)\">\\(v_1^2=iI_2\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_2=-I_2\\)\">\\(v_2=-I_2\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_1^2=(-i)v_2\\)\">\\(v_1^2=(-i)v_2\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(1,1)=-i\\)\">\\(\\lambda(1,1)=-i\\)<\/span>. In general the exponent calculation gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(m,n)=\\zeta^{-2mn}\\)\">\\(\\lambda(m,n)=\\zeta^{-2mn}\\)<\/span>. Character evaluation and its inverse phase cancel it, giving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_mw_n=w_{m+n}\\)\">\\(w_mw_n=w_{m+n}\\)<\/span>. In this example <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=G=\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(K=G=\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, so the extension is already <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_n=W_n\\)\">\\(r_n=W_n\\)<\/span>; its adjoint cancels the original action on all four matrix units. A proper subgroup still needs the complete extension theorem.<\/p>\n<p>The full proofs are <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cn0\">CN0 the character algebra<\/a>, <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cn1\">CN1 exact normalization and uniqueness<\/a>, <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cn2-topology\">CN2 the topology<\/a>, <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cn3\">CN3 the extension<\/a>, <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#ct\">CT full normal continuity<\/a>, <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cn4\">CN4 both kernel inclusions<\/a>, and <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cn6\">CN6 the exact model<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/render_character_normalization.py\">renderer<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization-data.json\">exact data<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/character-normalization.svg\">editable SVG<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/character-normalization-exact-cocycle-kernel/figure/FONT-LICENSE.txt\">font notice<\/a> accompany the diagram. The example and all integer phase identities are proved in CN6.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L127.md","start_offset":20827,"end_offset":23243,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f87f79be7cff1b9be919f7a19f792cf2649d86ef445ca6ff9a434eda1889df5f":{"markdown":"# Tensoring a weight with the usual trace\n\n*Original exposition and illustration sources: CC0 1.0; font components retain their accompanying terms.*\n\nThe usual trace assigns value one to a rank-one projection. We retain this normalization when adjoining a Hilbert-space factor to a von Neumann algebra with a weight. Finite matrix corners construct the weight on every positive operator, and its GNS coordinates determine the complete modular and relative domains. The Hilbert-space factor may have an uncountable orthonormal basis.\n\n<a id=\"tw-setting\"><\/a>\n## Setting and conventions\n\nLet $M\\ne0$ be a von Neumann algebra, let $\\varphi$ be a faithful normal semifinite weight on $M$, and let $E\\ne0$ be any complex Hilbert space. Fix an arbitrary orthonormal basis $(e_i)_{i\\in I}$ of $E$. No countability assumption is made on $I$, on a faithful representation of $M$, or on its predual. Set\n\\[\n B=M\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(E).\n \\tag{TW.0.a}\n\\]\nIn a faithful normal representation of $M$ on $K$, the entry $X_{ij}\\in M$ of $X\\in B$ is specified by\n\\[\n \\langle X(\\xi\\otimes e_j),\\eta\\otimes e_i\\rangle\n       =\\langle X_{ij}\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n \\qquad(\\xi,\\eta\\in K).\n \\tag{TW.0.b}\n\\]\nInner products are linear in their first variable. In particular, the rank-one operator $|u\\rangle\\langle v|$ sends $w$ to $\\langle w,v\\rangle u$.\n\nThe finite ideal $\\mathfrak n_\\varphi$, finite linear domain $\\mathfrak m_\\varphi$, and full GNS map $\\Lambda_\\varphi$ are those proved in [Finite ideals and GNS spaces for arbitrary weights](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1). Its representation is [faithful and normal](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.5). We use the actual closed finite-star operator $S_\\varphi$, modular operator $\\Delta_\\varphi$, and modular conjugation $J_\\varphi$ of [The full finite-star algebra](OA-FLOW-WR.md#wr-3) and [Modular covariance and its implementation](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4). All domain equalities below concern these complete operators.\n\nEvery sum of nonnegative scalars indexed by an arbitrary set means the supremum of its finite subsums. The same convention applies to sums of nonnegative squared Hilbert norms. Finite subsets and finite rectangles are directed by inclusion. We will construct, rather than assume, the weight\n\\[\n \\Omega(X)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\varphi(X_{ii})\n       \\qquad(X\\in B_+),\n \\tag{TW.0.c}\n\\]\nshow that it is independent of the chosen basis, and denote it by $\\varphi\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}_E$.\n\nIf $M=0$ or $E=0$, the tensor algebra and its GNS Hilbert space are zero and the weight is zero. The corresponding formulas have their vacuous zero-space interpretation. Rank-one normalization and the nonzero faithful models below concern the stated nonzero case.","html":"<h1>Tensoring a weight with the usual trace<\/h1>\n<p><em>Original exposition and illustration sources: CC0 1.0; font components retain their accompanying terms.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The usual trace assigns value one to a rank-one projection. We retain this normalization when adjoining a Hilbert-space factor to a von Neumann algebra with a weight. Finite matrix corners construct the weight on every positive operator, and its GNS coordinates determine the complete modular and relative domains. The Hilbert-space factor may have an uncountable orthonormal basis.<\/p>\n<h2>Setting and conventions<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\ne0\\)\">\\(M\\ne0\\)<\/span> be a von Neumann algebra, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> be a faithful normal semifinite weight on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\ne0\\)\">\\(E\\ne0\\)<\/span> be any complex Hilbert space. Fix an arbitrary orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((e_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>. No countability assumption is made on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>, on a faithful representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, or on its predual. Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n B=M\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(E).\n \\tag{TW.0.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n B=M\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(E).\n \\tag{TW.0.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn a faithful normal representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, the entry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_{ij}\\in M\\)\">\\(X_{ij}\\in M\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\in B\\)\">\\(X\\in B\\)<\/span> is specified by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\langle X(\\xi\\otimes e_j),\\eta\\otimes e_i\\rangle\n       =\\langle X_{ij}\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n \\qquad(\\xi,\\eta\\in K).\n \\tag{TW.0.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\langle X(\\xi\\otimes e_j),\\eta\\otimes e_i\\rangle\n       =\\langle X_{ij}\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n \\qquad(\\xi,\\eta\\in K).\n \\tag{TW.0.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nInner products are linear in their first variable. In particular, the rank-one operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|u\\rangle\\langle v|\\)\">\\(|u\\rangle\\langle v|\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle w,v\\rangle u\\)\">\\(\\langle w,v\\rangle u\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The finite ideal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak n_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, finite linear domain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak m_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak m_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, and full GNS map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\varphi\\)<\/span> are those proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">Finite ideals and GNS spaces for arbitrary weights<\/a>. Its representation is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.5\">faithful and normal<\/a>. We use the actual closed finite-star operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_\\varphi\\)\">\\(S_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, modular operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\Delta_\\varphi\\)<\/span>, and modular conjugation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_\\varphi\\)\">\\(J_\\varphi\\)<\/span> of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#wr-3\">The full finite-star algebra<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">Modular covariance and its implementation<\/a>. All domain equalities below concern these complete operators.<\/p>\n<p>Every sum of nonnegative scalars indexed by an arbitrary set means the supremum of its finite subsums. The same convention applies to sums of nonnegative squared Hilbert norms. Finite subsets and finite rectangles are directed by inclusion. We will construct, rather than assume, the weight\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Omega(X)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\varphi(X_{ii})\n       \\qquad(X\\in B_+),\n \\tag{TW.0.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Omega(X)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\varphi(X_{ii})\n       \\qquad(X\\in B_+),\n \\tag{TW.0.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nshow that it is independent of the chosen basis, and denote it by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=0\\)\">\\(M=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=0\\)\">\\(E=0\\)<\/span>, the tensor algebra and its GNS Hilbert space are zero and the weight is zero. The corresponding formulas have their vacuous zero-space interpretation. Rank-one normalization and the nonzero faithful models below concern the stated nonzero case.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-TW.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":2688,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f8b1d75063914cdcb83644fdfab6528e98a7e7b8e2844cac732dcaec86c6065d":{"markdown":"<a id=\"tp-8\"><\/a>\n## 8. Solved period diagnostics\n\n**A basic period can disappear while a multiple survives.** In (TP32), \\(p=\\pi/c\\) is not in \\(T(P)\\), but \\(2p=2\\pi/c\\) is. The output is neither forced to keep the input subtype nor forced to be type \\(\\mathrm{III}_1\\). The index \\(d=2\\) records exactly the surviving multiples.\n\n**The index is a root, not a power.** If \\(H=d\\mathbb Z\\), the surviving frequency is \\(dp\\). Its period is \\(2\\pi/(dp)=L/d\\), so the subtype is \\(e^{-L/d}=\\lambda^{1/d}\\). Using \\(\\lambda^d\\) would replace this period by \\(dL\\), contradicting the generator action (TP26).\n\n**No surviving nonzero circle coefficient gives type III-one.** If \\(np\\notin T(P)\\) for every nonzero integer \\(n\\), (TP21) kills all nonzero coefficients, and (TP22) makes the entire fixed center scalar. Merely failing the basic period \\(p\\) does not prove this stronger hypothesis.\n\n**Keeping the whole input period group preserves the subtype.** If \\(p\\in T(P)\\), the group property gives \\(p\\mathbb Z\\subset T(P)\\). Then \\(d=1\\), and the full fixed center has period \\(L\\). This proves sufficiency as well as the period obstruction.\n\n**The same-time diagonal halves the apparent period.** In the \\(d=2\\) example the correct residual period is \\(c\\). Applying the same parameter to both tensor factors gives \\(\\Theta_{2s}\\), with period \\(c/2\\); it is not the canonical product center flow. The factor of two comes from composing two equal residual actions, not from a Haar normalization.\n\n**A full spectral measure requires faithfulness.** A single normal state with Haar moments need not exclude an additional singular summand if that state is not faithful. Section 5 uses the faithful expectation \\(E=\\operatorname{id}\\otimes h_Q\\), computes every spectral projection through all normal states, and then proves the normal circle isomorphism. This is the point that permits arbitrary predual.\n\n**The positive lower bound must address nonproduct weights.** Testing only product modular operators proves statements about selected spectra, not the right side of (TP33). The full normal center identification and MIV's intersection over every faithful normal semifinite weight supply that quantifier. The zero point is then added by closedness in the full scalar line.","html":"<h2>8. Solved period diagnostics<\/h2>\n<p><strong>A basic period can disappear while a multiple survives.<\/strong> In (TP32), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=\\pi/c\\)\">\\(p=\\pi/c\\)<\/span> is not in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T(P)\\)\">\\(T(P)\\)<\/span>, but <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2p=2\\pi/c\\)\">\\(2p=2\\pi/c\\)<\/span> is. The output is neither forced to keep the input subtype nor forced to be type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{III}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{III}_1\\)<\/span>. The index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d=2\\)\">\\(d=2\\)<\/span> records exactly the surviving multiples.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The index is a root, not a power.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=d\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(H=d\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, the surviving frequency is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dp\\)\">\\(dp\\)<\/span>. Its period is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\pi/(dp)=L/d\\)\">\\(2\\pi/(dp)=L/d\\)<\/span>, so the subtype is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-L/d}=\\lambda^{1/d}\\)\">\\(e^{-L/d}=\\lambda^{1/d}\\)<\/span>. Using <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda^d\\)\">\\(\\lambda^d\\)<\/span> would replace this period by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dL\\)\">\\(dL\\)<\/span>, contradicting the generator action (TP26).<\/p>\n<p><strong>No surviving nonzero circle coefficient gives type III-one.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(np\\notin T(P)\\)\">\\(np\\notin T(P)\\)<\/span> for every nonzero integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, (TP21) kills all nonzero coefficients, and (TP22) makes the entire fixed center scalar. Merely failing the basic period <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> does not prove this stronger hypothesis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Keeping the whole input period group preserves the subtype.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\in T(P)\\)\">\\(p\\in T(P)\\)<\/span>, the group property gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\mathbb Z\\subset T(P)\\)\">\\(p\\mathbb Z\\subset T(P)\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d=1\\)\">\\(d=1\\)<\/span>, and the full fixed center has period <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>. This proves sufficiency as well as the period obstruction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The same-time diagonal halves the apparent period.<\/strong> In the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d=2\\)\">\\(d=2\\)<\/span> example the correct residual period is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>. Applying the same parameter to both tensor factors gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Theta_{2s}\\)\">\\(\\Theta_{2s}\\)<\/span>, with period <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c/2\\)\">\\(c/2\\)<\/span>; it is not the canonical product center flow. The factor of two comes from composing two equal residual actions, not from a Haar normalization.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A full spectral measure requires faithfulness.<\/strong> A single normal state with Haar moments need not exclude an additional singular summand if that state is not faithful. Section 5 uses the faithful expectation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=\\operatorname{id}\\otimes h_Q\\)\">\\(E=\\operatorname{id}\\otimes h_Q\\)<\/span>, computes every spectral projection through all normal states, and then proves the normal circle isomorphism. This is the point that permits arbitrary predual.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The positive lower bound must address nonproduct weights.<\/strong> Testing only product modular operators proves statements about selected spectra, not the right side of (TP33). The full normal center identification and MIV's intersection over every faithful normal semifinite weight supply that quantifier. The zero point is then added by closedness in the full scalar line.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-TP.md","start_offset":25738,"end_offset":28015,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f8605f946f29ba07eaa87df551f9b547f40652b9f05a150b202b89922d52e64c":{"markdown":"<a id=\"cp-gauge\"><\/a>\n## 2. Changes of phase and restriction to another group\n\nLet \\(f:N\\to\\mathbb T\\), \\(f(e)=1\\). Replacing a section by\n\\(s^f(a)=f(a)s(a)\\) gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n  \\lambda^f(a,g)&=\\lambda(a,g)\n       \\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)}{f(a)},\\\\\n  \\mu^f(a,b)&=\\mu(a,b)\\frac{f(a)f(b)}{f(ab)}.\n \\end{aligned}\\tag{CP8}\n\\]\nWrite the two factors on the right as \\(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f\\), and put\n\\(\\partial f=(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f)\\).\n\nAll their identities can also be checked without using an extension. The two sides of the multiplication equation for \\(\\partial_2f\\) reduce to\n\\(f(a)f(b)f(c)/f(abc)\\). For the action equation,\n\\[\n \\frac{f(h^{-1}g^{-1}agh)}{f(a)}\n =\\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)}{f(a)}\n   \\frac{f(h^{-1}g^{-1}agh)}{f(g^{-1}ag)}.\n\\]\nFor compatibility,\n\\[\n \\frac{\\partial_1f(a,g)\\partial_1f(b,g)}{\\partial_1f(ab,g)}\n =\\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)f(g^{-1}bg)/f(g^{-1}abg)}\n        {f(a)f(b)/f(ab)}.\n\\]\nThis is \\(\\partial_2f(g^{-1}ag,g^{-1}bg)/\\partial_2f(a,b)\\). For inner action,\n\\[\n \\frac{\\partial_2f(n,n^{-1}mn)}{\\partial_2f(m,n)}\n =\\frac{f(n^{-1}mn)}{f(m)}\n =\\partial_1f(m,n).\n\\]\nNormalization is immediate from \\(f(e)=1\\). Thus \\(\\partial f\\in Z\\). Also\n\\(\\partial(f_1f_2)=\\partial f_1\\,\\partial f_2\\), by direct cancellation in (CP8).\n\nLet\n\\[\n B(G,N,\\mathbb T)=\\{\\partial f:f(e)=1\\},\\qquad\n \\Lambda(G,N,\\mathbb T)=Z(G,N,\\mathbb T)/B(G,N,\\mathbb T).\n \\tag{CP9}\n\\]\nFor Borel pairs use normalized Borel \\(f\\) and write \\(B_{\\mathrm B},\\Lambda_{\\mathrm B}\\). These are abelian groups. Their definition does not presuppose that a Borel function space is a Polish space.\n\nThe change of phase is an actual isomorphism of compatible extensions:\n\\[\n T_f:E_{\\mu^f}\\longrightarrow E_\\mu,\\qquad\n T_f(z,n)=(zf(n),n).\n \\tag{CP10}\n\\]\nFor products, the necessary equality is\n\\(\\mu^f(a,b)f(ab)=f(a)f(b)\\mu(a,b)\\). For the action, with \\(a=gng^{-1}\\), it is\n\\(\\lambda^f(a,g)f(a)=\\lambda(a,g)f(n)\\). These are exactly (CP8). The inverse is \\(T_{f^{-1}}\\), with the corresponding changed pair. Thus it fixes the kernel and quotient and intertwines every \\(\\beta_g\\). It is Borel whenever \\(f\\) is Borel.\n\nConversely, an isomorphism of two such extensions that is the identity on the kernel and quotient must have the form (CP10): its value on \\((1,n)\\) determines a unique \\(f(n)\\), and centrality determines its value on \\((z,n)\\). Preserving multiplication and the action forces (CP8). In the Borel category that first coordinate is Borel. Therefore (CP9) classifies these compatible extensions, admitting a section in the stated category, up to isomorphisms fixing the kernel, quotient and \\(G\\)-action. A section is a choice of coordinates and need not be preserved by the isomorphism. Changing the section changes the pair by exactly a gauge, and leaves its class.\n\nThere is a useful functorial rule. If \\(\\eta:H\\to G\\) is a homomorphism and \\(K\\triangleleft H\\) satisfies \\(\\eta(K)\\subseteq N\\), set\n\\[\n \\lambda_\\eta(k,h)=\\lambda(\\eta(k),\\eta(h)),\\qquad\n \\mu_\\eta(k,l)=\\mu(\\eta(k),\\eta(l)).\n \\tag{CP11}\n\\]\nEach line of (CP1) pulls back because\n\\(\\eta(h^{-1}kh)=\\eta(h)^{-1}\\eta(k)\\eta(h)\\).\nFurthermore \\(\\eta^*(\\partial f)=\\partial(f\\circ\\eta|_K)\\), so (CP11) descends to \\(\\Lambda\\). Identity and composition hold on the functions themselves. In the Borel setting require the indicated homomorphism and restrictions to be Borel.\n\n![Compatible lifts and a change of phase](../assets/characteristic-pairs/compatible-lifts.png)\n\n*Figure 1.* The two rows are the central extensions (CP2) before and after a gauge. The vertical comparison is the actual isomorphism (CP10), with its kernel and quotient fixed. The right panel displays the two paths in (CP7) and the phase \\(\\lambda(a,g)\\) between them. The inner-action identity is precisely the condition that an \\(n\\in N\\) acts by conjugation with its lift. These are diagrams of proved maps, not additional topological assertions. The intrinsic-phase questions originate in Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras III*, XVII.3, Exercise 1, p.292; the diagrams and proofs here are original. [Reproduction source](../assets/characteristic-pairs/render.py).","html":"<h2>2. Changes of phase and restriction to another group<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:N\\to\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(f:N\\to\\mathbb T\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(e)=1\\)\">\\(f(e)=1\\)<\/span>. Replacing a section by\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s^f(a)=f(a)s(a)\\)\">\\(s^f(a)=f(a)s(a)\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n  \\lambda^f(a,g)&amp;=\\lambda(a,g)\n       \\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)}{f(a)},\\\\\n  \\mu^f(a,b)&amp;=\\mu(a,b)\\frac{f(a)f(b)}{f(ab)}.\n \\end{aligned}\\tag{CP8}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n  \\lambda^f(a,g)&amp;=\\lambda(a,g)\n       \\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)}{f(a)},\\\\\n  \\mu^f(a,b)&amp;=\\mu(a,b)\\frac{f(a)f(b)}{f(ab)}.\n \\end{aligned}\\tag{CP8}\n\\]<\/span>\nWrite the two factors on the right as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f\\)\">\\(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f\\)<\/span>, and put\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial f=(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f)\\)\">\\(\\partial f=(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>All their identities can also be checked without using an extension. The two sides of the multiplication equation for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial_2f\\)\">\\(\\partial_2f\\)<\/span> reduce to\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(a)f(b)f(c)/f(abc)\\)\">\\(f(a)f(b)f(c)/f(abc)\\)<\/span>. For the action equation,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\frac{f(h^{-1}g^{-1}agh)}{f(a)}\n =\\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)}{f(a)}\n   \\frac{f(h^{-1}g^{-1}agh)}{f(g^{-1}ag)}.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\frac{f(h^{-1}g^{-1}agh)}{f(a)}\n =\\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)}{f(a)}\n   \\frac{f(h^{-1}g^{-1}agh)}{f(g^{-1}ag)}.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor compatibility,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\frac{\\partial_1f(a,g)\\partial_1f(b,g)}{\\partial_1f(ab,g)}\n =\\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)f(g^{-1}bg)/f(g^{-1}abg)}\n        {f(a)f(b)/f(ab)}.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\frac{\\partial_1f(a,g)\\partial_1f(b,g)}{\\partial_1f(ab,g)}\n =\\frac{f(g^{-1}ag)f(g^{-1}bg)/f(g^{-1}abg)}\n        {f(a)f(b)/f(ab)}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial_2f(g^{-1}ag,g^{-1}bg)/\\partial_2f(a,b)\\)\">\\(\\partial_2f(g^{-1}ag,g^{-1}bg)/\\partial_2f(a,b)\\)<\/span>. For inner action,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\frac{\\partial_2f(n,n^{-1}mn)}{\\partial_2f(m,n)}\n =\\frac{f(n^{-1}mn)}{f(m)}\n =\\partial_1f(m,n).\n\\]\">\\[\n \\frac{\\partial_2f(n,n^{-1}mn)}{\\partial_2f(m,n)}\n =\\frac{f(n^{-1}mn)}{f(m)}\n =\\partial_1f(m,n).\n\\]<\/span>\nNormalization is immediate from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(e)=1\\)\">\\(f(e)=1\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial f\\in Z\\)\">\\(\\partial f\\in Z\\)<\/span>. Also\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial(f_1f_2)=\\partial f_1\\,\\partial f_2\\)\">\\(\\partial(f_1f_2)=\\partial f_1\\,\\partial f_2\\)<\/span>, by direct cancellation in (CP8).<\/p>\n<p>Let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n B(G,N,\\mathbb T)=\\{\\partial f:f(e)=1\\},\\qquad\n \\Lambda(G,N,\\mathbb T)=Z(G,N,\\mathbb T)/B(G,N,\\mathbb T).\n \\tag{CP9}\n\\]\">\\[\n B(G,N,\\mathbb T)=\\{\\partial f:f(e)=1\\},\\qquad\n \\Lambda(G,N,\\mathbb T)=Z(G,N,\\mathbb T)/B(G,N,\\mathbb T).\n \\tag{CP9}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor Borel pairs use normalized Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> and write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_{\\mathrm B},\\Lambda_{\\mathrm B}\\)\">\\(B_{\\mathrm B},\\Lambda_{\\mathrm B}\\)<\/span>. These are abelian groups. Their definition does not presuppose that a Borel function space is a Polish space.<\/p>\n<p>The change of phase is an actual isomorphism of compatible extensions:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_f:E_{\\mu^f}\\longrightarrow E_\\mu,\\qquad\n T_f(z,n)=(zf(n),n).\n \\tag{CP10}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_f:E_{\\mu^f}\\longrightarrow E_\\mu,\\qquad\n T_f(z,n)=(zf(n),n).\n \\tag{CP10}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor products, the necessary equality is\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^f(a,b)f(ab)=f(a)f(b)\\mu(a,b)\\)\">\\(\\mu^f(a,b)f(ab)=f(a)f(b)\\mu(a,b)\\)<\/span>. For the action, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=gng^{-1}\\)\">\\(a=gng^{-1}\\)<\/span>, it is\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda^f(a,g)f(a)=\\lambda(a,g)f(n)\\)\">\\(\\lambda^f(a,g)f(a)=\\lambda(a,g)f(n)\\)<\/span>. These are exactly (CP8). The inverse is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_{f^{-1}}\\)\">\\(T_{f^{-1}}\\)<\/span>, with the corresponding changed pair. Thus it fixes the kernel and quotient and intertwines every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g\\)\">\\(\\beta_g\\)<\/span>. It is Borel whenever <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> is Borel.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, an isomorphism of two such extensions that is the identity on the kernel and quotient must have the form (CP10): its value on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((1,n)\\)\">\\((1,n)\\)<\/span> determines a unique <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(n)\\)\">\\(f(n)\\)<\/span>, and centrality determines its value on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((z,n)\\)\">\\((z,n)\\)<\/span>. Preserving multiplication and the action forces (CP8). In the Borel category that first coordinate is Borel. Therefore (CP9) classifies these compatible extensions, admitting a section in the stated category, up to isomorphisms fixing the kernel, quotient and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>-action. A section is a choice of coordinates and need not be preserved by the isomorphism. Changing the section changes the pair by exactly a gauge, and leaves its class.<\/p>\n<p>There is a useful functorial rule. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta:H\\to G\\)\">\\(\\eta:H\\to G\\)<\/span> is a homomorphism and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\triangleleft H\\)\">\\(K\\triangleleft H\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta(K)\\subseteq N\\)\">\\(\\eta(K)\\subseteq N\\)<\/span>, set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\lambda_\\eta(k,h)=\\lambda(\\eta(k),\\eta(h)),\\qquad\n \\mu_\\eta(k,l)=\\mu(\\eta(k),\\eta(l)).\n \\tag{CP11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\lambda_\\eta(k,h)=\\lambda(\\eta(k),\\eta(h)),\\qquad\n \\mu_\\eta(k,l)=\\mu(\\eta(k),\\eta(l)).\n \\tag{CP11}\n\\]<\/span>\nEach line of (CP1) pulls back because\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta(h^{-1}kh)=\\eta(h)^{-1}\\eta(k)\\eta(h)\\)\">\\(\\eta(h^{-1}kh)=\\eta(h)^{-1}\\eta(k)\\eta(h)\\)<\/span>.\nFurthermore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta^*(\\partial f)=\\partial(f\\circ\\eta|_K)\\)\">\\(\\eta^*(\\partial f)=\\partial(f\\circ\\eta|_K)\\)<\/span>, so (CP11) descends to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda\\)\">\\(\\Lambda\\)<\/span>. Identity and composition hold on the functions themselves. In the Borel setting require the indicated homomorphism and restrictions to be Borel.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/characteristic-pairs/compatible-lifts.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/characteristic-pairs/compatible-lifts.png\" alt=\"Compatible lifts and a change of phase\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><em>Figure 1.<\/em> The two rows are the central extensions (CP2) before and after a gauge. The vertical comparison is the actual isomorphism (CP10), with its kernel and quotient fixed. The right panel displays the two paths in (CP7) and the phase <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(a,g)\\)\">\\(\\lambda(a,g)\\)<\/span> between them. The inner-action identity is precisely the condition that an <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\in N\\)\">\\(n\\in N\\)<\/span> acts by conjugation with its lift. These are diagrams of proved maps, not additional topological assertions. The intrinsic-phase questions originate in Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras III<\/em>, XVII.3, Exercise 1, p.292; the diagrams and proofs here are original. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/characteristic-pairs/render.py\">Reproduction source<\/a>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CPP.md","start_offset":5557,"end_offset":9650,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f8efff7f99877b2478095d4bbaff0342f06a7cf8608befc9e5ec596094e85ddd":{"markdown":"## 6. Stinespring's dilation theorem\n\n**Theorem 6.1** (Stinespring). Fix a Hilbert space \\(H\\) and a C\\(^*\\)-algebra \\(A\\).\n\n1. If \\(\\pi\\) is a representation of \\(A\\) on \\(K\\) and \\(V\\in B(H,K)\\), then \\(\\varphi(a)=V^*\\pi(a)V\\) is completely positive, and \\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\).\n2. Let \\(\\varphi:A\\to B(H)\\) be completely positive, and put \\(N=\\varphi(A)'\\). This is a von Neumann algebra, because \\(\\varphi(A)\\) is self-adjoint: positive maps are hermitian (Proposition 3.2(2)). There are a Hilbert space \\(K\\), a representation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(A\\) on \\(K\\), an operator \\(V\\in B(H,K)\\) and a unital normal representation \\(\\rho\\) of \\(N\\) on \\(K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\varphi(a)=V^*\\pi(a)V, \\\\\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\,\\pi(A)VH, \\\\\n\\rho(N)\\subseteq\\pi(A)', \\\\\n\\rho(x)V=Vx, \\\\\na\\in A,\\quad x\\in N.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n3. Let \\((u_i)\\) be any approximate identity of \\(A\\). The increasing net \\((\\varphi(u_i))\\) converges weakly to \\(a_0:=V^*V\\), which is its least upper bound, and\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|\\varphi\\|=\\|V\\|^2=\\|a_0\\| \\\\\n=\\lim_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nIf \\(A\\) has a unit, then \\(a_0=\\varphi(1)\\) and \\(\\|\\varphi\\|=\\|\\varphi(1)\\|\\).\n\n*Further reading:* [Blackadar, II.6.9.7]. The norm equality is proved in (3).\n\n**Proof.** (1) For \\(x\\in A^n\\) and \\(y\\in B(H)^n\\), put \\(W=\\sum_j\\pi(x_j)Vy_j\\in B(H,K)\\). Then \\(\\sum_{i,j}y_i^*V^*\\pi(x_i^*x_j)Vy_j=W^*W\\ge0\\), and the criterion of Proposition 3.2(1) applies. Also \\(\\|V^*\\pi(a)V\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\|a\\|\\), since \\(\\pi\\) is contractive.\n\n(2) *Step 1: the form.* The algebraic tensor product \\(A\\odot H\\) means the following concrete quotient. Take the complex vector space freely spanned by symbols \\((a,\\xi)\\in A\\times H\\), and divide by the subspace generated by the additivity and scalar-linearity relations in each entry. Write \\(a\\otimes\\xi\\) for the class of \\((a,\\xi)\\). On this quotient put\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Bigl\\langle\\sum_ix_i\\otimes\\xi_i,\\ \\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\Bigr\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(y_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nThe expression is bilinear in \\(x,\\xi\\) and conjugate-bilinear in \\(y,\\eta\\). It therefore vanishes on every defining relation in either variable, so it descends to a well-defined sesquilinear form on the quotient. For \\(\\zeta=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\) and \\(\\hat\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_n)\\in H^n\\), exchanging the names of the indices gives\n\\[\n\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=\\bigl\\langle\\varphi^{(n)}\\bigl([x_i^*x_j]\\bigr)\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\bigr\\rangle\\ \\ge\\ 0 ,\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nbecause \\([x_i^*x_j]\\) is positive (Lemma 2.2(1)), \\(\\varphi^{(n)}\\) preserves positivity, and a positive operator matrix has a nonnegative quadratic form (Proposition 2.1(5)). So the form is positive semidefinite. By [Cauchy–Schwarz for forms](hilbert-spaces-and-compact-operators.md#oa-fnd-hs-01), \\(N_0=\\{\\zeta:\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=0\\}\\) is the subspace of vectors orthogonal to everything. The [Hilbert completion lemma](hilbert-spaces-and-compact-operators.md#completing-normed-and-inner-product-spaces) constructs the Hilbert space \\(K\\) from \\((A\\odot H)/N_0\\), and let \\(q:A\\odot H\\to K\\) be the quotient map.\n\n*Step 2: the two actions.* For \\(a\\in A\\) and \\(b\\in N\\) define linear maps of \\(A\\odot H\\) by \\(\\pi_0(a)(x\\otimes\\xi)=ax\\otimes\\xi\\) and \\(\\rho_0(b)(x\\otimes\\xi)=x\\otimes b\\xi\\). They commute. We claim that, for all \\(\\zeta,\\eta\\in A\\odot H\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\pi_0(a)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|^2\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi, \\\\\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\rho_0(b)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|^2\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle\\zeta,\\pi_0(a^*)\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi, \\\\\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle\\zeta,\\rho_0(b^*)\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\zeta=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\), \\(X=[x_i^*x_j]\\) and \\(Y=[x_i^*a^*ax_j]\\). In a faithful representation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(A\\) on \\(L\\), for \\(\\vartheta\\in L^n\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\sigma^{(n)}(Y)\\vartheta,\\vartheta\\rangle \\\\\n=\\Bigl\\|\\sigma(a)\\sum_j\\sigma(x_j)\\vartheta_j\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|^2\\Bigl\\|\\sum_j\\sigma(x_j)\\vartheta_j\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n=\\|a\\|^2\\langle\\sigma^{(n)}(X)\\vartheta,\\vartheta\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nSo \\(Y\\le\\|a\\|^2X\\) by Lemma 2.2(3), since \\(\\sigma\\) is faithful, and \\(\\varphi^{(n)}(Y)\\le\\|a\\|^2\\varphi^{(n)}(X)\\). As in (6.4), \\(\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\pi_0(a)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=\\langle\\varphi^{(n)}(Y)\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\rangle\\), which gives the first bound. For the second, let \\(T=\\varphi^{(n)}(X)\\ge0\\) and \\(b_n=\\operatorname{diag}(b,\\dots,b)\\) on \\(H^n\\). Since \\(b\\) commutes with \\(\\varphi(A)\\), \\(b_n\\) commutes with \\(T\\), hence with \\(T^{1/2}\\), a norm limit of polynomials in \\(T\\). As in (6.4),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\rho_0(b)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle Tb_n\\hat\\xi,b_n\\hat\\xi\\rangle \\\\\n=\\|b_nT^{1/2}\\hat\\xi\\|^2 \\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|^2\\langle T\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nFor (6.6), let \\(\\eta=\\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\). Both sides of the first identity equal \\(\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(y_j^*ax_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle\\), since \\((a^*y_j)^*=y_j^*a\\). Both sides of the second equal \\(\\sum_{i,j}\\langle b\\,\\varphi(y_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle\\), because \\(b\\) commutes with \\(\\varphi(A)\\).\n\nBy (6.5), \\(\\pi_0(a)\\) and \\(\\rho_0(b)\\) map \\(N_0\\) into itself and induce operators of norm at most \\(\\|a\\|\\) and \\(\\|b\\|\\) on \\((A\\odot H)/N_0\\). Let \\(\\pi(a)\\) and \\(\\rho(b)\\) be their extensions to \\(K\\). The maps \\(a\\mapsto\\pi_0(a)\\) and \\(b\\mapsto\\rho_0(b)\\) are linear and multiplicative, so by (6.6) \\(\\pi\\) is a representation of \\(A\\) and \\(\\rho\\) is a representation of \\(N\\), with \\(\\rho(1)=1\\). The ranges commute, because \\(\\pi_0\\) and \\(\\rho_0\\) commute.\n\n*Step 3: the operator \\(V\\).* Fix an approximate identity \\((u_i)\\), and put \\(V_i\\xi=q(u_i\\otimes\\xi)\\). Then \\(\\|V_i\\xi\\|^2=\\langle\\varphi(u_i^2)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\|^2\\); \\(\\varphi\\) is bounded by Proposition 3.2(4). Let \\(i\\le j\\) and \\(c=u_j-u_i\\). In a faithful representation, \\(0\\le c\\le u_j\\le1\\), so \\(c^2=c^{1/2}cc^{1/2}\\le c\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|V_j\\xi-V_i\\xi\\|^2=\\langle\\varphi(c^2)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle \\\\\n\\le\\langle\\varphi(u_j)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle-\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nThe numbers \\(\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\) increase with \\(i\\) and are at most \\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\|^2\\), so they converge. Comparing \\(V_i\\xi\\) and \\(V_j\\xi\\) with \\(V_{i_0}\\xi\\) for \\(i,j\\ge i_0\\) shows that \\((V_i\\xi)\\) is a Cauchy net. A Cauchy net in a complete metric space converges: choose increasing indices \\(i_n\\) so that every tail after \\(i_n\\) has diameter at most \\(1/n\\), take the limit of the Cauchy sequence at those indices, and use the same tail bound for the whole net. So \\(V\\xi=\\lim_iV_i\\xi\\) defines \\(V\\in B(H,K)\\) with \\(\\|V\\|^2\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\).\n\n*Step 4: the identities.* Let \\(x\\in A\\) and \\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\).\n- (a) \\(V^*q(x\\otimes\\xi)=\\varphi(x)\\xi\\). Indeed \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle q(x\\otimes\\xi),V\\eta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle q(x\\otimes\\xi),q(u_i\\otimes\\eta)\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle\\varphi(u_ix)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle\\varphi(x)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] since \\(u_ix\\to x\\) and \\(\\varphi\\) is bounded.\n- (b) \\(\\pi(x)V\\xi=q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\). Indeed \\(\\pi(x)V_i\\xi=q(xu_i\\otimes\\xi)\\), and \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|q(xu_i\\otimes\\xi)-q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\|^2\\\\\n\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|xu_i-x\\|^2\\|\\xi\\|^2\\to0.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\n- (c) By (a) and (b), \\(V^*\\pi(x)V\\xi=\\varphi(x)\\xi\\). So \\(\\varphi=V^*\\pi(\\cdot)V\\).\n- (d) By (b), \\(q(A\\odot H)\\) is the span of \\(\\pi(A)VH\\). So \\(K\\) is the closed span of \\(\\pi(A)VH\\); in particular \\(\\pi\\) is nondegenerate.\n- (e) For \\(b\\in N\\), \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\pi(x)\\rho(b)V\\xi\\\\\n=\\rho(b)q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\\\\n=q(x\\otimes b\\xi)\\\\\n=\\pi(x)Vb\\xi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] So \\(w=\\rho(b)V\\xi-Vb\\xi\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(x)w=0\\) for all \\(x\\). Then \\(\\langle w,\\pi(x^*)\\kappa\\rangle=0\\) for all \\(x\\) and \\(\\kappa\\), and \\(w=0\\) by (d). Hence \\(\\rho(b)V=Vb\\).\n\n*Step 5: \\(\\rho\\) is normal.* For \\(\\zeta=\\sum_ix_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\) and \\(\\eta=\\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\),\n\\[\n\\langle\\rho(b)q(\\zeta),q(\\eta)\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle b\\,\\xi_i,\\ \\varphi(x_i^*y_j)\\eta_j\\rangle ,\n\\]\na finite sum of vector functionals of \\(b\\), hence ultraweakly continuous on \\(N\\). For arbitrary \\(\\kappa,\\lambda\\in K\\), choose \\(\\kappa_m\\to\\kappa\\) and \\(\\lambda_m\\to\\lambda\\) in \\(q(A\\odot H)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n|\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa,\\lambda\\rangle-\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa_m,\\lambda_m\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|\\,(\\|\\kappa-\\kappa_m\\|\\,\\|\\lambda\\|+\\|\\kappa_m\\|\\,\\|\\lambda-\\lambda_m\\|).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nSo \\(b\\mapsto\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa,\\lambda\\rangle\\) is a norm limit in \\(N^*\\) of elements of \\(N_*\\), and it lies in \\(N_*\\) because \\(N_*\\) is norm-closed (B11). Finally, let \\(\\psi=\\sum_n\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,\\kappa_n,\\lambda_n\\rangle\\in B(K)_*\\). The series \\(\\psi\\circ\\rho=\\sum_n\\langle\\rho(\\cdot)\\kappa_n,\\lambda_n\\rangle\\) converges in the norm of \\(N^*\\), since the \\(n\\)-th term has norm at most \\(\\|\\kappa_n\\|\\,\\|\\lambda_n\\|\\) and \\(\\sum_n\\|\\kappa_n\\|\\,\\|\\lambda_n\\|<\\infty\\). So \\(\\psi\\circ\\rho\\in N_*\\), and \\(\\rho\\) is normal.\n\n(3) Let \\((u_i)\\) be any approximate identity, not necessarily the one fixed in Step 3. By (b), \\(q(u_i\\otimes\\xi)=\\pi(u_i)V\\xi\\), and \\(\\pi(u_i)\\to1\\) strongly because \\(\\pi\\) is nondegenerate by (d) (Section 1). So \\(V\\xi=\\lim_iq(u_i\\otimes\\xi)\\), and (a) gives \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle V\\xi,V\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle V^*q(u_i\\otimes\\xi),\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] So \\(\\langle a_0\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\) for every \\(\\xi\\). By polarization \\(\\varphi(u_i)\\to a_0\\) weakly, and every self-adjoint upper bound \\(c\\) of the net satisfies \\(\\langle c\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\ge\\langle a_0\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\). By (P2), \\(\\|a_0\\|=\\sup_{\\|\\xi\\|=1}\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sup_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|\\), and this supremum is a limit because the norms increase. Now \\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\) by (1), while \\(\\|V\\|^2=\\|V^*V\\|=\\|a_0\\|=\\sup_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\). If \\(A\\) has a unit, then \\(\\|u_i-1\\|=\\|u_i1-1\\|\\to0\\), so \\(a_0=\\lim_i\\varphi(u_i)=\\varphi(1)\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n*Remark* (A second proof of the Kadison–Schwarz inequality for completely positive maps). If \\(\\varphi=V^*\\pi(\\cdot)V\\) as in (6.1), then \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\varphi(a)^*\\varphi(a)\\\\\n=V^*\\pi(a)^*VV^*\\pi(a)V\\\\\n\\le\\|V\\|^2V^*\\pi(a^*a)V,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] because \\(VV^*\\le\\|V\\|^21\\). By (6.2) the right side is \\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\varphi(a^*a)\\). This gives Theorem 4.1(1) for completely positive maps.\n\n**Theorem 6.2** (Uniqueness of the minimal dilation, and when \\(\\rho\\) is faithful). Let \\(\\varphi\\), \\(N\\), \\(K\\), \\(\\pi\\), \\(V\\), \\(\\rho\\) and \\(a_0\\) be as in Theorem 6.1.\n\n1. Let \\(\\pi'\\) be a representation of \\(A\\) on \\(K'\\) and \\(V'\\in B(H,K')\\) with \\(\\varphi=V'^*\\pi'(\\cdot)V'\\) and \\(K'=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\,\\pi'(A)V'H\\). There is exactly one unitary \\(U:K\\to K'\\) with \\(U\\pi(a)=\\pi'(a)U\\) for all \\(a\\in A\\) and \\(UV=V'\\). If a map \\(\\rho':N\\to B(K')\\) satisfies \\(\\rho'(N)\\subseteq\\pi'(A)'\\) and \\(\\rho'(x)V'=V'x\\) for all \\(x\\), then \\(\\rho'(x)=U\\rho(x)U^*\\). In particular \\(\\rho\\) is determined by (6.1), and any such \\(\\rho'\\) is automatically a unital normal representation.\n2. \\(\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in N:\\ a_0x=0\\}=N(1-z)\\), where \\(z\\) is the projection onto the closed span of \\(N'a_0H\\). The projection \\(z\\) lies in the centre \\(N\\cap N'\\). So \\(\\rho\\) is faithful if and only if \\(z=1\\). This holds when \\(a_0\\) has zero kernel, in particular when \\(a_0=1\\).\n3. There are completely positive maps for which \\(\\rho\\) is not faithful. So a triple as in (6.1) with a faithful \\(\\rho\\) need not exist, and the uniqueness in (1) must be stated without faithfulness.\n4. If \\(A\\) has a unit, then \\(V\\xi=q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\) and \\(V^*V=\\varphi(1)\\). \\(V\\) is an isometry if and only if \\(\\varphi(1)=1\\); for general \\(A\\), if and only if \\(a_0=1\\). Then \\(\\rho\\) is faithful, and \\(\\varphi(a)\\) is the compression of \\(\\pi(a)\\) to the subspace \\(VH\\), identified with \\(H\\).\n\nThe explicit example in (3) proves that faithfulness cannot be required for every minimal dilation; (2) gives the exact condition.\n\n**Proof.** (1) For finite sums,\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Bigl\\|\\sum_i\\pi'(x_i)V'\\xi_i\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle V'^*\\pi'(x_j^*x_i)V'\\xi_i,\\xi_j\\rangle \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(x_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\xi_j\\rangle \\\\\n=\\Bigl\\|\\sum_i\\pi(x_i)V\\xi_i\\Bigr\\|^2.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nSo \\(U_0:\\sum_i\\pi(x_i)V\\xi_i\\mapsto\\sum_i\\pi'(x_i)V'\\xi_i\\) is well defined and isometric from a dense subspace of \\(K\\) onto a dense subspace of \\(K'\\). It extends to a unitary \\(U\\). The relation \\(U\\pi(a)=\\pi'(a)U\\) holds on the dense subspace, hence everywhere. Both representations are nondegenerate, so \\(\\pi(u_i)\\to1\\) and \\(\\pi'(u_i)\\to1\\) strongly (Section 1). Hence \\(UV\\xi=\\lim_iU\\pi(u_i)V\\xi=\\lim_i\\pi'(u_i)V'\\xi=V'\\xi\\). Any unitary with the two properties satisfies \\(U\\pi(a)V\\xi=\\pi'(a)V'\\xi\\), so it is unique. For \\(\\rho'\\): on the total set of vectors \\(\\pi'(a)V'\\xi\\), \\(\\rho'(x)\\pi'(a)V'\\xi=\\pi'(a)\\rho'(x)V'\\xi=\\pi'(a)V'x\\xi\\). The operator \\(U\\rho(x)U^*\\) has the same values there, because \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nU\\rho(x)U^*\\pi'(a)V'\\xi\\\\\n=U\\rho(x)\\pi(a)V\\xi\\\\\n=U\\pi(a)Vx\\xi\\\\\n=\\pi'(a)V'x\\xi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] Two bounded operators that agree on a total set are equal.\n\n(2) If \\(\\rho(x)=0\\), then \\(Vx=\\rho(x)V=0\\) and \\(a_0x=V^*Vx=0\\). If \\(a_0x=0\\), then \\(\\|Vx\\xi\\|^2=\\langle a_0x\\xi,x\\xi\\rangle=0\\), so \\(Vx=0\\), and \\(\\rho(x)\\pi(a)V\\xi=\\pi(a)Vx\\xi=0\\) on a total set; so \\(\\rho(x)=0\\). Next, \\(a_0\\) is a weak limit of elements of \\(\\varphi(A)\\subseteq N'\\), and \\(N'\\) is weakly closed, so \\(a_0\\in N'\\). Let \\(x\\in N\\); it commutes with \\(a_0\\in N'\\). If \\(a_0x=0\\), then \\(xa_0=0\\), and for \\(y\\in N'\\) we get \\(xya_0=yxa_0=0\\); so \\(x\\) vanishes on \\(N'a_0H\\), and \\(xz=0\\). Conversely, if \\(xz=0\\), then \\(xa_0=xza_0=0\\), because \\(1\\in N'\\) gives \\(a_0H\\subseteq zH\\); and \\(a_0x=xa_0=0\\), since \\(x\\) and \\(a_0\\) commute. The subspace \\(zH\\) is invariant under \\(N'\\), and under \\(N\\) because \\(xya_0\\xi=ya_0x\\xi\\) for \\(x\\in N\\), \\(y\\in N'\\). Both sets are self-adjoint, so \\(z\\in N''\\cap N'=N\\cap N'\\). Hence \\(\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in N:xz=0\\}=N(1-z)\\). If \\(\\ker a_0=\\{0\\}\\), then \\(a_0H\\) is dense, because \\((a_0H)^\\perp=\\ker a_0\\), and \\(z=1\\).\n\n(3) Let \\(A=\\mathbb C\\), \\(H=\\mathbb C^2\\) and \\(\\varphi(\\lambda)=\\lambda e_{11}\\). With \\(V(\\xi_1,\\xi_2)=\\xi_1\\in\\mathbb C\\) and \\(\\pi(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\) on \\(K=\\mathbb C\\), we have \\(\\varphi(\\lambda)=V^*\\pi(\\lambda)V\\), so \\(\\varphi\\) is CP by Theorem 6.1(1), and \\(K\\) is spanned by \\(\\pi(\\mathbb C)VH\\). Here \\(N=\\varphi(\\mathbb C)'\\) is the algebra of diagonal matrices, and \\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(s,t))V=V\\operatorname{diag}(s,t)\\) forces \\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(s,t))=s\\). So \\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(0,1))=0\\). By (1), every triple satisfying (6.1) is unitarily equivalent to this one, so none of them has a faithful \\(\\rho\\). In the notation of (2), \\(a_0=e_{11}\\), \\(N'=N\\) and \\(z=e_{11}\\).\n\n(4) If \\(A\\) has a unit, then \\(\\|u_i-1\\|\\to0\\), and \\(\\|V_i\\xi-q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\|^2\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|u_i-1\\|^2\\|\\xi\\|^2\\) (Step 3 of the proof of Theorem 6.1); so \\(V\\xi=q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\), and \\(a_0=\\varphi(1)\\) by Theorem 6.1(3). \\(V\\) is an isometry exactly when \\(V^*V=a_0=1\\). Then \\(\\rho\\) is faithful by (2), and \\(VV^*\\) is the projection onto \\(VH\\). \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>6. Stinespring's dilation theorem<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Theorem 6.1<\/strong> (Stinespring). Fix a Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> and a C<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(^*\\)\">\\(^*\\)<\/span>-algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is a representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\in B(H,K)\\)\">\\(V\\in B(H,K)\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(a)=V^*\\pi(a)V\\)\">\\(\\varphi(a)=V^*\\pi(a)V\\)<\/span> is completely positive, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi:A\\to B(H)\\)\">\\(\\varphi:A\\to B(H)\\)<\/span> be completely positive, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\varphi(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=\\varphi(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span>. This is a von Neumann algebra, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(A)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(A)\\)<\/span> is self-adjoint: positive maps are hermitian (Proposition 3.2(2)). There are a Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, a representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, an operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\in B(H,K)\\)\">\\(V\\in B(H,K)\\)<\/span> and a unital normal representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\varphi(a)=V^*\\pi(a)V, \\\\\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\,\\pi(A)VH, \\\\\n\\rho(N)\\subseteq\\pi(A)&#x27;, \\\\\n\\rho(x)V=Vx, \\\\\na\\in A,\\quad x\\in N.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\varphi(a)=V^*\\pi(a)V, \\\\\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\,\\pi(A)VH, \\\\\n\\rho(N)\\subseteq\\pi(A)&#x27;, \\\\\n\\rho(x)V=Vx, \\\\\na\\in A,\\quad x\\in N.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((u_i)\\)\">\\((u_i)\\)<\/span> be any approximate identity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. The increasing net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\varphi(u_i))\\)\">\\((\\varphi(u_i))\\)<\/span> converges weakly to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0:=V^*V\\)\">\\(a_0:=V^*V\\)<\/span>, which is its least upper bound, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|\\varphi\\|=\\|V\\|^2=\\|a_0\\| \\\\\n=\\lim_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|\\varphi\\|=\\|V\\|^2=\\|a_0\\| \\\\\n=\\lim_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> has a unit, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=\\varphi(1)\\)\">\\(a_0=\\varphi(1)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\varphi\\|=\\|\\varphi(1)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\varphi\\|=\\|\\varphi(1)\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><em>Further reading:<\/em> [Blackadar, II.6.9.7]. The norm equality is proved in (3).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in A^n\\)\">\\(x\\in A^n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in B(H)^n\\)\">\\(y\\in B(H)^n\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W=\\sum_j\\pi(x_j)Vy_j\\in B(H,K)\\)\">\\(W=\\sum_j\\pi(x_j)Vy_j\\in B(H,K)\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{i,j}y_i^*V^*\\pi(x_i^*x_j)Vy_j=W^*W\\ge0\\)\">\\(\\sum_{i,j}y_i^*V^*\\pi(x_i^*x_j)Vy_j=W^*W\\ge0\\)<\/span>, and the criterion of Proposition 3.2(1) applies. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|V^*\\pi(a)V\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\|a\\|\\)\">\\(\\|V^*\\pi(a)V\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\|a\\|\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is contractive.<\/p>\n<p>(2) <em>Step 1: the form.<\/em> The algebraic tensor product <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\odot H\\)\">\\(A\\odot H\\)<\/span> means the following concrete quotient. Take the complex vector space freely spanned by symbols <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a,\\xi)\\in A\\times H\\)\">\\((a,\\xi)\\in A\\times H\\)<\/span>, and divide by the subspace generated by the additivity and scalar-linearity relations in each entry. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\otimes\\xi\\)\">\\(a\\otimes\\xi\\)<\/span> for the class of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a,\\xi)\\)\">\\((a,\\xi)\\)<\/span>. On this quotient put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Bigl\\langle\\sum_ix_i\\otimes\\xi_i,\\ \\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\Bigr\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(y_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Bigl\\langle\\sum_ix_i\\otimes\\xi_i,\\ \\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\Bigr\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(y_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe expression is bilinear in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,\\xi\\)\">\\(x,\\xi\\)<\/span> and conjugate-bilinear in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y,\\eta\\)\">\\(y,\\eta\\)<\/span>. It therefore vanishes on every defining relation in either variable, so it descends to a well-defined sesquilinear form on the quotient. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\)\">\\(\\zeta=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\hat\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_n)\\in H^n\\)\">\\(\\hat\\xi=(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_n)\\in H^n\\)<\/span>, exchanging the names of the indices gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=\\bigl\\langle\\varphi^{(n)}\\bigl([x_i^*x_j]\\bigr)\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\bigr\\rangle\\ \\ge\\ 0 ,\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=\\bigl\\langle\\varphi^{(n)}\\bigl([x_i^*x_j]\\bigr)\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\bigr\\rangle\\ \\ge\\ 0 ,\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nbecause <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([x_i^*x_j]\\)\">\\([x_i^*x_j]\\)<\/span> is positive (Lemma 2.2(1)), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi^{(n)}\\)\">\\(\\varphi^{(n)}\\)<\/span> preserves positivity, and a positive operator matrix has a nonnegative quadratic form (Proposition 2.1(5)). So the form is positive semidefinite. By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/hilbert-spaces-and-compact-operators.html#oa-fnd-hs-01\">Cauchy–Schwarz for forms<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_0=\\{\\zeta:\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=0\\}\\)\">\\(N_0=\\{\\zeta:\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=0\\}\\)<\/span> is the subspace of vectors orthogonal to everything. The <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/hilbert-spaces-and-compact-operators.html#completing-normed-and-inner-product-spaces\">Hilbert completion lemma<\/a> constructs the Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((A\\odot H)/N_0\\)\">\\((A\\odot H)/N_0\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q:A\\odot H\\to K\\)\">\\(q:A\\odot H\\to K\\)<\/span> be the quotient map.<\/p>\n<p><em>Step 2: the two actions.<\/em> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in N\\)\">\\(b\\in N\\)<\/span> define linear maps of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\odot H\\)\">\\(A\\odot H\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_0(a)(x\\otimes\\xi)=ax\\otimes\\xi\\)\">\\(\\pi_0(a)(x\\otimes\\xi)=ax\\otimes\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_0(b)(x\\otimes\\xi)=x\\otimes b\\xi\\)\">\\(\\rho_0(b)(x\\otimes\\xi)=x\\otimes b\\xi\\)<\/span>. They commute. We claim that, for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta,\\eta\\in A\\odot H\\)\">\\(\\zeta,\\eta\\in A\\odot H\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\pi_0(a)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|^2\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi, \\\\\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\rho_0(b)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|^2\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\pi_0(a)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|^2\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi, \\\\\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\rho_0(b)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|^2\\langle\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]<\/span>\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle\\zeta,\\pi_0(a^*)\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi, \\\\\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle\\zeta,\\rho_0(b^*)\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle\\zeta,\\pi_0(a^*)\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi, \\\\\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle\\zeta,\\rho_0(b^*)\\eta\\rangle_\\varphi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]<\/span>\nLet <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\)\">\\(\\zeta=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X=[x_i^*x_j]\\)\">\\(X=[x_i^*x_j]\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y=[x_i^*a^*ax_j]\\)\">\\(Y=[x_i^*a^*ax_j]\\)<\/span>. In a faithful representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\vartheta\\in L^n\\)\">\\(\\vartheta\\in L^n\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\sigma^{(n)}(Y)\\vartheta,\\vartheta\\rangle \\\\\n=\\Bigl\\|\\sigma(a)\\sum_j\\sigma(x_j)\\vartheta_j\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|^2\\Bigl\\|\\sum_j\\sigma(x_j)\\vartheta_j\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n=\\|a\\|^2\\langle\\sigma^{(n)}(X)\\vartheta,\\vartheta\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\sigma^{(n)}(Y)\\vartheta,\\vartheta\\rangle \\\\\n=\\Bigl\\|\\sigma(a)\\sum_j\\sigma(x_j)\\vartheta_j\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|^2\\Bigl\\|\\sum_j\\sigma(x_j)\\vartheta_j\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n=\\|a\\|^2\\langle\\sigma^{(n)}(X)\\vartheta,\\vartheta\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nSo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\le\\|a\\|^2X\\)\">\\(Y\\le\\|a\\|^2X\\)<\/span> by Lemma 2.2(3), since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span> is faithful, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi^{(n)}(Y)\\le\\|a\\|^2\\varphi^{(n)}(X)\\)\">\\(\\varphi^{(n)}(Y)\\le\\|a\\|^2\\varphi^{(n)}(X)\\)<\/span>. As in (6.4), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\pi_0(a)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=\\langle\\varphi^{(n)}(Y)\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle\\pi_0(a)\\zeta,\\pi_0(a)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi=\\langle\\varphi^{(n)}(Y)\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span>, which gives the first bound. For the second, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=\\varphi^{(n)}(X)\\ge0\\)\">\\(T=\\varphi^{(n)}(X)\\ge0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_n=\\operatorname{diag}(b,\\dots,b)\\)\">\\(b_n=\\operatorname{diag}(b,\\dots,b)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H^n\\)\">\\(H^n\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(A)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(A)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_n\\)\">\\(b_n\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>, hence with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T^{1/2}\\)\">\\(T^{1/2}\\)<\/span>, a norm limit of polynomials in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>. As in (6.4),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\rho_0(b)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle Tb_n\\hat\\xi,b_n\\hat\\xi\\rangle \\\\\n=\\|b_nT^{1/2}\\hat\\xi\\|^2 \\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|^2\\langle T\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle\\rho_0(b)\\zeta,\\rho_0(b)\\zeta\\rangle_\\varphi \\\\\n=\\langle Tb_n\\hat\\xi,b_n\\hat\\xi\\rangle \\\\\n=\\|b_nT^{1/2}\\hat\\xi\\|^2 \\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|^2\\langle T\\hat\\xi,\\hat\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor (6.6), let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta=\\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\)\">\\(\\eta=\\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\)<\/span>. Both sides of the first identity equal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(y_j^*ax_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(y_j^*ax_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a^*y_j)^*=y_j^*a\\)\">\\((a^*y_j)^*=y_j^*a\\)<\/span>. Both sides of the second equal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{i,j}\\langle b\\,\\varphi(y_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\sum_{i,j}\\langle b\\,\\varphi(y_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\eta_j\\rangle\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(A)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(A)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>By (6.5), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_0(a)\\)\">\\(\\pi_0(a)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_0(b)\\)\">\\(\\rho_0(b)\\)<\/span> map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_0\\)\">\\(N_0\\)<\/span> into itself and induce operators of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a\\|\\)\">\\(\\|a\\|\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|b\\|\\)\">\\(\\|b\\|\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((A\\odot H)/N_0\\)\">\\((A\\odot H)/N_0\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(a)\\)\">\\(\\pi(a)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(b)\\)\">\\(\\rho(b)\\)<\/span> be their extensions to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. The maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\mapsto\\pi_0(a)\\)\">\\(a\\mapsto\\pi_0(a)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\mapsto\\rho_0(b)\\)\">\\(b\\mapsto\\rho_0(b)\\)<\/span> are linear and multiplicative, so by (6.6) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is a representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is a representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\rho(1)=1\\)<\/span>. The ranges commute, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_0\\)\">\\(\\pi_0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_0\\)\">\\(\\rho_0\\)<\/span> commute.<\/p>\n<p><em>Step 3: the operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>.<\/em> Fix an approximate identity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((u_i)\\)\">\\((u_i)\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_i\\xi=q(u_i\\otimes\\xi)\\)\">\\(V_i\\xi=q(u_i\\otimes\\xi)\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|V_i\\xi\\|^2=\\langle\\varphi(u_i^2)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|V_i\\xi\\|^2=\\langle\\varphi(u_i^2)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> is bounded by Proposition 3.2(4). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\le j\\)\">\\(i\\le j\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=u_j-u_i\\)\">\\(c=u_j-u_i\\)<\/span>. In a faithful representation, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le c\\le u_j\\le1\\)\">\\(0\\le c\\le u_j\\le1\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c^2=c^{1/2}cc^{1/2}\\le c\\)\">\\(c^2=c^{1/2}cc^{1/2}\\le c\\)<\/span>. Hence\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|V_j\\xi-V_i\\xi\\|^2=\\langle\\varphi(c^2)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle \\\\\n\\le\\langle\\varphi(u_j)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle-\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|V_j\\xi-V_i\\xi\\|^2=\\langle\\varphi(c^2)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle \\\\\n\\le\\langle\\varphi(u_j)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle-\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe numbers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span> increase with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span> and are at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>, so they converge. Comparing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_i\\xi\\)\">\\(V_i\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_j\\xi\\)\">\\(V_j\\xi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_{i_0}\\xi\\)\">\\(V_{i_0}\\xi\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i,j\\ge i_0\\)\">\\(i,j\\ge i_0\\)<\/span> shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((V_i\\xi)\\)\">\\((V_i\\xi)\\)<\/span> is a Cauchy net. A Cauchy net in a complete metric space converges: choose increasing indices <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i_n\\)\">\\(i_n\\)<\/span> so that every tail after <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i_n\\)\">\\(i_n\\)<\/span> has diameter at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span>, take the limit of the Cauchy sequence at those indices, and use the same tail bound for the whole net. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\xi=\\lim_iV_i\\xi\\)\">\\(V\\xi=\\lim_iV_i\\xi\\)<\/span> defines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\in B(H,K)\\)\">\\(V\\in B(H,K)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|V\\|^2\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\)\">\\(\\|V\\|^2\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><em>Step 4: the identities.<\/em> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in A\\)\">\\(x\\in A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V^*q(x\\otimes\\xi)=\\varphi(x)\\xi\\)\">\\(V^*q(x\\otimes\\xi)=\\varphi(x)\\xi\\)<\/span>. Indeed <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle q(x\\otimes\\xi),V\\eta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle q(x\\otimes\\xi),q(u_i\\otimes\\eta)\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle\\varphi(u_ix)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle\\varphi(x)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle q(x\\otimes\\xi),V\\eta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle q(x\\otimes\\xi),q(u_i\\otimes\\eta)\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle\\varphi(u_ix)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle\\varphi(x)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_ix\\to x\\)\">\\(u_ix\\to x\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> is bounded.<\/li>\n<li>(b) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(x)V\\xi=q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\)\">\\(\\pi(x)V\\xi=q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\)<\/span>. Indeed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(x)V_i\\xi=q(xu_i\\otimes\\xi)\\)\">\\(\\pi(x)V_i\\xi=q(xu_i\\otimes\\xi)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|q(xu_i\\otimes\\xi)-q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\|^2\\\\\n\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|xu_i-x\\|^2\\|\\xi\\|^2\\to0.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|q(xu_i\\otimes\\xi)-q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\|^2\\\\\n\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|xu_i-x\\|^2\\|\\xi\\|^2\\to0.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span><\/li>\n<li>(c) By (a) and (b), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V^*\\pi(x)V\\xi=\\varphi(x)\\xi\\)\">\\(V^*\\pi(x)V\\xi=\\varphi(x)\\xi\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=V^*\\pi(\\cdot)V\\)\">\\(\\varphi=V^*\\pi(\\cdot)V\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>(d) By (b), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(A\\odot H)\\)\">\\(q(A\\odot H)\\)<\/span> is the span of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)VH\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)VH\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is the closed span of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)VH\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)VH\\)<\/span>; in particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate.<\/li>\n<li>(e) For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in N\\)\">\\(b\\in N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\pi(x)\\rho(b)V\\xi\\\\\n=\\rho(b)q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\\\\n=q(x\\otimes b\\xi)\\\\\n=\\pi(x)Vb\\xi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\pi(x)\\rho(b)V\\xi\\\\\n=\\rho(b)q(x\\otimes\\xi)\\\\\n=q(x\\otimes b\\xi)\\\\\n=\\pi(x)Vb\\xi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w=\\rho(b)V\\xi-Vb\\xi\\)\">\\(w=\\rho(b)V\\xi-Vb\\xi\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(x)w=0\\)\">\\(\\pi(x)w=0\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle w,\\pi(x^*)\\kappa\\rangle=0\\)\">\\(\\langle w,\\pi(x^*)\\kappa\\rangle=0\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa\\)\">\\(\\kappa\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w=0\\)\">\\(w=0\\)<\/span> by (d). Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(b)V=Vb\\)\">\\(\\rho(b)V=Vb\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Step 5: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is normal.<\/em> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta=\\sum_ix_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\)\">\\(\\zeta=\\sum_ix_i\\otimes\\xi_i\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta=\\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\)\">\\(\\eta=\\sum_jy_j\\otimes\\eta_j\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\langle\\rho(b)q(\\zeta),q(\\eta)\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle b\\,\\xi_i,\\ \\varphi(x_i^*y_j)\\eta_j\\rangle ,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\langle\\rho(b)q(\\zeta),q(\\eta)\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle b\\,\\xi_i,\\ \\varphi(x_i^*y_j)\\eta_j\\rangle ,\n\\]<\/span>\na finite sum of vector functionals of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, hence ultraweakly continuous on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. For arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa,\\lambda\\in K\\)\">\\(\\kappa,\\lambda\\in K\\)<\/span>, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa_m\\to\\kappa\\)\">\\(\\kappa_m\\to\\kappa\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_m\\to\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda_m\\to\\lambda\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(A\\odot H)\\)\">\\(q(A\\odot H)\\)<\/span>. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n|\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa,\\lambda\\rangle-\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa_m,\\lambda_m\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|\\,(\\|\\kappa-\\kappa_m\\|\\,\\|\\lambda\\|+\\|\\kappa_m\\|\\,\\|\\lambda-\\lambda_m\\|).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n|\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa,\\lambda\\rangle-\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa_m,\\lambda_m\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\|b\\|\\,(\\|\\kappa-\\kappa_m\\|\\,\\|\\lambda\\|+\\|\\kappa_m\\|\\,\\|\\lambda-\\lambda_m\\|).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nSo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\mapsto\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa,\\lambda\\rangle\\)\">\\(b\\mapsto\\langle\\rho(b)\\kappa,\\lambda\\rangle\\)<\/span> is a norm limit in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N^*\\)\">\\(N^*\\)<\/span> of elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_*\\)\">\\(N_*\\)<\/span>, and it lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_*\\)\">\\(N_*\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_*\\)\">\\(N_*\\)<\/span> is norm-closed (B11). Finally, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi=\\sum_n\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,\\kappa_n,\\lambda_n\\rangle\\in B(K)_*\\)\">\\(\\psi=\\sum_n\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,\\kappa_n,\\lambda_n\\rangle\\in B(K)_*\\)<\/span>. The series <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\circ\\rho=\\sum_n\\langle\\rho(\\cdot)\\kappa_n,\\lambda_n\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\psi\\circ\\rho=\\sum_n\\langle\\rho(\\cdot)\\kappa_n,\\lambda_n\\rangle\\)<\/span> converges in the norm of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N^*\\)\">\\(N^*\\)<\/span>, since the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>-th term has norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\kappa_n\\|\\,\\|\\lambda_n\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\kappa_n\\|\\,\\|\\lambda_n\\|\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n\\|\\kappa_n\\|\\,\\|\\lambda_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_n\\|\\kappa_n\\|\\,\\|\\lambda_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\circ\\rho\\in N_*\\)\">\\(\\psi\\circ\\rho\\in N_*\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is normal.<\/p>\n<p>(3) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((u_i)\\)\">\\((u_i)\\)<\/span> be any approximate identity, not necessarily the one fixed in Step 3. By (b), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(u_i\\otimes\\xi)=\\pi(u_i)V\\xi\\)\">\\(q(u_i\\otimes\\xi)=\\pi(u_i)V\\xi\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(u_i)\\to1\\)\">\\(\\pi(u_i)\\to1\\)<\/span> strongly because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate by (d) (Section 1). So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\xi=\\lim_iq(u_i\\otimes\\xi)\\)\">\\(V\\xi=\\lim_iq(u_i\\otimes\\xi)\\)<\/span>, and (a) gives <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle V\\xi,V\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle V^*q(u_i\\otimes\\xi),\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\langle V\\xi,V\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle V^*q(u_i\\otimes\\xi),\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\lim_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle a_0\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle a_0\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>. By polarization <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(u_i)\\to a_0\\)\">\\(\\varphi(u_i)\\to a_0\\)<\/span> weakly, and every self-adjoint upper bound <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> of the net satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle c\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\ge\\langle a_0\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle c\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\ge\\langle a_0\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span>. By (P2), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a_0\\|=\\sup_{\\|\\xi\\|=1}\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sup_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|a_0\\|=\\sup_{\\|\\xi\\|=1}\\sup_i\\langle\\varphi(u_i)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle=\\sup_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|\\)<\/span>, and this supremum is a limit because the norms increase. Now <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\le\\|V\\|^2\\)<\/span> by (1), while <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|V\\|^2=\\|V^*V\\|=\\|a_0\\|=\\sup_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\)\">\\(\\|V\\|^2=\\|V^*V\\|=\\|a_0\\|=\\sup_i\\|\\varphi(u_i)\\|\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> has a unit, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|u_i-1\\|=\\|u_i1-1\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|u_i-1\\|=\\|u_i1-1\\|\\to0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=\\lim_i\\varphi(u_i)=\\varphi(1)\\)\">\\(a_0=\\lim_i\\varphi(u_i)=\\varphi(1)\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><em>Remark<\/em> (A second proof of the Kadison–Schwarz inequality for completely positive maps). If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=V^*\\pi(\\cdot)V\\)\">\\(\\varphi=V^*\\pi(\\cdot)V\\)<\/span> as in (6.1), then <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\varphi(a)^*\\varphi(a)\\\\\n=V^*\\pi(a)^*VV^*\\pi(a)V\\\\\n\\le\\|V\\|^2V^*\\pi(a^*a)V,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\varphi(a)^*\\varphi(a)\\\\\n=V^*\\pi(a)^*VV^*\\pi(a)V\\\\\n\\le\\|V\\|^2V^*\\pi(a^*a)V,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(VV^*\\le\\|V\\|^21\\)\">\\(VV^*\\le\\|V\\|^21\\)<\/span>. By (6.2) the right side is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\varphi(a^*a)\\)\">\\(\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\varphi(a^*a)\\)<\/span>. This gives Theorem 4.1(1) for completely positive maps.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 6.2<\/strong> (Uniqueness of the minimal dilation, and when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is faithful). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0\\)\">\\(a_0\\)<\/span> be as in Theorem 6.1.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi&#x27;\\)<\/span> be a representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K&#x27;\\)\">\\(K&#x27;\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V&#x27;\\in B(H,K&#x27;)\\)\">\\(V&#x27;\\in B(H,K&#x27;)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=V&#x27;^*\\pi&#x27;(\\cdot)V&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\varphi=V&#x27;^*\\pi&#x27;(\\cdot)V&#x27;\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K&#x27;=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\,\\pi&#x27;(A)V&#x27;H\\)\">\\(K&#x27;=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\,\\pi&#x27;(A)V&#x27;H\\)<\/span>. There is exactly one unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U:K\\to K&#x27;\\)\">\\(U:K\\to K&#x27;\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\pi(a)=\\pi&#x27;(a)U\\)\">\\(U\\pi(a)=\\pi&#x27;(a)U\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(UV=V&#x27;\\)\">\\(UV=V&#x27;\\)<\/span>. If a map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;:N\\to B(K&#x27;)\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;:N\\to B(K&#x27;)\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;(N)\\subseteq\\pi&#x27;(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;(N)\\subseteq\\pi&#x27;(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;(x)V&#x27;=V&#x27;x\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;(x)V&#x27;=V&#x27;x\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;(x)=U\\rho(x)U^*\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;(x)=U\\rho(x)U^*\\)<\/span>. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is determined by (6.1), and any such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;\\)<\/span> is automatically a unital normal representation.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in N:\\ a_0x=0\\}=N(1-z)\\)\">\\(\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in N:\\ a_0x=0\\}=N(1-z)\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> is the projection onto the closed span of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;a_0H\\)\">\\(N&#x27;a_0H\\)<\/span>. The projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> lies in the centre <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\cap N&#x27;\\)\">\\(N\\cap N&#x27;\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is faithful if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=1\\)\">\\(z=1\\)<\/span>. This holds when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0\\)\">\\(a_0\\)<\/span> has zero kernel, in particular when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=1\\)\">\\(a_0=1\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>There are completely positive maps for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is not faithful. So a triple as in (6.1) with a faithful <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> need not exist, and the uniqueness in (1) must be stated without faithfulness.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> has a unit, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\xi=q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\)\">\\(V\\xi=q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V^*V=\\varphi(1)\\)\">\\(V^*V=\\varphi(1)\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span> is an isometry if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\varphi(1)=1\\)<\/span>; for general <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=1\\)\">\\(a_0=1\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is faithful, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(a)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(a)\\)<\/span> is the compression of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(a)\\)\">\\(\\pi(a)\\)<\/span> to the subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(VH\\)\">\\(VH\\)<\/span>, identified with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The explicit example in (3) proves that faithfulness cannot be required for every minimal dilation; (2) gives the exact condition.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) For finite sums,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Bigl\\|\\sum_i\\pi&#x27;(x_i)V&#x27;\\xi_i\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle V&#x27;^*\\pi&#x27;(x_j^*x_i)V&#x27;\\xi_i,\\xi_j\\rangle \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(x_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\xi_j\\rangle \\\\\n=\\Bigl\\|\\sum_i\\pi(x_i)V\\xi_i\\Bigr\\|^2.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\Bigl\\|\\sum_i\\pi&#x27;(x_i)V&#x27;\\xi_i\\Bigr\\|^2 \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle V&#x27;^*\\pi&#x27;(x_j^*x_i)V&#x27;\\xi_i,\\xi_j\\rangle \\\\\n=\\sum_{i,j}\\langle\\varphi(x_j^*x_i)\\xi_i,\\xi_j\\rangle \\\\\n=\\Bigl\\|\\sum_i\\pi(x_i)V\\xi_i\\Bigr\\|^2.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nSo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_0:\\sum_i\\pi(x_i)V\\xi_i\\mapsto\\sum_i\\pi&#x27;(x_i)V&#x27;\\xi_i\\)\">\\(U_0:\\sum_i\\pi(x_i)V\\xi_i\\mapsto\\sum_i\\pi&#x27;(x_i)V&#x27;\\xi_i\\)<\/span> is well defined and isometric from a dense subspace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> onto a dense subspace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K&#x27;\\)\">\\(K&#x27;\\)<\/span>. It extends to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span>. The relation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\pi(a)=\\pi&#x27;(a)U\\)\">\\(U\\pi(a)=\\pi&#x27;(a)U\\)<\/span> holds on the dense subspace, hence everywhere. Both representations are nondegenerate, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(u_i)\\to1\\)\">\\(\\pi(u_i)\\to1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi&#x27;(u_i)\\to1\\)\">\\(\\pi&#x27;(u_i)\\to1\\)<\/span> strongly (Section 1). Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(UV\\xi=\\lim_iU\\pi(u_i)V\\xi=\\lim_i\\pi&#x27;(u_i)V&#x27;\\xi=V&#x27;\\xi\\)\">\\(UV\\xi=\\lim_iU\\pi(u_i)V\\xi=\\lim_i\\pi&#x27;(u_i)V&#x27;\\xi=V&#x27;\\xi\\)<\/span>. Any unitary with the two properties satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\pi(a)V\\xi=\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi\\)\">\\(U\\pi(a)V\\xi=\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi\\)<\/span>, so it is unique. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;\\)<\/span>: on the total set of vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi\\)\">\\(\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&#x27;(x)\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi=\\pi&#x27;(a)\\rho&#x27;(x)V&#x27;\\xi=\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;x\\xi\\)\">\\(\\rho&#x27;(x)\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi=\\pi&#x27;(a)\\rho&#x27;(x)V&#x27;\\xi=\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;x\\xi\\)<\/span>. The operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\rho(x)U^*\\)\">\\(U\\rho(x)U^*\\)<\/span> has the same values there, because <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nU\\rho(x)U^*\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi\\\\\n=U\\rho(x)\\pi(a)V\\xi\\\\\n=U\\pi(a)Vx\\xi\\\\\n=\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;x\\xi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nU\\rho(x)U^*\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;\\xi\\\\\n=U\\rho(x)\\pi(a)V\\xi\\\\\n=U\\pi(a)Vx\\xi\\\\\n=\\pi&#x27;(a)V&#x27;x\\xi.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> Two bounded operators that agree on a total set are equal.<\/p>\n<p>(2) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(x)=0\\)\">\\(\\rho(x)=0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Vx=\\rho(x)V=0\\)\">\\(Vx=\\rho(x)V=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0x=V^*Vx=0\\)\">\\(a_0x=V^*Vx=0\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0x=0\\)\">\\(a_0x=0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|Vx\\xi\\|^2=\\langle a_0x\\xi,x\\xi\\rangle=0\\)\">\\(\\|Vx\\xi\\|^2=\\langle a_0x\\xi,x\\xi\\rangle=0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Vx=0\\)\">\\(Vx=0\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(x)\\pi(a)V\\xi=\\pi(a)Vx\\xi=0\\)\">\\(\\rho(x)\\pi(a)V\\xi=\\pi(a)Vx\\xi=0\\)<\/span> on a total set; so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(x)=0\\)\">\\(\\rho(x)=0\\)<\/span>. Next, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0\\)\">\\(a_0\\)<\/span> is a weak limit of elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(A)\\subseteq N&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\varphi(A)\\subseteq N&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;\\)\">\\(N&#x27;\\)<\/span> is weakly closed, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(a_0\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in N\\)\">\\(x\\in N\\)<\/span>; it commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(a_0\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0x=0\\)\">\\(a_0x=0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xa_0=0\\)\">\\(xa_0=0\\)<\/span>, and for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(y\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span> we get <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xya_0=yxa_0=0\\)\">\\(xya_0=yxa_0=0\\)<\/span>; so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> vanishes on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;a_0H\\)\">\\(N&#x27;a_0H\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xz=0\\)\">\\(xz=0\\)<\/span>. Conversely, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xz=0\\)\">\\(xz=0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xa_0=xza_0=0\\)\">\\(xa_0=xza_0=0\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(1\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0H\\subseteq zH\\)\">\\(a_0H\\subseteq zH\\)<\/span>; and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0x=xa_0=0\\)\">\\(a_0x=xa_0=0\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0\\)\">\\(a_0\\)<\/span> commute. The subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(zH\\)\">\\(zH\\)<\/span> is invariant under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;\\)\">\\(N&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xya_0\\xi=ya_0x\\xi\\)\">\\(xya_0\\xi=ya_0x\\xi\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in N\\)\">\\(x\\in N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(y\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Both sets are self-adjoint, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in N&#x27;&#x27;\\cap N&#x27;=N\\cap N&#x27;\\)\">\\(z\\in N&#x27;&#x27;\\cap N&#x27;=N\\cap N&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in N:xz=0\\}=N(1-z)\\)\">\\(\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in N:xz=0\\}=N(1-z)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker a_0=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\ker a_0=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0H\\)\">\\(a_0H\\)<\/span> is dense, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a_0H)^\\perp=\\ker a_0\\)\">\\((a_0H)^\\perp=\\ker a_0\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=1\\)\">\\(z=1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(3) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(A=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(H=\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(\\lambda)=\\lambda e_{11}\\)\">\\(\\varphi(\\lambda)=\\lambda e_{11}\\)<\/span>. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\xi_1,\\xi_2)=\\xi_1\\in\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(V(\\xi_1,\\xi_2)=\\xi_1\\in\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\pi(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(K=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>, we have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(\\lambda)=V^*\\pi(\\lambda)V\\)\">\\(\\varphi(\\lambda)=V^*\\pi(\\lambda)V\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> is CP by Theorem 6.1(1), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is spanned by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(\\mathbb C)VH\\)\">\\(\\pi(\\mathbb C)VH\\)<\/span>. Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\varphi(\\mathbb C)&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=\\varphi(\\mathbb C)&#x27;\\)<\/span> is the algebra of diagonal matrices, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(s,t))V=V\\operatorname{diag}(s,t)\\)\">\\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(s,t))V=V\\operatorname{diag}(s,t)\\)<\/span> forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(s,t))=s\\)\">\\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(s,t))=s\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(0,1))=0\\)\">\\(\\rho(\\operatorname{diag}(0,1))=0\\)<\/span>. By (1), every triple satisfying (6.1) is unitarily equivalent to this one, so none of them has a faithful <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>. In the notation of (2), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=e_{11}\\)\">\\(a_0=e_{11}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;=N\\)\">\\(N&#x27;=N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=e_{11}\\)\">\\(z=e_{11}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(4) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> has a unit, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|u_i-1\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|u_i-1\\|\\to0\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|V_i\\xi-q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\|^2\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|u_i-1\\|^2\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|V_i\\xi-q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\|^2\\le\\|\\varphi\\|\\,\\|u_i-1\\|^2\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span> (Step 3 of the proof of Theorem 6.1); so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\xi=q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\)\">\\(V\\xi=q(1\\otimes\\xi)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_0=\\varphi(1)\\)\">\\(a_0=\\varphi(1)\\)<\/span> by Theorem 6.1(3). <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span> is an isometry exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V^*V=a_0=1\\)\">\\(V^*V=a_0=1\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is faithful by (2), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(VV^*\\)\">\\(VV^*\\)<\/span> is the projection onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(VH\\)\">\\(VH\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/completely-positive-maps.md","start_offset":50276,"end_offset":65882,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f882a7ee7d62b5c399550492cdb2314d53e5f04f84a15b2fde575646ab1c6311":{"markdown":"## 5. Central supports of representations and quasi-equivalence\n\nEach representation of \\(A\\) determines a central projection of \\(\\tilde A\\), its support. Two representations generate isomorphic von Neumann algebras, by an isomorphism compatible with \\(A\\), exactly when their supports agree.\n\n**Definition 5.1.** Representations \\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\) of \\(A\\) are *quasi-equivalent*, written \\(\\pi_1\\sim\\pi_2\\), if there is a \\(*\\)-isomorphism \\(\\theta\\) of \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_1)\\) onto \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_2)\\) with \\(\\theta\\circ\\pi_1=\\pi_2\\). For a representation \\(\\pi\\), the *support* of \\(\\pi\\) is the central projection \\(z(\\pi)\\in\\tilde A\\) of [Lemma 2.1](#oa-fnd-wa-02)(4), so that \\(\\ker\\bar\\pi=\\tilde A(1-z(\\pi))\\). The subspace *associated with* \\(\\pi\\) is\n\\[\nV(\\pi)=\\{\\psi\\circ\\pi:\\psi\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\}\\subseteq A^* .\n\\]\n\nWe do not ask \\(\\theta\\) to be \\(\\sigma\\)-weakly bicontinuous: every \\(*\\)-isomorphism between von Neumann algebras is normal with normal inverse (background), so that requirement would not change the relation.\n\n**Proposition 5.2.**\n\n1. \\(\\psi\\mapsto\\psi\\circ\\pi\\) is an isometry of \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\) onto \\(V(\\pi)\\).\n2. \\(V(\\pi)=A^*z(\\pi)\\): a functional \\(f\\) lies in \\(V(\\pi)\\) exactly when \\(\\hat f=\\hat f z(\\pi)\\). In particular \\(V(\\pi)\\) is a norm-closed invariant subspace, and its support in the sense of [Theorem 4.3](#oa-fnd-wa-07)(3) is \\(z(\\pi)\\).\n3. Every norm-closed invariant subspace \\(V\\subseteq A^*\\) equals \\(V(\\rho)\\) for some representation \\(\\rho\\).\n\n**Proof.** (1) Let \\(\\psi\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\). The functional \\(\\psi\\circ\\bar\\pi\\) is normal on \\(\\tilde A\\) and restricts to \\(\\psi\\circ\\pi\\) on \\(A\\), so it is \\(\\widehat{\\psi\\circ\\pi}\\). The norm of a functional equals the norm of its normal extension, and \\(\\bar\\pi\\) maps the unit ball onto the unit ball (Lemma 2.1(3)). Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|\\psi\\circ\\pi\\|\\\\\n=\\sup_{\\|X\\|\\le1}|\\psi(\\bar\\pi(X))|\\\\\n=\\sup_{\\|y\\|\\le1,\\,y\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)}|\\psi(y)|\\\\\n=\\|\\psi\\| .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\n\n(2) If \\(f=\\psi\\circ\\pi\\), then \\(\\hat f=\\psi\\circ\\bar\\pi\\) vanishes on \\(\\ker\\bar\\pi=\\tilde A(1-z(\\pi))\\), which says \\(\\hat f(X(1-z(\\pi)))=0\\) for all \\(X\\), that is \\(\\hat f=\\hat fz(\\pi)\\). Conversely, if \\(\\hat f\\) vanishes on \\(\\ker\\bar\\pi\\), put \\(\\psi=\\hat f\\circ(\\bar\\pi|_{\\tilde Az(\\pi)})^{-1}\\). This is normal because the inverse is normal (Lemma 2.1(4)), and \\(\\psi\\circ\\pi=f\\) because \\(\\bar\\pi(j(a)z(\\pi))=\\pi(a)\\). The support statement follows from Theorem 4.3(3), \\(z(\\pi)\\) being central.\n\n(3) By Theorem 4.3(3), \\(V=A^*z\\) for a central projection \\(z\\in\\tilde A\\). Realise \\(\\tilde A\\) on \\(H_u\\) through \\(\\bar\\pi_u\\) ([Theorem 3.3](#oa-fnd-wa-03)), let \\(K=\\bar\\pi_u(z)H_u\\), and let \\(\\rho(a)=\\pi_u(a)|_K\\). This is a representation because \\(\\bar\\pi_u(z)\\) commutes with \\(\\pi_u(A)\\). It is nondegenerate: for an approximate unit, \\(\\rho(e_i)\\to1_K\\) strongly, since \\(\\pi_u(e_i)\\to1\\) strongly. The map \\(X\\mapsto\\bar\\pi_u(X)|_K\\) is normal and extends \\(\\rho\\), so it is \\(\\bar\\rho\\) (Lemma 2.1(2)). It vanishes exactly when \\(\\bar\\pi_u(Xz)=0\\), that is when \\(Xz=0\\). So \\(z(\\rho)=z\\) and \\(V(\\rho)=A^*z=V\\) by (2). If \\(z=0\\) this is the zero representation on the zero space. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Theorem 5.3** (Quasi-equivalence and supports). For representations \\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\) of \\(A\\) the following are equivalent: (i) \\(\\pi_1\\sim\\pi_2\\); (ii) \\(V(\\pi_1)=V(\\pi_2)\\); (iii) \\(z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\). When they hold, the isomorphism \\(\\theta\\) in (i) is unique, and \\(\\theta\\) and \\(\\theta^{-1}\\) are normal.\n\n**Proof.** (ii)\\(\\Leftrightarrow\\)(iii): \\(V(\\pi_k)=A^*z(\\pi_k)\\), and the support of an invariant subspace is unique (Theorem 4.3(3)).\n\n(iii)\\(\\Rightarrow\\)(i): let \\(z=z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\). By Lemma 2.1(4), each \\(\\bar\\pi_k\\) restricts to a \\(*\\)-isomorphism of \\(\\tilde Az\\) onto \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_k)\\), normal with normal inverse. Put \\(\\theta=\\bar\\pi_2\\circ(\\bar\\pi_1|_{\\tilde Az})^{-1}\\). Since \\(\\bar\\pi_k(j(a))=\\bar\\pi_k(j(a)z)\\), we get \\(\\theta(\\pi_1(a))=\\pi_2(a)\\).\n\n(i)\\(\\Rightarrow\\)(iii): let \\(\\theta\\) be a \\(*\\)-isomorphism with \\(\\theta\\circ\\pi_1=\\pi_2\\). A \\(*\\)-isomorphism between von Neumann algebras is normal, and so is its inverse: use [Corollary 11.4](#oa-fnd-wa-24), whose proof from predual uniqueness is independent of this quasi-equivalence theorem. Then \\(\\theta\\circ\\bar\\pi_1\\) is a normal \\(*\\)-homomorphism extending \\(\\pi_2\\), so it equals \\(\\bar\\pi_2\\) (Lemma 2.1(2)). As \\(\\theta\\) is injective, \\(\\ker\\bar\\pi_2=\\ker\\bar\\pi_1\\), and so \\(z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\). Uniqueness of \\(\\theta\\): it is normal and determined on the \\(\\sigma\\)-weakly dense set \\(\\pi_1(A)\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Remark 5.4** (Degenerate representations). With \\(N(\\pi)\\) of [Lemma 2.1](#oa-fnd-wa-02) in place of \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\), everything above holds for possibly degenerate representations, with the same proofs. With \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\pi(A)''\\) it fails. Take \\(A=\\mathbb C\\), \\(\\pi_1=\\mathrm{id}\\) on \\(\\mathbb C\\) and \\(\\pi_2(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\oplus0\\) on \\(\\mathbb C^2\\). Both have support \\(1\\) and \\(V(\\pi_k)=A^*\\), but \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_1)=\\mathbb C\\) and \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_2)\\cong\\mathbb C^2\\) are not isomorphic.\n\nTwo examples show the bidual concretely.\n\nFor completeness the sequence-space dualities here are elementary. If \\(f\\in c_0^*\\), put \\(c_n=f(e_n)\\). Choosing phases on any finite set gives \\(\\sum_{n\\in F}|c_n|\\le\\|f\\|\\), so \\(c\\in\\ell^1\\). Finite sequences are norm dense in \\(c_0\\), hence \\(f(x)=\\sum_nc_nx_n\\), and the same finite phase tests give \\(\\|f\\|=\\|c\\|_1\\). Conversely each \\(\\ell^1\\) sequence defines such a functional. Similarly every \\(g\\in(\\ell^1)^*\\) is \\(g(c)=\\sum_nb_nc_n\\) for \\(b_n=g(e_n)\\in\\ell^\\infty\\), by density of finite sequences, and \\(\\|g\\|=\\|b\\|_\\infty\\). Thus \\(c_0^{**}=\\ell^\\infty\\), with evaluation embedding the usual inclusion.\n\n**Example 5.5** (\\(c_0^{**}=\\ell^\\infty\\)). The identity representation of \\(c_0\\) on \\(\\ell^2\\) is nondegenerate, and \\(\\mathcal M(\\mathrm{id})=\\ell^\\infty\\) (diagonal operators). Its normal functionals are \\(\\ell^1\\) (background fact on preduals), and their restrictions to \\(c_0\\) are all of \\(c_0^*=\\ell^1\\). So \\(V(\\mathrm{id})=c_0^*\\), \\(z(\\mathrm{id})=1\\), and \\(\\overline{\\mathrm{id}}:\\tilde{c_0}\\to\\ell^\\infty\\) is an isomorphism ([Proposition 5.2](#oa-fnd-wa-09) and Lemma 2.1(4)). Here \\(j(c_0)\\) is \\(\\sigma\\)-weakly dense but far from closed: the unit \\(1\\in\\ell^\\infty\\) is a weak\\(^*\\) limit of the finite sequences \\(1_{\\{1,\\dots,n\\}}\\).\n\n**Example 5.6** (\\(\\mathcal K(H)^{**}=B(H)\\)). The identity representation of \\(\\mathcal K(H)\\) is nondegenerate and irreducible, and \\(\\mathcal K(H)''=B(H)\\). We show that every positive \\(f\\in\\mathcal K(H)^*\\) is normal on \\(B(H)\\). For a finite-rank projection \\(P\\), the compression of \\(f\\) to \\(P\\mathcal K(H)P=B(PH)\\) is \\(x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_Px)\\) for a unique \\(\\rho_P\\ge0\\) on \\(PH\\), with \\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_P=f(P)\\le\\|f\\|\\), and \\(\\rho_P=P\\rho_QP\\) for \\(P\\le Q\\). So there is a positive trace-class \\(\\rho\\) with \\(P\\rho P=\\rho_P\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho\\le\\|f\\|\\): put \\(\\langle\\rho\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle\\rho_P\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\) for any \\(P\\) whose range contains \\(\\xi\\) and \\(\\eta\\), which the compatibility makes independent of \\(P\\), and note \\(\\sum_k\\langle\\rho e_k,e_k\\rangle=\\sup_P\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_P\\le\\|f\\|\\) for an orthonormal basis \\((e_k)\\). For compact \\(x\\), \\(PxP\\to x\\) in norm along the finite-rank projections, so \\(f(x)=\\lim f(PxP)=\\lim\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho PxP)=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho x)\\), and \\(x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho x)\\) is a vector series, hence normal. As positive functionals span \\(\\mathcal K(H)^*\\) (see the proof of Proposition 1.1(2)), \\(V(\\mathrm{id})=\\mathcal K(H)^*\\), so \\(z(\\mathrm{id})=1\\) and \\(\\mathcal K(H)^{**}\\cong B(H)\\).\n\nA representation of an ideal extends to the whole algebra, and the bidual shows why.\n\n**Proposition 5.7** (Extending representations from an ideal). Let \\(J\\) be a closed two-sided ideal of \\(A\\) and \\(\\pi\\) a representation of \\(J\\) on \\(H\\). There is exactly one representation \\(\\pi^A\\) of \\(A\\) on \\(H\\) with \\(\\pi^A|_J=\\pi\\). It satisfies \\(\\pi^A(a)\\pi(x)\\zeta=\\pi(ax)\\zeta\\) for \\(a\\in A\\), \\(x\\in J\\), \\(\\zeta\\in H\\), and \\(\\pi^A(A)''=\\pi(J)''\\).\n\n**Proof.** Let \\(i:J\\to A\\) be the inclusion. By the background fact on second adjoints, \\(i^{**}:J^{**}\\to\\tilde A\\) is an isometric normal \\(*\\)-homomorphism, and its range is the \\(\\sigma\\)-weak closure of \\(j_A(J)\\). This closure is a two-sided ideal of \\(\\tilde A\\): it is stable under multiplication by \\(j_A(A)\\) because \\(J\\) is an ideal and multiplication is separately continuous, and then under multiplication by \\(\\tilde A\\) by density of \\(j_A(A)\\). By [Lemma 4.2](#oa-fnd-wa-06) it equals \\(\\tilde Az\\) for a central projection \\(z\\). Let \\(\\bar\\pi:J^{**}\\to\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\pi(J)''\\) be the normal extension ([Lemma 2.1](#oa-fnd-wa-02)). Define\n\\[\n\\pi^A(a)=\\bar\\pi\\big((i^{**})^{-1}(j_A(a)z)\\big)\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]\nThe map \\(a\\mapsto j_A(a)z\\) is a \\(*\\)-homomorphism into \\(\\tilde Az\\), because \\(z\\) is central; \\((i^{**})^{-1}\\) is a \\(*\\)-isomorphism of \\(\\tilde Az\\) onto \\(J^{**}\\); and \\(\\bar\\pi\\) is a \\(*\\)-homomorphism. So \\(\\pi^A\\) is a \\(*\\)-homomorphism. For \\(x\\in J\\), \\(j_A(x)=i^{**}(j_J(x))\\) lies in \\(\\tilde Az\\), so \\(\\pi^A(x)=\\bar\\pi(j_J(x))=\\pi(x)\\). Next, \\(\\pi(J)\\subseteq\\pi^A(A)\\subseteq\\bar\\pi(J^{**})=\\pi(J)''\\), so \\(\\pi^A(A)''=\\pi(J)''\\); in particular \\(\\pi^A\\) is nondegenerate. For uniqueness, any extension \\(\\rho\\) satisfies \\(\\rho(a)\\pi(x)\\zeta=\\rho(ax)\\zeta=\\pi(ax)\\zeta\\), and the vectors \\(\\pi(x)\\zeta\\) span a dense subspace. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Exercise 5.8.** (easy) Let \\(A\\ne0\\) and let \\(\\pi=0\\) on a Hilbert space \\(H\\ne0\\). Compute \\(N(\\pi)\\), \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\), \\(\\bar\\pi\\), \\(z(\\pi)\\) and \\(V(\\pi)\\), and say which statements of Lemma 2.1 would fail with \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\) in place of \\(N(\\pi)\\).\n\n**Solution.** \\(N(\\pi)=\\{0\\}\\), \\(\\bar\\pi=0\\), \\(z(\\pi)=0\\) and \\(V(\\pi)=\\{0\\}\\). But \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\{0\\}''=\\mathbb C1\\). So \\(\\bar\\pi\\) is not onto \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\), and the unit ball of \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\) is not the image of that of \\(\\tilde A\\). With \\(N(\\pi)\\) in place of \\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\), [Lemma 2.1](#oa-fnd-wa-02) holds, in agreement with (2.1): \\(\\pi(A)''=\\{0\\}+\\mathbb C1\\). \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>5. Central supports of representations and quasi-equivalence<\/h2>\n<p>Each representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> determines a central projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span>, its support. Two representations generate isomorphic von Neumann algebras, by an isomorphism compatible with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, exactly when their supports agree.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Definition 5.1.<\/strong> Representations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> are <em>quasi-equivalent<\/em>, written <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_1\\sim\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\pi_1\\sim\\pi_2\\)<\/span>, if there is a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_1)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_1)\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_2)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_2)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\circ\\pi_1=\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\theta\\circ\\pi_1=\\pi_2\\)<\/span>. For a representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span>, the <em>support<\/em> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is the central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi)\\in\\tilde A\\)\">\\(z(\\pi)\\in\\tilde A\\)<\/span> of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-02\">Lemma 2.1<\/a>(4), so that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi=\\tilde A(1-z(\\pi))\\)\">\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi=\\tilde A(1-z(\\pi))\\)<\/span>. The subspace <em>associated with<\/em> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nV(\\pi)=\\{\\psi\\circ\\pi:\\psi\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\}\\subseteq A^* .\n\\]\">\\[\nV(\\pi)=\\{\\psi\\circ\\pi:\\psi\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\}\\subseteq A^* .\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>We do not ask <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> to be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weakly bicontinuous: every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism between von Neumann algebras is normal with normal inverse (background), so that requirement would not change the relation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 5.2.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\mapsto\\psi\\circ\\pi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\mapsto\\psi\\circ\\pi\\)<\/span> is an isometry of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi)=A^*z(\\pi)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi)=A^*z(\\pi)\\)<\/span>: a functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi)\\)<\/span> exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\hat f=\\hat f z(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\hat f=\\hat f z(\\pi)\\)<\/span>. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi)\\)<\/span> is a norm-closed invariant subspace, and its support in the sense of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-07\">Theorem 4.3<\/a>(3) is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi)\\)\">\\(z(\\pi)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Every norm-closed invariant subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\subseteq A^*\\)\">\\(V\\subseteq A^*\\)<\/span> equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\rho)\\)\">\\(V(\\rho)\\)<\/span> for some representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\)\">\\(\\psi\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)_*\\)<\/span>. The functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\circ\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\circ\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span> is normal on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span> and restricts to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\circ\\pi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\circ\\pi\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, so it is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat{\\psi\\circ\\pi}\\)\">\\(\\widehat{\\psi\\circ\\pi}\\)<\/span>. The norm of a functional equals the norm of its normal extension, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span> maps the unit ball onto the unit ball (Lemma 2.1(3)). Hence\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|\\psi\\circ\\pi\\|\\\\\n=\\sup_{\\|X\\|\\le1}|\\psi(\\bar\\pi(X))|\\\\\n=\\sup_{\\|y\\|\\le1,\\,y\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)}|\\psi(y)|\\\\\n=\\|\\psi\\| .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|\\psi\\circ\\pi\\|\\\\\n=\\sup_{\\|X\\|\\le1}|\\psi(\\bar\\pi(X))|\\\\\n=\\sup_{\\|y\\|\\le1,\\,y\\in\\mathcal M(\\pi)}|\\psi(y)|\\\\\n=\\|\\psi\\| .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>(2) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f=\\psi\\circ\\pi\\)\">\\(f=\\psi\\circ\\pi\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\hat f=\\psi\\circ\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\hat f=\\psi\\circ\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span> vanishes on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi=\\tilde A(1-z(\\pi))\\)\">\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi=\\tilde A(1-z(\\pi))\\)<\/span>, which says <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\hat f(X(1-z(\\pi)))=0\\)\">\\(\\hat f(X(1-z(\\pi)))=0\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>, that is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\hat f=\\hat fz(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\hat f=\\hat fz(\\pi)\\)<\/span>. Conversely, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\hat f\\)\">\\(\\hat f\\)<\/span> vanishes on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi=\\hat f\\circ(\\bar\\pi|_{\\tilde Az(\\pi)})^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\psi=\\hat f\\circ(\\bar\\pi|_{\\tilde Az(\\pi)})^{-1}\\)<\/span>. This is normal because the inverse is normal (Lemma 2.1(4)), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\circ\\pi=f\\)\">\\(\\psi\\circ\\pi=f\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi(j(a)z(\\pi))=\\pi(a)\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi(j(a)z(\\pi))=\\pi(a)\\)<\/span>. The support statement follows from Theorem 4.3(3), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi)\\)\">\\(z(\\pi)\\)<\/span> being central.<\/p>\n<p>(3) By Theorem 4.3(3), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V=A^*z\\)\">\\(V=A^*z\\)<\/span> for a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in\\tilde A\\)\">\\(z\\in\\tilde A\\)<\/span>. Realise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_u\\)\">\\(H_u\\)<\/span> through <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi_u\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi_u\\)<\/span> (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-03\">Theorem 3.3<\/a>), let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\bar\\pi_u(z)H_u\\)\">\\(K=\\bar\\pi_u(z)H_u\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a)=\\pi_u(a)|_K\\)\">\\(\\rho(a)=\\pi_u(a)|_K\\)<\/span>. This is a representation because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi_u(z)\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi_u(z)\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_u(A)\\)\">\\(\\pi_u(A)\\)<\/span>. It is nondegenerate: for an approximate unit, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(e_i)\\to1_K\\)\">\\(\\rho(e_i)\\to1_K\\)<\/span> strongly, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_u(e_i)\\to1\\)\">\\(\\pi_u(e_i)\\to1\\)<\/span> strongly. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\mapsto\\bar\\pi_u(X)|_K\\)\">\\(X\\mapsto\\bar\\pi_u(X)|_K\\)<\/span> is normal and extends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>, so it is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\rho\\)\">\\(\\bar\\rho\\)<\/span> (Lemma 2.1(2)). It vanishes exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi_u(Xz)=0\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi_u(Xz)=0\\)<\/span>, that is when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Xz=0\\)\">\\(Xz=0\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\rho)=z\\)\">\\(z(\\rho)=z\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\rho)=A^*z=V\\)\">\\(V(\\rho)=A^*z=V\\)<\/span> by (2). If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=0\\)\">\\(z=0\\)<\/span> this is the zero representation on the zero space. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 5.3<\/strong> (Quasi-equivalence and supports). For representations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> the following are equivalent: (i) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_1\\sim\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\pi_1\\sim\\pi_2\\)<\/span>; (ii) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi_1)=V(\\pi_2)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi_1)=V(\\pi_2)\\)<\/span>; (iii) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\)\">\\(z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\)<\/span>. When they hold, the isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> in (i) is unique, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\theta^{-1}\\)<\/span> are normal.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (ii)<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Leftrightarrow\\)\">\\(\\Leftrightarrow\\)<\/span>(iii): <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi_k)=A^*z(\\pi_k)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi_k)=A^*z(\\pi_k)\\)<\/span>, and the support of an invariant subspace is unique (Theorem 4.3(3)).<\/p>\n<p>(iii)<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Rightarrow\\)\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)<\/span>(i): let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\)\">\\(z=z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\)<\/span>. By Lemma 2.1(4), each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi_k\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi_k\\)<\/span> restricts to a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde Az\\)\">\\(\\tilde Az\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_k)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_k)\\)<\/span>, normal with normal inverse. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta=\\bar\\pi_2\\circ(\\bar\\pi_1|_{\\tilde Az})^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\theta=\\bar\\pi_2\\circ(\\bar\\pi_1|_{\\tilde Az})^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi_k(j(a))=\\bar\\pi_k(j(a)z)\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi_k(j(a))=\\bar\\pi_k(j(a)z)\\)<\/span>, we get <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta(\\pi_1(a))=\\pi_2(a)\\)\">\\(\\theta(\\pi_1(a))=\\pi_2(a)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(i)<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Rightarrow\\)\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)<\/span>(iii): let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> be a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\circ\\pi_1=\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\theta\\circ\\pi_1=\\pi_2\\)<\/span>. A <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism between von Neumann algebras is normal, and so is its inverse: use <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-24\">Corollary 11.4<\/a>, whose proof from predual uniqueness is independent of this quasi-equivalence theorem. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\circ\\bar\\pi_1\\)\">\\(\\theta\\circ\\bar\\pi_1\\)<\/span> is a normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-homomorphism extending <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\pi_2\\)<\/span>, so it equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi_2\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi_2\\)<\/span> (Lemma 2.1(2)). As <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> is injective, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi_2=\\ker\\bar\\pi_1\\)\">\\(\\ker\\bar\\pi_2=\\ker\\bar\\pi_1\\)<\/span>, and so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\)\">\\(z(\\pi_1)=z(\\pi_2)\\)<\/span>. Uniqueness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span>: it is normal and determined on the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weakly dense set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_1(A)\\)\">\\(\\pi_1(A)\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Remark 5.4<\/strong> (Degenerate representations). With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N(\\pi)\\)\">\\(N(\\pi)\\)<\/span> of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-02\">Lemma 2.1<\/a> in place of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)<\/span>, everything above holds for possibly degenerate representations, with the same proofs. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span> it fails. Take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(A=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_1=\\mathrm{id}\\)\">\\(\\pi_1=\\mathrm{id}\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_2(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\oplus0\\)\">\\(\\pi_2(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\oplus0\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>. Both have support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi_k)=A^*\\)\">\\(V(\\pi_k)=A^*\\)<\/span>, but <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_1)=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_1)=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_2)\\cong\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi_2)\\cong\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span> are not isomorphic.<\/p>\n<p>Two examples show the bidual concretely.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness the sequence-space dualities here are elementary. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in c_0^*\\)\">\\(f\\in c_0^*\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_n=f(e_n)\\)\">\\(c_n=f(e_n)\\)<\/span>. Choosing phases on any finite set gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{n\\in F}|c_n|\\le\\|f\\|\\)\">\\(\\sum_{n\\in F}|c_n|\\le\\|f\\|\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\in\\ell^1\\)\">\\(c\\in\\ell^1\\)<\/span>. Finite sequences are norm dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0\\)\">\\(c_0\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)=\\sum_nc_nx_n\\)\">\\(f(x)=\\sum_nc_nx_n\\)<\/span>, and the same finite phase tests give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|f\\|=\\|c\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|f\\|=\\|c\\|_1\\)<\/span>. Conversely each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^1\\)\">\\(\\ell^1\\)<\/span> sequence defines such a functional. Similarly every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\in(\\ell^1)^*\\)\">\\(g\\in(\\ell^1)^*\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g(c)=\\sum_nb_nc_n\\)\">\\(g(c)=\\sum_nb_nc_n\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_n=g(e_n)\\in\\ell^\\infty\\)\">\\(b_n=g(e_n)\\in\\ell^\\infty\\)<\/span>, by density of finite sequences, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|g\\|=\\|b\\|_\\infty\\)\">\\(\\|g\\|=\\|b\\|_\\infty\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0^{**}=\\ell^\\infty\\)\">\\(c_0^{**}=\\ell^\\infty\\)<\/span>, with evaluation embedding the usual inclusion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example 5.5<\/strong> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0^{**}=\\ell^\\infty\\)\">\\(c_0^{**}=\\ell^\\infty\\)<\/span>). The identity representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0\\)\">\\(c_0\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2\\)\">\\(\\ell^2\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\mathrm{id})=\\ell^\\infty\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\mathrm{id})=\\ell^\\infty\\)<\/span> (diagonal operators). Its normal functionals are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^1\\)\">\\(\\ell^1\\)<\/span> (background fact on preduals), and their restrictions to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0\\)\">\\(c_0\\)<\/span> are all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0^*=\\ell^1\\)\">\\(c_0^*=\\ell^1\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\mathrm{id})=c_0^*\\)\">\\(V(\\mathrm{id})=c_0^*\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\mathrm{id})=1\\)\">\\(z(\\mathrm{id})=1\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline{\\mathrm{id}}:\\tilde{c_0}\\to\\ell^\\infty\\)\">\\(\\overline{\\mathrm{id}}:\\tilde{c_0}\\to\\ell^\\infty\\)<\/span> is an isomorphism (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-09\">Proposition 5.2<\/a> and Lemma 2.1(4)). Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(c_0)\\)\">\\(j(c_0)\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weakly dense but far from closed: the unit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\in\\ell^\\infty\\)\">\\(1\\in\\ell^\\infty\\)<\/span> is a weak<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(^*\\)\">\\(^*\\)<\/span> limit of the finite sequences <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_{\\{1,\\dots,n\\}}\\)\">\\(1_{\\{1,\\dots,n\\}}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example 5.6<\/strong> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K(H)^{**}=B(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K(H)^{**}=B(H)\\)<\/span>). The identity representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K(H)\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate and irreducible, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K(H)&#x27;&#x27;=B(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K(H)&#x27;&#x27;=B(H)\\)<\/span>. We show that every positive <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in\\mathcal K(H)^*\\)\">\\(f\\in\\mathcal K(H)^*\\)<\/span> is normal on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span>. For a finite-rank projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, the compression of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\mathcal K(H)P=B(PH)\\)\">\\(P\\mathcal K(H)P=B(PH)\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_Px)\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_Px)\\)<\/span> for a unique <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_P\\ge0\\)\">\\(\\rho_P\\ge0\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(PH\\)\">\\(PH\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_P=f(P)\\le\\|f\\|\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_P=f(P)\\le\\|f\\|\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_P=P\\rho_QP\\)\">\\(\\rho_P=P\\rho_QP\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\le Q\\)\">\\(P\\le Q\\)<\/span>. So there is a positive trace-class <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\rho P=\\rho_P\\)\">\\(P\\rho P=\\rho_P\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho\\le\\|f\\|\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho\\le\\|f\\|\\)<\/span>: put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle\\rho\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle\\rho_P\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle\\rho\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle\\rho_P\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)<\/span> for any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> whose range contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span>, which the compatibility makes independent of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, and note <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_k\\langle\\rho e_k,e_k\\rangle=\\sup_P\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_P\\le\\|f\\|\\)\">\\(\\sum_k\\langle\\rho e_k,e_k\\rangle=\\sup_P\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho_P\\le\\|f\\|\\)<\/span> for an orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_k)\\)\">\\((e_k)\\)<\/span>. For compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(PxP\\to x\\)\">\\(PxP\\to x\\)<\/span> in norm along the finite-rank projections, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)=\\lim f(PxP)=\\lim\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho PxP)=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho x)\\)\">\\(f(x)=\\lim f(PxP)=\\lim\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho PxP)=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho x)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho x)\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho x)\\)<\/span> is a vector series, hence normal. As positive functionals span <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K(H)^*\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K(H)^*\\)<\/span> (see the proof of Proposition 1.1(2)), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\mathrm{id})=\\mathcal K(H)^*\\)\">\\(V(\\mathrm{id})=\\mathcal K(H)^*\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\mathrm{id})=1\\)\">\\(z(\\mathrm{id})=1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K(H)^{**}\\cong B(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K(H)^{**}\\cong B(H)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>A representation of an ideal extends to the whole algebra, and the bidual shows why.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 5.7<\/strong> (Extending representations from an ideal). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> be a closed two-sided ideal of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> a representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>. There is exactly one representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A\\)\">\\(\\pi^A\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A|_J=\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi^A|_J=\\pi\\)<\/span>. It satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A(a)\\pi(x)\\zeta=\\pi(ax)\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\pi^A(a)\\pi(x)\\zeta=\\pi(ax)\\zeta\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in J\\)\">\\(x\\in J\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\in H\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\in H\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A(A)&#x27;&#x27;=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi^A(A)&#x27;&#x27;=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i:J\\to A\\)\">\\(i:J\\to A\\)<\/span> be the inclusion. By the background fact on second adjoints, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i^{**}:J^{**}\\to\\tilde A\\)\">\\(i^{**}:J^{**}\\to\\tilde A\\)<\/span> is an isometric normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-homomorphism, and its range is the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weak closure of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_A(J)\\)\">\\(j_A(J)\\)<\/span>. This closure is a two-sided ideal of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span>: it is stable under multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_A(A)\\)\">\\(j_A(A)\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> is an ideal and multiplication is separately continuous, and then under multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span> by density of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_A(A)\\)\">\\(j_A(A)\\)<\/span>. By <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-06\">Lemma 4.2<\/a> it equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde Az\\)\">\\(\\tilde Az\\)<\/span> for a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi:J^{**}\\to\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi:J^{**}\\to\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span> be the normal extension (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-02\">Lemma 2.1<\/a>). Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\pi^A(a)=\\bar\\pi\\big((i^{**})^{-1}(j_A(a)z)\\big)\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]\">\\[\n\\pi^A(a)=\\bar\\pi\\big((i^{**})^{-1}(j_A(a)z)\\big)\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\mapsto j_A(a)z\\)\">\\(a\\mapsto j_A(a)z\\)<\/span> is a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-homomorphism into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde Az\\)\">\\(\\tilde Az\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> is central; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((i^{**})^{-1}\\)\">\\((i^{**})^{-1}\\)<\/span> is a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde Az\\)\">\\(\\tilde Az\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J^{**}\\)\">\\(J^{**}\\)<\/span>; and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span> is a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-homomorphism. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A\\)\">\\(\\pi^A\\)<\/span> is a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-homomorphism. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in J\\)\">\\(x\\in J\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j_A(x)=i^{**}(j_J(x))\\)\">\\(j_A(x)=i^{**}(j_J(x))\\)<\/span> lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde Az\\)\">\\(\\tilde Az\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A(x)=\\bar\\pi(j_J(x))=\\pi(x)\\)\">\\(\\pi^A(x)=\\bar\\pi(j_J(x))=\\pi(x)\\)<\/span>. Next, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(J)\\subseteq\\pi^A(A)\\subseteq\\bar\\pi(J^{**})=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi(J)\\subseteq\\pi^A(A)\\subseteq\\bar\\pi(J^{**})=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A(A)&#x27;&#x27;=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi^A(A)&#x27;&#x27;=\\pi(J)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>; in particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi^A\\)\">\\(\\pi^A\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate. For uniqueness, any extension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a)\\pi(x)\\zeta=\\rho(ax)\\zeta=\\pi(ax)\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\rho(a)\\pi(x)\\zeta=\\rho(ax)\\zeta=\\pi(ax)\\zeta\\)<\/span>, and the vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(x)\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\pi(x)\\zeta\\)<\/span> span a dense subspace. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Exercise 5.8.<\/strong> (easy) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\ne0\\)\">\\(A\\ne0\\)<\/span> and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi=0\\)\">\\(\\pi=0\\)<\/span> on a Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\ne0\\)\">\\(H\\ne0\\)<\/span>. Compute <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N(\\pi)\\)\">\\(N(\\pi)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi)\\)\">\\(z(\\pi)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi)\\)\">\\(V(\\pi)\\)<\/span>, and say which statements of Lemma 2.1 would fail with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)<\/span> in place of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N(\\pi)\\)\">\\(N(\\pi)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N(\\pi)=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(N(\\pi)=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi=0\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi=0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z(\\pi)=0\\)\">\\(z(\\pi)=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V(\\pi)=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(V(\\pi)=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>. But <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\{0\\}&#x27;&#x27;=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)=\\{0\\}&#x27;&#x27;=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\pi\\)\">\\(\\bar\\pi\\)<\/span> is not onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)<\/span>, and the unit ball of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)<\/span> is not the image of that of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span>. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N(\\pi)\\)\">\\(N(\\pi)\\)<\/span> in place of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal M(\\pi)\\)<\/span>, <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-wa-02\">Lemma 2.1<\/a> holds, in agreement with (2.1): <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;=\\{0\\}+\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;=\\{0\\}+\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.md","start_offset":58064,"end_offset":68382,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f8d3247d32100836b03f79d92498ecf9fe741f9de6c86969c740408d2d3e1de4":{"markdown":"## 18. Vector states and spatial isomorphisms\n\nThe transfer theorem answers three natural questions: when one cyclic representation of a C\\(^*\\)-algebra is contained in another; which normal functionals are vector functionals; and, for algebras with cyclic and separating vectors, whether an isomorphism is implemented by a unitary.\n\n**Proposition 18.1** (Subrepresentations and cyclic projections). Let \\(A\\) be a C\\(^*\\)-algebra, \\(\\{\\pi,H\\}\\) a nondegenerate representation of \\(A\\), \\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\), \\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\circ\\pi\\), \\(\\psi=\\omega_\\eta\\circ\\pi\\), and \\(N=\\pi(A)''\\). The following are equivalent.\n\n1. The cyclic representation \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) of \\(\\varphi\\) *embeds* in \\(\\pi_\\psi\\): some isometry \\(H_\\varphi\\to H_\\psi\\) intertwines \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) with \\(\\pi_\\psi\\). (Its range is then an invariant subspace, so this says that \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) is carried by a unitary onto a subrepresentation of \\(\\pi_\\psi\\).)\n2. \\(p'_\\xi\\precsim p'_\\eta\\) in \\(N'=\\pi(A)'\\).\n3. \\(p_\\xi\\precsim p_\\eta\\) in \\(N\\).\n\nIn the universal representation (Fact 2.7), \\(p_\\xi=s(\\varphi)\\) and \\(p_\\eta=s(\\psi)\\). So \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) embeds in \\(\\pi_\\psi\\) exactly when \\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim s(\\psi)\\) in \\(\\tilde A\\).\n\n**Proof.** Since \\(\\pi\\) is nondegenerate, \\(\\pi(A)\\) is strongly dense in \\(N\\) by the double commutant theorem (Fact 2.7). So every vector \\(a\\xi\\) with \\(a\\in N\\) is a limit of vectors \\(\\pi(b)\\xi\\) with \\(b\\in A\\), and \\([N\\xi]=[\\pi(A)\\xi]\\). Thus \\(p'_\\xi\\) is the projection onto \\([\\pi(A)\\xi]\\). The map \\(\\pi_\\varphi(a)\\xi_\\varphi\\mapsto\\pi(a)\\xi\\) is isometric, since both sides have squared norm \\(\\varphi(a^*a)\\). It carries a dense subspace of \\(H_\\varphi\\) onto a dense subspace of \\(p'_\\xi H\\), so it extends to a unitary \\(U_\\xi:H_\\varphi\\to p'_\\xi H\\) that intertwines \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) with the restriction of \\(\\pi\\) to \\(p'_\\xi H\\). The same holds for \\(\\eta\\) and \\(\\psi\\).\n\n(2)⇒(1). Let \\(v\\in\\pi(A)'\\) have \\(v^*v=p'_\\xi\\) and \\(vv^*\\le p'_\\eta\\). The range \\(L=vv^*H\\) is invariant under \\(\\pi(A)\\), and \\(v\\) restricts to a unitary \\(p'_\\xi H\\to L\\) that intertwines the restrictions of \\(\\pi\\). Composing with \\(U_\\xi\\) and \\(U_\\eta^{-1}\\) shows that \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) is equivalent to the restriction of \\(\\pi_\\psi\\) to the invariant subspace \\(U_\\eta^*L\\).\n\n(1)⇒(2). Let \\(T:H_\\varphi\\to L_0\\) be a unitary onto a closed \\(\\pi_\\psi\\)-invariant subspace, intertwining \\(\\pi_\\varphi\\) with the restriction of \\(\\pi_\\psi\\). Define \\(v=U_\\eta TU_\\xi^*\\) on \\(p'_\\xi H\\) and \\(v=0\\) on its orthogonal complement. On \\(p'_\\xi H\\), \\(v\\) intertwines. On \\((p'_\\xi H)^\\perp\\), both \\(v\\pi(a)\\) and \\(\\pi(a)v\\) vanish, because \\(\\pi(a)\\) leaves that space invariant. So \\(v\\in\\pi(A)'\\), \\(v^*v=p'_\\xi\\) and \\(vv^*\\le p'_\\eta\\).\n\n(2)⇔(3) is Theorem 17.5 in \\(N\\), whose commutant is \\(\\pi(A)'\\).\n\nIn the state-indexed universal representation, normalize each nonzero functional and rescale its cyclic vector as in Fact 2.7; use the zero vector for a zero functional. This gives \\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H_u\\) with \\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\circ\\pi_u\\) and \\(\\psi=\\omega_\\eta\\circ\\pi_u\\). The restriction of \\(\\omega_\\xi\\) to \\(\\tilde A\\) is \\(\\sigma\\)-weakly continuous and extends \\(\\varphi\\), so it is \\(\\tilde\\varphi\\), and its support is \\(p_\\xi\\) ([Lemma 4.6](#oa-fnd-ty-07)). The same holds for \\(\\psi\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 18.2** (Which normal functionals are vector functionals). Let \\(\\xi_0\\in H\\) and let \\(\\varphi\\) be a positive normal functional on \\(M\\). Then \\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi|_M\\) for some \\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\) if and only if \\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\). In particular, if \\(M\\) has a separating vector \\(\\xi_0\\), then \\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\), and every positive normal functional on \\(M\\) is a vector functional \\(\\omega_\\xi\\) with \\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\).\n\n**Proof.** If \\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\) with \\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\), then \\(s(\\varphi)=p_\\xi\\) ([Lemma 4.6](#oa-fnd-ty-07)), and \\(p_\\xi\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\) by Step 2 of the proof of Theorem 17.5.\n\nConversely, suppose \\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\). By Fact 2.5, \\(\\varphi=\\sum_n\\omega_{\\xi_n}\\) with \\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\). On \\(K=H\\otimes\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\), with orthonormal basis \\((\\delta_n)\\) of \\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\), let \\(N=M\\otimes1\\). By [Lemma 8.2](#oa-fnd-ty-11), \\(N\\) is a von Neumann algebra, its commutant \\(N'\\) consists of the operators whose matrix entries lie in \\(M'\\), and \\(x\\mapsto x\\otimes1\\) is an isomorphism of \\(M\\) onto \\(N\\). Put \\(\\zeta=\\sum_n\\xi_n\\otimes\\delta_n\\) and \\(\\zeta_0=\\xi_0\\otimes\\delta_1\\). Then \\(\\omega_\\zeta(x\\otimes1)=\\varphi(x)\\). A projection \\(p\\otimes1\\) of \\(N\\) fixes \\(\\zeta\\) iff \\(\\varphi(1-p)=0\\), so the cyclic projection of \\(N\\) at \\(\\zeta\\) is \\(p^N_\\zeta=s(\\varphi)\\otimes1\\). Next, \\([N'\\zeta_0]=[M'\\xi_0]\\otimes\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\): the inclusion \"⊇\" holds because \\(N'\\) contains the operators \\(x'\\otimes1\\) and the matrix units \\(1\\otimes E_{n1}\\), and \"⊆\" holds because every \\(T\\in N'\\) maps \\(\\zeta_0\\) to \\(\\sum_nT_{n1}\\xi_0\\otimes\\delta_n\\) with \\(T_{n1}\\in M'\\). So \\(p^N_{\\zeta_0}=p_{\\xi_0}\\otimes1\\). The isomorphism \\(x\\mapsto x\\otimes1\\) turns \\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\) into \\(p^N_\\zeta\\precsim p^N_{\\zeta_0}\\). By Theorem 17.5 in \\(N\\), \\(p'^N_\\zeta\\precsim p'^N_{\\zeta_0}\\) in \\(N'\\): some \\(v\\in N'\\) has \\(v^*v=p'^N_\\zeta\\) and \\(vv^*\\le p'^N_{\\zeta_0}\\). The range of \\(p'^N_{\\zeta_0}\\) is \\([N\\zeta_0]=[M\\xi_0]\\otimes\\delta_1\\). Hence \\(v\\zeta=\\xi\\otimes\\delta_1\\) with \\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\), and for \\(x\\in M\\)\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\omega_\\xi(x)\\\\\n=\\langle(x\\otimes1)v\\zeta,v\\zeta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle(x\\otimes1)v^*v\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\omega_\\zeta(x\\otimes1)\\\\\n=\\varphi(x).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\xi_0\\) is separating for \\(M\\), it is cyclic for \\(M'\\) (Fact 2.3), so \\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\) and the condition holds for every \\(\\varphi\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Example 18.3** (A normal state that is not a vector state). Let \\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\) on \\(\\mathbb C^2\\) and \\(\\varphi=\\frac12\\mathrm{tr}\\). Its support is \\(1\\). Each vector functional \\(\\omega_\\xi\\) has support \\(p_\\xi\\), the rank-one projection onto \\(\\mathbb C\\xi\\) (here \\(M'=\\mathbb C1\\)). So \\(\\varphi\\) is not a vector functional, in line with Proposition 18.2: \\(s(\\varphi)=1\\not\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\) for every \\(\\xi_0\\). Accordingly \\(M\\) has no separating vector. On \\(\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\), with \\(M\\) acting as \\(M\\otimes1\\), the vector \\((\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\otimes\\delta_2)/\\sqrt2\\) is separating and gives \\(\\varphi\\).\n\n**Lemma 18.4** (Interpolating two vectors). Let \\(\\xi_1,\\xi_2\\in H\\) with \\(p'_{\\xi_1}\\sim p'_{\\xi_2}\\) in \\(M'\\); by Theorem 17.5 and Proposition 5.1 this is the same as \\(p_{\\xi_1}\\sim p_{\\xi_2}\\) in \\(M\\). Then some \\(\\xi_0\\in H\\) has \\(p'_{\\xi_0}=p'_{\\xi_1}\\) and \\(p_{\\xi_0}=p_{\\xi_2}\\).\n\n**Proof.** Let \\(u\\in M'\\) implement \\(p'_{\\xi_2}\\sim p'_{\\xi_1}\\), and put \\(\\xi_0=u\\xi_2\\). Since \\(u\\) commutes with \\(M\\) and maps \\([M\\xi_2]\\) isometrically onto \\(p'_{\\xi_1}H\\), we get \\([M\\xi_0]=u[M\\xi_2]=p'_{\\xi_1}H\\). Since \\(u^*u\\xi_2=\\xi_2\\), Step 1 of the proof of Theorem 17.5 gives \\(p_{\\xi_0}=p_{\\xi_2}\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 18.5** (A cyclic and separating vector). If some vector is cyclic for \\(M\\) and some vector is separating for \\(M\\), then a single vector is both cyclic and separating.\n\n**Proof.** Let \\(\\xi_1\\) be cyclic and \\(\\xi_2\\) separating for \\(M\\). Then \\(p'_{\\xi_1}=1\\), and \\(p_{\\xi_2}=1\\) by Fact 2.3. As \\(p_{\\xi_1}\\le1=p_{\\xi_2}\\), Theorem 17.5 gives \\(1=p'_{\\xi_1}\\precsim p'_{\\xi_2}\\), and Proposition 5.1 gives \\(p'_{\\xi_2}\\sim1=p'_{\\xi_1}\\). Lemma 18.4 yields \\(\\xi_0\\) with \\(p'_{\\xi_0}=1\\) and \\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\). So \\(\\xi_0\\) is cyclic for \\(M\\) and for \\(M'\\), hence also separating for \\(M\\) (Fact 2.3). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 18.6** (Isomorphisms are spatial). Let \\(\\{M_1,H_1\\}\\) and \\(\\{M_2,H_2\\}\\) be von Neumann algebras with cyclic and separating vectors \\(\\xi_1\\) and \\(\\xi_2\\). Every isomorphism \\(\\pi:M_1\\to M_2\\) is spatial.\n\n**Proof.** By Fact 2.6, \\(\\pi\\) is \\(\\sigma\\)-weakly continuous, so \\(\\psi=\\omega_{\\xi_2}\\circ\\pi\\) is a positive normal functional on \\(M_1\\). It is faithful: \\(\\psi(x^*x)=\\|\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\|^2=0\\) forces \\(\\pi(x)=0\\) and \\(x=0\\). By Proposition 18.2, applied to \\(M_1\\) and its separating vector \\(\\xi_1\\), \\(\\psi=\\omega_\\zeta\\) for some \\(\\zeta\\in H_1\\). Faithfulness makes \\(\\zeta\\) separating for \\(M_1\\), so \\(p_\\zeta=1=p_{\\xi_1}\\) (Fact 2.3), and Theorem 17.5 with Proposition 5.1 gives \\(p'_\\zeta\\sim p'_{\\xi_1}=1\\). Lemma 18.4 gives \\(\\zeta_0=u\\zeta\\), with \\(u\\in M_1'\\) isometric on \\([M_1\\zeta]\\), such that \\(p'_{\\zeta_0}=1\\). Then \\(\\omega_{\\zeta_0}=\\omega_\\zeta=\\psi\\) on \\(M_1\\), because \\(\\langle xu\\zeta,u\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle u^*u\\,x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle\\).\n\nDefine \\(U\\) on \\(M_2\\xi_2=\\pi(M_1)\\xi_2\\) by \\(U\\pi(x)\\xi_2=x\\zeta_0\\). It is well defined and isometric, since \\(\\|x\\zeta_0\\|^2=\\psi(x^*x)=\\|\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\|^2\\). Its domain is dense in \\(H_2\\) and its range is dense in \\(H_1\\), so \\(U\\) extends to a unitary \\(H_2\\to H_1\\). For \\(x,y\\in M_1\\), \\(U\\pi(y)\\pi(x)\\xi_2=yx\\zeta_0=yU\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\). So \\(U\\pi(y)=yU\\), that is, \\(\\pi(y)=U^*yU\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n## Where this leads\n\nThe following topics extend this lesson. Their further assertions and constructions are not proved here and are not inputs to any proof or solution above.\n\n- *Traces.* Traces and dimension functions are built on the comparison theory of this lesson. The lesson Traces on von Neumann algebras constructs the centre-valued trace of a finite algebra, of which the map \\(\\Phi\\) of Proposition 9.1(2) is the simplest case. It also proves that the family \\(\\{z_\\alpha\\}\\) of Proposition 15.3(1) is unique.\n- *Types II and III.* This lesson gives examples of type I only. Factors of types II and III exist, but their construction needs other tools, such as group von Neumann algebras, infinite tensor products or crossed products.\n- *Multiplicity.* The spatial form of type I algebras (Theorem 11.4) and the normal form of properly infinite algebras (Proposition 15.3) are the starting points of the multiplicity theory of normal representations.\n\n## References\n\n- [Kostecki] R. P. Kostecki, *W\\(^*\\)-algebras and noncommutative integration*, [arXiv:1307.4818](https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4818v5), 2013.\n- [Blackadar] B. Blackadar, *Operator Algebras: Theory of C*-Algebras and von Neumann Algebras*, [revised author edition, 8 February 2017](https://www.bruceblackadar.com/Mathematics/Cycr.pdf).\n\n*Freely accessible reading:* [B. Blackadar, *Operator Algebras*, III.1.1–III.1.4](https://bruceblackadar.com/Mathematics/Cycr.pdf) gives a route through projection comparison and type decomposition; all cardinal and boundary cases stated here retain their complete proofs. The lesson includes its own complete proofs at the stated hypotheses; references to human sources do not imply permission to adapt their expression.","html":"<h2>18. Vector states and spatial isomorphisms<\/h2>\n<p>The transfer theorem answers three natural questions: when one cyclic representation of a C<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(^*\\)\">\\(^*\\)<\/span>-algebra is contained in another; which normal functionals are vector functionals; and, for algebras with cyclic and separating vectors, whether an isomorphism is implemented by a unitary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 18.1<\/strong> (Subrepresentations and cyclic projections). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> be a C<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(^*\\)\">\\(^*\\)<\/span>-algebra, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{\\pi,H\\}\\)\">\\(\\{\\pi,H\\}\\)<\/span> a nondegenerate representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\circ\\pi\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\circ\\pi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi=\\omega_\\eta\\circ\\pi\\)\">\\(\\psi=\\omega_\\eta\\circ\\pi\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=\\pi(A)&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>. The following are equivalent.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The cyclic representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> <em>embeds<\/em> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span>: some isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_\\varphi\\to H_\\psi\\)\">\\(H_\\varphi\\to H_\\psi\\)<\/span> intertwines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span>. (Its range is then an invariant subspace, so this says that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> is carried by a unitary onto a subrepresentation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span>.)<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi\\precsim p&#x27;_\\eta\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi\\precsim p&#x27;_\\eta\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;=\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(N&#x27;=\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\precsim p_\\eta\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\precsim p_\\eta\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>In the universal representation (Fact 2.7), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi=s(\\varphi)\\)\">\\(p_\\xi=s(\\varphi)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\eta=s(\\psi)\\)\">\\(p_\\eta=s(\\psi)\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> embeds in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span> exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim s(\\psi)\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim s(\\psi)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)\\)<\/span> is strongly dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> by the double commutant theorem (Fact 2.7). So every vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\xi\\)\">\\(a\\xi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in N\\)\">\\(a\\in N\\)<\/span> is a limit of vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(b)\\xi\\)\">\\(\\pi(b)\\xi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in A\\)\">\\(b\\in A\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([N\\xi]=[\\pi(A)\\xi]\\)\">\\([N\\xi]=[\\pi(A)\\xi]\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi\\)<\/span> is the projection onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\pi(A)\\xi]\\)\">\\([\\pi(A)\\xi]\\)<\/span>. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi(a)\\xi_\\varphi\\mapsto\\pi(a)\\xi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi(a)\\xi_\\varphi\\mapsto\\pi(a)\\xi\\)<\/span> is isometric, since both sides have squared norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(a^*a)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(a^*a)\\)<\/span>. It carries a dense subspace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_\\varphi\\)\">\\(H_\\varphi\\)<\/span> onto a dense subspace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)<\/span>, so it extends to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_\\xi:H_\\varphi\\to p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)\">\\(U_\\xi:H_\\varphi\\to p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)<\/span> that intertwines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> with the restriction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)<\/span>. The same holds for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(2)⇒(1). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(v\\in\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span> have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*v=p&#x27;_\\xi\\)\">\\(v^*v=p&#x27;_\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*\\le p&#x27;_\\eta\\)\">\\(vv^*\\le p&#x27;_\\eta\\)<\/span>. The range <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L=vv^*H\\)\">\\(L=vv^*H\\)<\/span> is invariant under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> restricts to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\to L\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\to L\\)<\/span> that intertwines the restrictions of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span>. Composing with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_\\xi\\)\">\\(U_\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_\\eta^{-1}\\)\">\\(U_\\eta^{-1}\\)<\/span> shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> is equivalent to the restriction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span> to the invariant subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_\\eta^*L\\)\">\\(U_\\eta^*L\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(1)⇒(2). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T:H_\\varphi\\to L_0\\)\">\\(T:H_\\varphi\\to L_0\\)<\/span> be a unitary onto a closed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span>-invariant subspace, intertwining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\varphi\\)<\/span> with the restriction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span>. Define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=U_\\eta TU_\\xi^*\\)\">\\(v=U_\\eta TU_\\xi^*\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=0\\)\">\\(v=0\\)<\/span> on its orthogonal complement. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\xi H\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> intertwines. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((p&#x27;_\\xi H)^\\perp\\)\">\\((p&#x27;_\\xi H)^\\perp\\)<\/span>, both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\pi(a)\\)\">\\(v\\pi(a)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(a)v\\)\">\\(\\pi(a)v\\)<\/span> vanish, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(a)\\)\">\\(\\pi(a)\\)<\/span> leaves that space invariant. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(v\\in\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*v=p&#x27;_\\xi\\)\">\\(v^*v=p&#x27;_\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*\\le p&#x27;_\\eta\\)\">\\(vv^*\\le p&#x27;_\\eta\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(2)⇔(3) is Theorem 17.5 in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, whose commutant is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\pi(A)&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>In the state-indexed universal representation, normalize each nonzero functional and rescale its cyclic vector as in Fact 2.7; use the zero vector for a zero functional. This gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H_u\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H_u\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\circ\\pi_u\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\circ\\pi_u\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi=\\omega_\\eta\\circ\\pi_u\\)\">\\(\\psi=\\omega_\\eta\\circ\\pi_u\\)<\/span>. The restriction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde A\\)\">\\(\\tilde A\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weakly continuous and extends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>, so it is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tilde\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\tilde\\varphi\\)<\/span>, and its support is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\)<\/span> (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-ty-07\">Lemma 4.6<\/a>). The same holds for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 18.2<\/strong> (Which normal functionals are vector functionals). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\in H\\)<\/span> and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> be a positive normal functional on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi|_M\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi|_M\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)<\/span> if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)<\/span>. In particular, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> has a separating vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\)<\/span>, and every positive normal functional on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is a vector functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\omega_\\xi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)=p_\\xi\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)=p_\\xi\\)<\/span> (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-ty-07\">Lemma 4.6<\/a>), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)<\/span> by Step 2 of the proof of Theorem 17.5.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)<\/span>. By Fact 2.5, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\sum_n\\omega_{\\xi_n}\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\sum_n\\omega_{\\xi_n}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|^2&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|^2&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=H\\otimes\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)\">\\(K=H\\otimes\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)<\/span>, with orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\delta_n)\\)\">\\((\\delta_n)\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)\">\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M\\otimes1\\)\">\\(N=M\\otimes1\\)<\/span>. By <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-ty-11\">Lemma 8.2<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is a von Neumann algebra, its commutant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;\\)\">\\(N&#x27;\\)<\/span> consists of the operators whose matrix entries lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;\\)\">\\(M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto x\\otimes1\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto x\\otimes1\\)<\/span> is an isomorphism of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta=\\sum_n\\xi_n\\otimes\\delta_n\\)\">\\(\\zeta=\\sum_n\\xi_n\\otimes\\delta_n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta_0=\\xi_0\\otimes\\delta_1\\)\">\\(\\zeta_0=\\xi_0\\otimes\\delta_1\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\zeta(x\\otimes1)=\\varphi(x)\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\zeta(x\\otimes1)=\\varphi(x)\\)<\/span>. A projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\otimes1\\)\">\\(p\\otimes1\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span> iff <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(1-p)=0\\)\">\\(\\varphi(1-p)=0\\)<\/span>, so the cyclic projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p^N_\\zeta=s(\\varphi)\\otimes1\\)\">\\(p^N_\\zeta=s(\\varphi)\\otimes1\\)<\/span>. Next, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([N&#x27;\\zeta_0]=[M&#x27;\\xi_0]\\otimes\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)\">\\([N&#x27;\\zeta_0]=[M&#x27;\\xi_0]\\otimes\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)\\)<\/span>: the inclusion &quot;⊇&quot; holds because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;\\)\">\\(N&#x27;\\)<\/span> contains the operators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x&#x27;\\otimes1\\)\">\\(x&#x27;\\otimes1\\)<\/span> and the matrix units <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\otimes E_{n1}\\)\">\\(1\\otimes E_{n1}\\)<\/span>, and &quot;⊆&quot; holds because every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(T\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span> maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta_0\\)\">\\(\\zeta_0\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_nT_{n1}\\xi_0\\otimes\\delta_n\\)\">\\(\\sum_nT_{n1}\\xi_0\\otimes\\delta_n\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_{n1}\\in M&#x27;\\)\">\\(T_{n1}\\in M&#x27;\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p^N_{\\zeta_0}=p_{\\xi_0}\\otimes1\\)\">\\(p^N_{\\zeta_0}=p_{\\xi_0}\\otimes1\\)<\/span>. The isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto x\\otimes1\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto x\\otimes1\\)<\/span> turns <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p^N_\\zeta\\precsim p^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)\">\\(p^N_\\zeta\\precsim p^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)<\/span>. By Theorem 17.5 in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;^N_\\zeta\\precsim p&#x27;^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)\">\\(p&#x27;^N_\\zeta\\precsim p&#x27;^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N&#x27;\\)\">\\(N&#x27;\\)<\/span>: some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in N&#x27;\\)\">\\(v\\in N&#x27;\\)<\/span> has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*v=p&#x27;^N_\\zeta\\)\">\\(v^*v=p&#x27;^N_\\zeta\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*\\le p&#x27;^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)\">\\(vv^*\\le p&#x27;^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)<\/span>. The range of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)\">\\(p&#x27;^N_{\\zeta_0}\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([N\\zeta_0]=[M\\xi_0]\\otimes\\delta_1\\)\">\\([N\\zeta_0]=[M\\xi_0]\\otimes\\delta_1\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\zeta=\\xi\\otimes\\delta_1\\)\">\\(v\\zeta=\\xi\\otimes\\delta_1\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in[M\\xi_0]\\)<\/span>, and for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in M\\)\">\\(x\\in M\\)<\/span>\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\omega_\\xi(x)\\\\\n=\\langle(x\\otimes1)v\\zeta,v\\zeta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle(x\\otimes1)v^*v\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\omega_\\zeta(x\\otimes1)\\\\\n=\\varphi(x).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\omega_\\xi(x)\\\\\n=\\langle(x\\otimes1)v\\zeta,v\\zeta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle(x\\otimes1)v^*v\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle\\\\\n=\\omega_\\zeta(x\\otimes1)\\\\\n=\\varphi(x).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\)<\/span> is separating for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, it is cyclic for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;\\)\">\\(M&#x27;\\)<\/span> (Fact 2.3), so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\)<\/span> and the condition holds for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Example 18.3<\/strong> (A normal state that is not a vector state). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\frac12\\mathrm{tr}\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\frac12\\mathrm{tr}\\)<\/span>. Its support is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. Each vector functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)<\/span> has support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\)<\/span>, the rank-one projection onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\xi\\)<\/span> (here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(M&#x27;=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>). So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> is not a vector functional, in line with Proposition 18.2: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)=1\\not\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)=1\\not\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\)<\/span>. Accordingly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> has no separating vector. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> acting as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\otimes1\\)\">\\(M\\otimes1\\)<\/span>, the vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\otimes\\delta_2)/\\sqrt2\\)\">\\((\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\otimes\\delta_2)/\\sqrt2\\)<\/span> is separating and gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma 18.4<\/strong> (Interpolating two vectors). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_1,\\xi_2\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi_1,\\xi_2\\in H\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\sim p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\sim p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;\\)\">\\(M&#x27;\\)<\/span>; by Theorem 17.5 and Proposition 5.1 this is the same as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_1}\\sim p_{\\xi_2}\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_1}\\sim p_{\\xi_2}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Then some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\in H\\)<\/span> has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_0}=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_0}=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_0}=p_{\\xi_2}\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_0}=p_{\\xi_2}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in M&#x27;\\)\">\\(u\\in M&#x27;\\)<\/span> implement <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\sim p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\sim p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0=u\\xi_2\\)\">\\(\\xi_0=u\\xi_2\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([M\\xi_2]\\)\">\\([M\\xi_2]\\)<\/span> isometrically onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}H\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}H\\)<\/span>, we get <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([M\\xi_0]=u[M\\xi_2]=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}H\\)\">\\([M\\xi_0]=u[M\\xi_2]=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}H\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*u\\xi_2=\\xi_2\\)\">\\(u^*u\\xi_2=\\xi_2\\)<\/span>, Step 1 of the proof of Theorem 17.5 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_0}=p_{\\xi_2}\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_0}=p_{\\xi_2}\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 18.5<\/strong> (A cyclic and separating vector). If some vector is cyclic for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and some vector is separating for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, then a single vector is both cyclic and separating.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_1\\)\">\\(\\xi_1\\)<\/span> be cyclic and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_2\\)\">\\(\\xi_2\\)<\/span> separating for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}=1\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}=1\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_2}=1\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_2}=1\\)<\/span> by Fact 2.3. As <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_1}\\le1=p_{\\xi_2}\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_1}\\le1=p_{\\xi_2}\\)<\/span>, Theorem 17.5 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\precsim p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\)\">\\(1=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\precsim p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\)<\/span>, and Proposition 5.1 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\sim1=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_2}\\sim1=p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}\\)<\/span>. Lemma 18.4 yields <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_0}=1\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\xi_0}=1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\)\">\\(p_{\\xi_0}=1\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\)<\/span> is cyclic for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;\\)\">\\(M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, hence also separating for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> (Fact 2.3). <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 18.6<\/strong> (Isomorphisms are spatial). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{M_1,H_1\\}\\)\">\\(\\{M_1,H_1\\}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{M_2,H_2\\}\\)\">\\(\\{M_2,H_2\\}\\)<\/span> be von Neumann algebras with cyclic and separating vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_1\\)\">\\(\\xi_1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_2\\)\">\\(\\xi_2\\)<\/span>. Every isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi:M_1\\to M_2\\)\">\\(\\pi:M_1\\to M_2\\)<\/span> is spatial.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> By Fact 2.6, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weakly continuous, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi=\\omega_{\\xi_2}\\circ\\pi\\)\">\\(\\psi=\\omega_{\\xi_2}\\circ\\pi\\)<\/span> is a positive normal functional on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_1\\)\">\\(M_1\\)<\/span>. It is faithful: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(x^*x)=\\|\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\|^2=0\\)\">\\(\\psi(x^*x)=\\|\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\|^2=0\\)<\/span> forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(x)=0\\)\">\\(\\pi(x)=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=0\\)\">\\(x=0\\)<\/span>. By Proposition 18.2, applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_1\\)\">\\(M_1\\)<\/span> and its separating vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_1\\)\">\\(\\xi_1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi=\\omega_\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\psi=\\omega_\\zeta\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\in H_1\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\in H_1\\)<\/span>. Faithfulness makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span> separating for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_1\\)\">\\(M_1\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\zeta=1=p_{\\xi_1}\\)\">\\(p_\\zeta=1=p_{\\xi_1}\\)<\/span> (Fact 2.3), and Theorem 17.5 with Proposition 5.1 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_\\zeta\\sim p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}=1\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_\\zeta\\sim p&#x27;_{\\xi_1}=1\\)<\/span>. Lemma 18.4 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta_0=u\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta_0=u\\zeta\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in M_1&#x27;\\)\">\\(u\\in M_1&#x27;\\)<\/span> isometric on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([M_1\\zeta]\\)\">\\([M_1\\zeta]\\)<\/span>, such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p&#x27;_{\\zeta_0}=1\\)\">\\(p&#x27;_{\\zeta_0}=1\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_{\\zeta_0}=\\omega_\\zeta=\\psi\\)\">\\(\\omega_{\\zeta_0}=\\omega_\\zeta=\\psi\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_1\\)\">\\(M_1\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle xu\\zeta,u\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle u^*u\\,x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle xu\\zeta,u\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle u^*u\\,x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2\\xi_2=\\pi(M_1)\\xi_2\\)\">\\(M_2\\xi_2=\\pi(M_1)\\xi_2\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\pi(x)\\xi_2=x\\zeta_0\\)\">\\(U\\pi(x)\\xi_2=x\\zeta_0\\)<\/span>. It is well defined and isometric, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\zeta_0\\|^2=\\psi(x^*x)=\\|\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|x\\zeta_0\\|^2=\\psi(x^*x)=\\|\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\|^2\\)<\/span>. Its domain is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_2\\)\">\\(H_2\\)<\/span> and its range is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_1\\)\">\\(H_1\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> extends to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H_2\\to H_1\\)\">\\(H_2\\to H_1\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,y\\in M_1\\)\">\\(x,y\\in M_1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\pi(y)\\pi(x)\\xi_2=yx\\zeta_0=yU\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\)\">\\(U\\pi(y)\\pi(x)\\xi_2=yx\\zeta_0=yU\\pi(x)\\xi_2\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\pi(y)=yU\\)\">\\(U\\pi(y)=yU\\)<\/span>, that is, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(y)=U^*yU\\)\">\\(\\pi(y)=U^*yU\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<h2>Where this leads<\/h2>\n<p>The following topics extend this lesson. Their further assertions and constructions are not proved here and are not inputs to any proof or solution above.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Traces.<\/em> Traces and dimension functions are built on the comparison theory of this lesson. The lesson Traces on von Neumann algebras constructs the centre-valued trace of a finite algebra, of which the map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Phi\\)<\/span> of Proposition 9.1(2) is the simplest case. It also proves that the family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{z_\\alpha\\}\\)\">\\(\\{z_\\alpha\\}\\)<\/span> of Proposition 15.3(1) is unique.<\/li>\n<li><em>Types II and III.<\/em> This lesson gives examples of type I only. Factors of types II and III exist, but their construction needs other tools, such as group von Neumann algebras, infinite tensor products or crossed products.<\/li>\n<li><em>Multiplicity.<\/em> The spatial form of type I algebras (Theorem 11.4) and the normal form of properly infinite algebras (Proposition 15.3) are the starting points of the multiplicity theory of normal representations.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>References<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[Kostecki] R. P. Kostecki, <em>W<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(^*\\)\">\\(^*\\)<\/span>-algebras and noncommutative integration<\/em>, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4818v5\">arXiv:1307.4818<\/a>, 2013.<\/li>\n<li>[Blackadar] B. Blackadar, <em>Operator Algebras: Theory of C<\/em>-Algebras and von Neumann Algebras*, <a href=\"https://www.bruceblackadar.com/Mathematics/Cycr.pdf\">revised author edition, 8 February 2017<\/a>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Freely accessible reading:<\/em> <a href=\"https://bruceblackadar.com/Mathematics/Cycr.pdf\">B. Blackadar, <em>Operator Algebras<\/em>, III.1.1–III.1.4<\/a> gives a route through projection comparison and type decomposition; all cardinal and boundary cases stated here retain their complete proofs. The lesson includes its own complete proofs at the stated hypotheses; references to human sources do not imply permission to adapt their expression.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.md","start_offset":128422,"end_offset":139319,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"f8a6737ec408d5c3564e63fab363d9875134f5c97183fed0f53a7682bf5af7ec":{"markdown":"## Statement\n\n**Theorem (automatic continuity and Kadison–Sakai innerness).** A derivation of a \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(A\\) is a complex-linear map \\(\\delta:A\\to A\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\delta(xy)=\\delta(x)y+x\\delta(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in A).\n\\]\nNo continuity is included in this definition.\n\nEvery derivation of every \\(C^*\\)-algebra is bounded. If \\(M\\) is a von Neumann algebra and \\(\\delta:M\\to M\\) is a derivation, then there exists \\(a\\in M\\) such that\n\\[\n\\delta(x)=ax-xa\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\qquad \\|a\\|\\le 2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\nTwo implementers in \\(M\\) differ by an element of \\(Z(M)\\), and adding an element of \\(Z(M)\\) to an implementer does not change the derivation. Consequently every implementer \\(b\\in M\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,Z(M))\\le 2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\nIf, in addition,\n\\[\n\\delta(x^*)=\\delta(x)^*\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]\nthen one can choose\n\\[\na=ih,\\qquad h=h^*\\in M,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\nIn this case every implementer in \\(M\\) has distance at most \\(\\|\\delta\\|\\) from \\(Z(M)\\).\n\nThere is no separability, countable decomposability, factor, type, or trace hypothesis. These are bounds furnished by the proof; no optimality assertion is made.\n\n## Proof\n\nWrite \\(A_h=\\{x\\in A:x=x^*\\}\\), \\(\\mathcal U(A)\\) for the unitary group of a unital algebra, and \\([a,x]=ax-xa\\). A derivation preserving the involution is called a *-derivation. For a concrete von Neumann algebra, weak-* means its \\(\\sigma\\)-weak topology. Central sums are indexed by arbitrary sets and are limits of finite partial sums. The lesson abbreviations used in citations are expanded, with result numbers, under **Results used**.\n\nThe zero algebra is immediate, with implementer zero. All algebras represented on Hilbert spaces below act nondegenerately.\n\n### 1. Automatic continuity\n\n**1.1. Algebraic reduction to *-derivations.** For an arbitrary derivation define\n\\[\n\\delta^\\#(x)=\\delta(x^*)^*,\\qquad\n\\delta_1=\\frac{\\delta+\\delta^\\#}{2},\\qquad\n\\delta_2=\\frac{\\delta-\\delta^\\#}{2i}.\n\\]\nThe map \\(\\delta^\\#\\) is complex-linear. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\delta^\\#(xy)\n=\\bigl(\\delta(y^*)x^*+y^*\\delta(x^*)\\bigr)^*\n=x\\delta^\\#(y)+\\delta^\\#(x)y.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\) are derivations, and\n\\[\n\\delta_1(x^*)=\\frac{\\delta(x^*)+\\delta(x)^*}{2}=\\delta_1(x)^*,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_2(x^*)=\\frac{\\delta(x^*)-\\delta(x)^*}{2i}=\\delta_2(x)^*.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\delta=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2.\n\\]\nThese assertions are algebraic and do not assume continuity. Once boundedness is established, they also give\n\\[\n\\|\\delta^\\#\\|=\\|\\delta\\|,\n\\qquad \\|\\delta_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\\quad(j=1,2).\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nIt suffices to treat a unital algebra. Indeed, represent a nonunital \\(A\\) faithfully, using [GNS, Theorem 7.2](../../representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.html#faithful-representations-of-c--algebras). The algebra \\(A+\\mathbb C1\\subseteq B(H)\\) is closed: a finite-dimensional extension of a closed subspace of a Banach space is closed. The [closedness bridge below](#OA-FND-INNER-CLOSEDNESS) proves this implication explicitly. Since \\(1\\notin A\\), the expression \\(x+\\lambda1\\) is unique, and\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\delta(x+\\lambda1)=\\delta(x)\n\\]\ndefines a derivation: both sides of its product rule at \\(x+\\lambda1,y+\\mu1\\) equal\n\\[\n\\delta(x)y+x\\delta(y)+\\lambda\\delta(y)+\\mu\\delta(x).\n\\]\nIt preserves the involution when \\(\\delta\\) does, since\n\\(\\widetilde\\delta((x+\\lambda1)^*)=\\delta(x^*)=\\delta(x)^*\\).\nBoundedness of this extension implies boundedness on \\(A\\). In a unital algebra every derivation satisfies \\(\\delta(1)=0\\), by applying the product rule to \\(1\\cdot1\\).\n\n**1.2. A state calculation.** Let \\(D\\) be any derivation of a unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra, let \\(b=b^*\\), and let \\(\\varphi\\) be a state whose restriction to \\(C^*(1,b)\\) is a character. Then\n\\[\n\\varphi(D(b))=0.\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\nTo prove this, put \\(c=b-\\varphi(b)1\\). Continuous functional calculus [CF, Theorem 5.1](../../c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-07) gives positive elements\n\\[\ns_+=(c_+)^{1/2},\\qquad s_-=(c_-)^{1/2},\n\\qquad c=s_+^2-s_-^2\n\\]\nin \\(C^*(1,b)\\). The character takes \\(c\\) to zero and therefore takes \\(s_+^2,s_-^2\\) to zero. Hence \\(\\varphi(s_\\pm^2)=0\\). The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality for a positive functional [GNS, Proposition 3.2](../../representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.html#OA-FND-GN-03), in the form\n\\[\n|\\varphi(rs)|^2\\le\\varphi(rr^*)\\varphi(s^*s),\n\\]\nshows that\n\\[\n\\varphi(D(s_\\pm)s_\\pm)=\\varphi(s_\\pm D(s_\\pm))=0.\n\\]\nApplying \\(D\\) to \\(c=s_+^2-s_-^2\\), and using \\(D(1)=0\\), proves (1.2).\n\n**1.3. Closed graph.** Let \\(D\\) be a *-derivation. Suppose that\n\\[\nx_n\\in A_h,\\qquad x_n\\longrightarrow0,\\qquad D(x_n)\\longrightarrow b\\in A_h\n\\]\nin norm. We prove \\(b=0\\). If \\(b\\ne0\\), its spectrum contains a nonzero real number \\(\\lambda\\). Replacing \\(x_n,b\\) by \\(x_n/\\lambda,b/\\lambda\\), we can assume \\(1\\in\\sigma(b)\\).\n\nChoose a continuous real function \\(f\\) on \\(\\sigma(b)\\) such that\n\\[\n0\\le f\\le1,\\qquad f(1)=1,\\qquad f(t)=0\\text{ for }t\\le\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nFor \\(v=f(b)\\), functional calculus gives\n\\[\n0\\le v\\le1,\\qquad \\|v\\|=1,\\qquad vbv\\ge\\tfrac12v^2.\n\\]\nAfter discarding finitely many terms, \\(r_n=\\|x_n\\|>0\\). Set\n\\[\ny_n=x_n+3r_n1,\\qquad b_n=vy_nv,\\qquad t_n=\\frac{b_n}{4r_n}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n2r_n1\\le y_n\\le4r_n1,\n\\qquad \\tfrac12v^2\\le t_n\\le v^2,\n\\qquad \\|t_n\\|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\tag{1.3}\n\\]\nThe product rule, without any continuity assumption on \\(D\\), gives\n\\[\nD(b_n)=D(v)y_nv+vD(x_n)v+vy_nD(v)\\longrightarrow vbv.\n\\tag{1.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(D(v)\\) is one fixed element, and \\(\\|y_n\\|\\to0\\).\n\nEvaluation at the spectral point \\(\\|b_n\\|\\) is a character of \\(C^*(1,b_n)\\). Extend it to a state \\(\\varphi_n\\) of \\(A\\), using [GNS, Proposition 7.1a](../../representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.html#faithful-representations-of-c--algebras). Equation (1.2) gives \\(\\varphi_n(D(b_n))=0\\), while\n\\[\n\\varphi_n(t_n)=\\|t_n\\|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nConsequently, for every sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\frac14\n\\le\\frac12\\varphi_n(t_n)\n\\le\\varphi_n(vbv)\n=\\varphi_n(vbv-D(b_n))\n\\le\\|vbv-D(b_n)\\|\n<\\frac18,\n\\]\na contradiction.\n\nThe graph of the real-linear map \\(D:A_h\\to A_h\\) is therefore closed: a convergent graph sequence is reduced to the preceding case by subtracting its limiting first coordinate. The real Closed Graph Theorem makes this restriction bounded. Every \\(x\\in A\\) is \\(x=h+ik\\), where \\(h,k\\in A_h\\) and \\(\\|h\\|,\\|k\\|\\le\\|x\\|\\); hence \\(D\\) is bounded on \\(A\\). Applying this to \\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\) proves automatic continuity for \\(\\delta\\).\n\nThe spectral-cut argument above is the automatic-continuity argument of [S60, unnumbered theorem, pp. 31–32], with the algebraic *-splitting and the nonunital reduction included explicitly.\n\n### 2. The centre and uniformly bounded central sums\n\nFor a central projection \\(z\\) and any derivation \\(D\\),\n\\[\nD(z)=D(z)z+zD(z)=2zD(z).\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(z\\) first gives \\(zD(z)=0\\), and then \\(D(z)=0\\). For a bounded derivation on a von Neumann algebra, spectral approximation of central self-adjoint elements by finite linear combinations of central projections gives\n\\[\nD(c)=0\\quad(c\\in Z(M)),\\qquad D(zx)=zD(x).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nThe approximation follows from [ST, Theorem 3.1 and Proposition 5.1](../../the-spectral-theorem-for-bounded-self-adjoint-operators.html#OA-FND-ST-03). In particular, \\(D\\) restricts to each central summand \\(Mz\\), with norm at most \\(\\|D\\|\\).\n\nWe record the precise summation statement. Suppose that \\((z_i)_{i\\in I}\\) are pairwise orthogonal central projections with strong sum \\(1\\), and that\n\\[\na_i\\in Mz_i,\\qquad \\|a_i\\|\\le C,\\qquad\nD(x)=[a_i,x]\\quad(x\\in Mz_i).\n\\]\nFor finite \\(F\\subset I\\), put \\(a_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i\\). Since the ranges of the summands are in orthogonal subspaces,\n\\[\n\\|a_F\\xi\\|^2\n=\\sum_{i\\in F}\\|a_i z_i\\xi\\|^2\n\\le C^2\\sum_{i\\in F}\\|z_i\\xi\\|^2\n\\le C^2\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe same estimate for differences of partial sums proves strong convergence to an operator \\(a\\). Strong closedness gives \\(a\\in M\\), and \\(\\|a\\|\\le C\\), \\(az_i=a_i\\). If all \\(a_i\\) are self-adjoint, or all are skew-adjoint, the same property holds for \\(a\\), since the adjoint relation passes to matrix coefficients.\n\nFor every \\(x\\in M\\), (2.1) gives\n\\[\n\\bigl(D(x)-[a,x]\\bigr)z_i\n=D(xz_i)-[a_i,xz_i]=0.\n\\]\nTaking the strong sum of the \\(z_i\\) yields \\(D(x)=[a,x]\\). This argument never applies \\(D\\) to a strong limit.\n\nA *-isomorphism transports derivations and their implementers, preserves their norms, and preserves self-adjointness. Isometry of *-isomorphisms is [CF, Corollary 4.6](../../c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-12). Between von Neumann algebras such an isomorphism is also normal, by [UE, Corollary 11.4](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18).\n\n### 3. Normal extension without a continuity assumption on the representation\n\nLet \\(A\\subseteq B(H)\\) be a nondegenerate \\(C^*\\)-algebra and \\(D:A\\to A\\) a bounded derivation. We show that it has a unique normal extension\n\\[\n\\overline D:A''\\longrightarrow A'',\\qquad\n\\|\\overline D\\|=\\|D\\|.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\) is a *-derivation, so is \\(\\overline D\\).\n\nIdentify \\(A^{**}\\) with its enveloping von Neumann algebra by [UE, Theorem 3.3](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-03), and write \\(j:A\\to A^{**}\\) for the canonical embedding. The second adjoint \\(D^{**}\\) is weak-* continuous and has norm \\(\\|D\\|\\) [WT, Proposition 5.3](../../weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-05). It is a derivation. Here is the required verification of the product rule.\n\nFor \\(a\\in A\\) and \\(Y\\in A^{**}\\), choose a net \\(b_\\beta\\in A\\) with \\(j(b_\\beta)\\to Y\\) weak-*, using [WT, Theorem 5.2](../../weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-05). Passing to the limit in the product rule for \\(ab_\\beta\\), by separate weak-* continuity of multiplication, gives\n\\[\nD^{**}(j(a)Y)=j(D(a))Y+j(a)D^{**}(Y).\n\\]\nNow choose \\(j(a_\\alpha)\\to X\\) weak-* and pass to the limit in this identity, keeping \\(Y\\) fixed. It follows that\n\\[\nD^{**}(XY)=D^{**}(X)Y+XD^{**}(Y).\n\\]\nOnly one variable is allowed to tend to its limit at each passage. The involution is weak-* continuous, so the same density argument preserves the *-condition.\n\nBy [UE, Lemma 2.1(2)–(4)](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-02), the given representation extends to a normal surjection\n\\[\nq:A^{**}\\longrightarrow A''\n\\]\nwhose kernel is \\(A^{**}(1-z)\\) for a central projection \\(z\\). Its restriction\n\\[\n\\theta:A^{**}z\\longrightarrow A''\n\\]\nis an isometric *-isomorphism, normal in both directions. Step 2's algebraic calculation gives \\(D^{**}(z)=0\\). Thus \\(D^{**}\\) preserves \\(A^{**}z\\), and\n\\[\n\\overline D=\\theta\\circ\\bigl(D^{**}|_{A^{**}z}\\bigr)\\circ\\theta^{-1}\n\\]\nis a normal derivation of norm at most \\(\\|D\\|\\). For \\(a\\in A\\),\n\\[\n\\theta^{-1}(a)=j(a)z,\n\\qquad D^{**}(j(a)z)=j(D(a))z,\n\\]\nso \\(\\overline D(a)=D(a)\\). This also proves the reverse norm inequality. Uniqueness follows from weak-* density of \\(A\\) in \\(A''\\), [DC, Theorem 4.4](../../the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-06).\n\nIn particular, on taking \\(A=M=M''\\), every bounded derivation of a von Neumann algebra is normal. The topology facts used here are [OT, Proposition 8.6 and Theorem 9.4](../../compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-09).\n\n### 4. Type I algebras: finite averaging with a uniform bound\n\n**4.1. Finite-dimensional averaging.** Every finite-dimensional unital \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(F\\) has a finite subgroup of its unitary group whose complex linear span is \\(F\\).\n\nAfter a faithful unital representation, \\(F\\) is a von Neumann algebra: a finite-dimensional subspace is closed for the Hausdorff \\(\\sigma\\)-weak topology. By [UE, Lemma 6.3](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-13), \\(F\\) is therefore a finite direct sum of full matrix algebras. On \\(\\mathbb C^n\\), with indices modulo \\(n\\), let\n\\[\nS e_j=e_{j+1},\\qquad T e_j=\\zeta^j e_j,\n\\qquad \\zeta=e^{2\\pi i/n}.\n\\]\nThe group generated by \\(S,T,-1\\) is finite, because every word has the form \\(\\pm\\zeta^kS^rT^s\\), with exponents taken modulo \\(n\\). Its span contains\n\\[\nE_{jj}=\\frac1n\\sum_{s=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{-js}T^s,\n\\qquad E_{ij}=S^{i-j}E_{jj},\n\\]\nand thus all matrices. For a direct sum, take the product of these groups. Its span is an algebra and contains the central projection of each block: this projection is \\((1-g)/2\\), where \\(g\\) is \\(-1\\) on that block and \\(1\\) elsewhere. Multiplying by these projections isolates the matrix spans of all blocks.\n\nLet \\(F\\subseteq P\\) be a finite-dimensional unital subalgebra of a von Neumann algebra, and let \\(D\\) be a bounded derivation of \\(P\\). Choose such a finite group \\(G\\subseteq\\mathcal U(F)\\), and set\n\\[\nb(u)=D(u)u^*,\\qquad a_G=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_{u\\in G}b(u).\n\\]\nThe product rule gives the cocycle identity\n\\[\nb(vu)=b(v)+vb(u)v^*.\n\\]\nAveraging over \\(u\\in G\\) yields\n\\[\na_G=b(v)+va_Gv^*,\\qquad D(v)=a_Gv-va_G\\quad(v\\in G).\n\\]\nSince \\(G\\) spans \\(F\\),\n\\[\nD|_F=[a_G,\\cdot]|_F,\n\\qquad \\|a_G\\|\\le\\|D\\|.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nFor a *-derivation,\n\\[\nb(u)^*=uD(u^*)=-D(u)u^*=-b(u),\n\\]\nso \\(a_G\\) is skew-adjoint.\n\n**4.2. Homogeneous type I algebras.** Suppose first that \\(P\\) is homogeneous of type I. By [PT, Theorem 10.3, Proposition 8.4 and Proposition 9.1](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-14), it has the form\n\\[\nP\\cong B\\,\\bar\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2(I)),\\qquad B\\text{ abelian},\n\\]\nwith matrix units \\(w_{ij}\\). Every finite matrix compression has an expression\n\\[\ne_Jxe_J=\\sum_{i,j\\in J}c_{ij}w_{ij},\n\\qquad e_J=\\sum_{i\\in J}w_{ii},\\qquad c_{ij}\\in Z(P),\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(J\\subset I\\) is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe finite-dimensional unital algebras\n\\[\nF_J=\\operatorname{span}\\{w_{ij}:i,j\\in J\\}+\\mathbb C(1-e_J)\n\\]\nform an increasing net; the zero last summand is omitted when \\(e_J=1\\). By (4.1), for a *-derivation \\(D\\) there is a skew-adjoint \\(a_J\\in P\\) with\n\\[\n\\|a_J\\|\\le\\|D\\|,\\qquad D|_{F_J}=[a_J,\\cdot]|_{F_J}.\n\\]\nThe weak-* compact ball has a cluster point \\(a\\), still skew-adjoint and of norm at most \\(\\|D\\|\\). For fixed \\(i,j\\), the identity for \\(w_{ij}\\) holds eventually along the net, and separate weak-* continuity of multiplication gives\n\\[\nD(w_{ij})=[a,w_{ij}].\n\\]\nStep 2 gives \\(D(c)=0\\) for \\(c\\in Z(P)\\). Hence (4.2) implies\n\\[\nD(e_Jxe_J)=[a,e_Jxe_J].\n\\]\nThe projections \\(e_J\\) increase strongly to \\(1\\); consequently \\(e_Jxe_J\\to x\\) strongly, with norms bounded by \\(\\|x\\|\\). Bounded strong convergence implies weak-* convergence [OT, Lemma 8.5](../../compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-09). By Step 3 and separate normality of multiplication, \\(D(x)=[a,x]\\).\n\n**4.3. Arbitrary type I algebras.** Decompose a type I algebra into its homogeneous central summands by [PT, Theorem 10.3](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-14). Apply 4.2 on each summand and combine the implementers by Step 2. Setting \\(h=-ia\\) for the combined skew-adjoint implementer gives\n\\[\nD(x)=i[h,x],\\qquad h=h^*\\in P,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le\\|D\\|\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nfor every *-derivation of every type I von Neumann algebra. There is no restriction on the cardinalities of the homogeneous summands.\n\n### 5. Spatiality with the same norm bound\n\nLet \\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\), and let \\(D:M\\to M\\) be a *-derivation with \\(\\|D\\|\\le C\\). We prove that\n\\[\nD(x)=i[h,x]\\quad(x\\in M)\n\\]\nfor some \\(h=h^*\\in B(H)\\) with \\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\).\n\nChoose a maximal abelian von Neumann subalgebra \\(B\\subseteq M'\\). Existence follows from Zorn's lemma: the von Neumann algebra generated by the union of a chain of abelian unital algebras is abelian. Maximality gives\n\\[\nM'\\cap B'=B.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nIndeed, a self-adjoint element of the left side can be adjoined to \\(B\\) while preserving commutativity; applying this to the real and imaginary parts proves (5.1).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal A_0\\) be the linear span of products \\(xe\\), with \\(x\\in M\\) and \\(e\\) a projection of \\(B\\). Any finite family of projections in \\(B\\) can be refined into pairwise orthogonal Boolean atoms whose sum is \\(1\\). Thus every element of \\(\\mathcal A_0\\) can be written\n\\[\nT=\\sum_{j=1}^n x_je_j,\n\\qquad e_je_k=0\\ (j\\ne k),\\qquad\\sum_j e_j=1.\n\\]\nFor \\(e\\in\\mathcal P(B)\\), put \\(z=c_{M'}(e)\\). Then \\(z\\in Z(M)\\), and the induction map\n\\[\nMz\\longrightarrow Me\\subseteq B(eH),\\qquad xz\\longmapsto xe,\n\\]\nis an isometric *-isomorphism [DC, Theorem 5.8; PT, Proposition 3.5(3)](../../the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04). By (2.1),\n\\[\n\\|D(x)e\\|=\\|D(x)z\\|=\\|D(xz)\\|\n\\le C\\|xz\\|=C\\|xe\\|.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nE(T)=\\sum_{j=1}^nD(x_j)e_j.\n\\]\nIf \\(T=0\\), multiplying by \\(e_j\\) gives \\(x_je_j=0\\), and (5.2) gives \\(D(x_j)e_j=0\\). Refining two expressions to common Boolean atoms therefore proves that \\(E\\) is well-defined. The orthogonal block decomposition gives\n\\[\n\\|E(T)\\|=\\max_j\\|D(x_j)e_j\\|\n\\le C\\max_j\\|x_je_j\\|=C\\|T\\|.\n\\]\nProducts of generators satisfy \\((xe)(yf)=xyef\\), and \\(e,f\\) commute with all elements of \\(M\\), including their derivatives. The product rule for \\(D\\) therefore proves the product rule for \\(E\\). The same calculation with adjoints proves that \\(E\\) is a *-derivation.\n\nExtend \\(E\\) by norm continuity to \\(\\mathcal A=\\overline{\\mathcal A_0}^{\\|\\cdot\\|}\\), then normally to \\(\\mathcal A''\\) by Step 3. Its norm remains at most \\(C\\). Spectral approximation shows that \\(\\mathcal A\\) contains \\(B\\); hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal A''=(M\\cup B)'',\\qquad (\\mathcal A'')'=M'\\cap B'=B.\n\\]\nAn algebra with abelian commutant is type I [PT, Proposition 11.2](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-15). Step 4 supplies \\(h=h^*\\in\\mathcal A''\\), \\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\), implementing \\(E\\), and therefore implementing \\(D\\) on \\(M\\). This is the spatial construction underlying [K66, Theorem 4], with its well-definedness and norm estimate supplied by (5.2).\n\nTwo consequences will be used repeatedly. First, (2.1) gives\n\\[\n[h,z]=0\\qquad(z\\in Z(M)).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nSecond, if \\(N=M'\\), then \\([h,N]\\subseteq N\\). For \\(y\\in N,x\\in M\\), direct expansion gives\n\\[\n[[h,y],x]=[[h,x],y]=0,\n\\]\nsince \\([h,x]\\in M\\). Consequently\n\\[\nuhu^*-h\\in N\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(N)).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nReplacing \\(h\\) by \\(h+t\\) with \\(t\\in N_h\\) does not change its commutator with \\(M\\).\n\n### 6. A fixed-point theorem for linear isometries\n\nWe need the following result, including its proof.\n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(E\\) be a real Banach space, \\(K\\subseteq E\\) a nonempty weakly compact convex set, and \\(G\\) a group of surjective linear isometries of \\(E\\) with \\(gK=K\\). Then some point of \\(K\\) is fixed by every \\(g\\in G\\).\n\n**6.1. One bounded linear map.** If a bounded linear map \\(F:E\\to E\\) satisfies \\(F(K)\\subseteq K\\), fix \\(x_0\\in K\\) and put\n\\[\nx_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^jx_0.\n\\]\nThe set \\(K\\) is norm bounded: weak compactness gives pointwise boundedness on \\(E^*\\), and the Uniform Boundedness Theorem applies to the canonical images in \\(E^{**}\\). Write \\(R=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\|x\\|\\). Then\n\\[\nx_n\\in K,\\qquad\n\\|Fx_n-x_n\\|=\\frac1n\\|F^nx_0-x_0\\|\\le\\frac{2R}{n}.\n\\]\nA weakly convergent subnet has limit \\(x\\in K\\). Bounded linear maps are weakly continuous, so \\(Fx=x\\).\n\n**6.2. Small caps in a separable space.** Suppose that \\(E\\) is separable and \\(K\\) has more than one point. For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is a proper weakly compact convex subset \\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(K\\setminus K_0)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nHere and below closures in this substep are weak closures.\n\nLet \\(L=\\overline{\\operatorname{Ext}K}\\). By [WT, Theorem 6.1](../../weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06), \\(L\\) is nonempty and compact and \\(K=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}L\\). Separability provides a countable cover of \\(E\\) by closed norm balls of radius \\(\\varepsilon/3\\). Such balls are weakly closed, by Hahn–Banach separation. The Baire theorem for compact Hausdorff spaces, applied to \\(L\\), gives a ball \\(B_0\\) for which \\(L\\cap B_0\\) contains a nonempty relatively weakly open subset \\(U\\) of \\(L\\).\n\nIf \\(L\\setminus U\\) is empty, then \\(K\\subseteq B_0\\), so \\(\\operatorname{diam}K\\le2\\varepsilon/3\\). Any singleton in \\(K\\) is a proper subset satisfying (6.1).\n\nOtherwise put\n\\[\nK_1=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\setminus U),\\qquad\nK_2=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\cap B_0).\n\\]\nBoth are nonempty compact convex subsets of \\(K\\), and \\(K_2\\subseteq B_0\\). The convex join\n\\[\n\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1\\}\n\\]\nis compact, convex, and contains \\(L\\); it is therefore \\(K\\).\n\nChoose \\(y\\in\\operatorname{Ext}K\\cap U\\), which exists because \\(\\operatorname{Ext}K\\) is dense in \\(L\\). We have \\(y\\notin K_1\\). Indeed, otherwise \\(y\\) would be extreme in \\(K_1\\), and the converse to Krein–Milman [WT, Theorem 6.2](../../weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06), applied to the compact set \\(L\\setminus U\\), would give \\(y\\in L\\setminus U\\).\n\nFor \\(0<\\eta<1\\), define\n\\[\nK_\\eta=\\eta K_1+(1-\\eta)K\n=\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ \\eta\\le t\\le1\\}.\n\\]\nThe equality follows by substituting the preceding description of \\(K\\) into the first expression. This set is compact and convex. It does not contain \\(y\\): an expression \\(y=\\eta v+(1-\\eta)k\\), with \\(v\\in K_1,k\\in K\\), would force \\(v=y\\) by extremality.\n\nIf \\(x,x'\\notin K_\\eta\\), write\n\\[\nx=tv+(1-t)w,\\qquad x'=t'v'+(1-t')w'\n\\]\nwith \\(v,v'\\in K_1\\), \\(w,w'\\in K_2\\). Necessarily \\(t,t'<\\eta\\). Since \\(R=\\sup_K\\|x\\|>0\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x-x'\\|\n&\\le t\\|v-w\\|+\\|w-w'\\|+t'\\|v'-w'\\|\\\\\n&\\le2R(t+t')+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}\n\\le4R\\eta+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\eta<\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon/(12R)\\}\\). Then \\(K_0=K_\\eta\\) proves (6.1).\n\n**6.3. Finitely many isometries.** Given \\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\in G\\), apply 6.1 to \\(F=n^{-1}\\sum_jg_j\\), obtaining \\(x\\in K\\) with\n\\[\nx=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ng_jx.\n\\]\nIf some, but not all, of the \\(g_j\\) fix \\(x\\), cancel their contributions and renumber. To obtain a contradiction, we may thus assume\n\\[\nx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^mg_jx,\n\\qquad g_jx\\ne x\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nThe subgroup \\(G_0\\) generated by these finitely many isometries is countable. Replace \\(K\\) by the weakly closed convex hull of \\(G_0x\\). This hull is a weakly compact subset of the original \\(K\\). It lies in the norm-closed linear span \\(E_0\\) of the countable orbit: a norm-closed linear subspace is weakly closed by Hahn–Banach. The space \\(E_0\\) is separable, and its weak topology is the subspace weak topology, again by Hahn–Banach extension. Thus 6.2 applies.\n\nChoose\n\\[\n0<\\varepsilon<\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}\\|g_jx-x\\|\n\\]\nand a set \\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\) satisfying (6.1). Some \\(hx\\), \\(h\\in G_0\\), lies outside \\(K_0\\), since otherwise the closed convex hull of the orbit would be contained in \\(K_0\\). Equation (6.2) gives\n\\[\nhx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^m hg_jx.\n\\]\nConvexity of \\(K_0\\) implies that \\(hg_jx\\notin K_0\\) for some \\(j\\). But then\n\\[\n\\|g_jx-x\\|=\\|hg_jx-hx\\|\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]\ncontrary to the choice of \\(\\varepsilon\\). Therefore the original \\(x\\) is fixed by all \\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\).\n\n**6.4. An arbitrary group.** For each \\(g\\), its fixed-point set in \\(K\\) is weakly closed. Substep 6.3 proves the finite intersection property for these sets. Compactness gives a point in their intersection. This proves the lemma.\n\nThe fixed-point argument follows [L26, Theorem 1, Proposition 2 and Lemma 3].\n\n### 7. Finite algebras have nonzero normal finite traces\n\nWe first prove the projection estimates needed to construct a trace.\n\n**7.1. Increasing comparison in a finite algebra.** Let \\(P\\) be a finite von Neumann algebra. If\n\\[\ne_n\\uparrow e,\\qquad e_n\\precsim f\\quad(n\\ge1),\n\\]\nthen \\(e\\precsim f\\).\n\nConstruct partial isometries \\(v_n\\) with\n\\[\nv_n^*v_n=e_n,\\qquad v_nv_n^*\\le f,\\qquad v_{n+1}e_n=v_n.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nChoose \\(v_1\\) using \\(e_1\\precsim f\\). If \\(v_n\\) is chosen, choose \\(w\\) with \\(w^*w=e_{n+1}\\), \\(ww^*\\le f\\). Put\n\\[\np=we_nw^*,\\qquad r=v_nv_n^*,\\qquad t=v_nw^*.\n\\]\nThen \\(t^*t=p\\), \\(tt^*=r\\). The algebra \\(fPf\\) is finite [PT, Lemma 6.2](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-09). Complement equivalence in this finite algebra [PT, Proposition 14.2](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-18) gives a partial isometry \\(t_0\\) from \\(f-p\\) to \\(f-r\\). Hence \\(u=t+t_0\\) is a unitary of \\(fPf\\). Define \\(v_{n+1}=uw\\). It has the required initial and final projections, and\n\\[\nv_{n+1}e_n=tw e_n=v_nw^*we_n=v_n.\n\\]\nThis proves (7.1).\n\nFor \\(m\\ge n\\),\n\\[\nv_m-v_n=v_m(e_m-e_n),\\qquad\n\\|(v_m-v_n)\\xi\\|=\\|(e_m-e_n)\\xi\\|.\n\\]\nThus \\(v_n\\) converges strongly to \\(v\\in P\\). Moreover, \\(\\|v\\xi\\|=\\|e\\xi\\|\\) for every \\(\\xi\\), so \\(v^*v=e\\), while \\(fv=v\\). Consequently \\(vv^*\\le f\\), proving \\(e\\precsim f\\).\n\nWe will also use reversal of comparison on complements in \\(P\\). If \\(e\\precsim f\\), choose \\(e_1\\le f\\) with \\(e_1\\sim e\\). Proposition 14.2 gives \\(1-e_1\\sim1-e\\). Since \\(1-f\\le1-e_1\\),\n\\[\n1-f\\precsim1-e.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\n**7.2. Equivalent copies of orthogonal projections tend strongly to zero.** Suppose that \\(f_n\\in\\mathcal P(P)\\) are pairwise orthogonal and \\(g_n\\sim f_n\\). We claim that \\(g_n\\to0\\) strongly.\n\nFor \\(n\\ge m\\), let\n\\[\nr_{m,n}=\\bigvee_{j=m}^n g_j,\\qquad r_{m,m-1}=0,\n\\qquad d_{m,n}=r_{m,n}-r_{m,n-1}.\n\\]\nThe parallelogram law [PT, Proposition 4.4](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-04) gives\n\\[\nd_{m,n}\\sim g_n-(g_n\\wedge r_{m,n-1})\\precsim g_n\\sim f_n.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(m\\), the \\(d_{m,n}\\) are orthogonal. Additivity of subequivalence [PT, Lemma 3.3](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-01) yields\n\\[\nr_{m,n}\\precsim\\sum_{j=m}^n f_j\\le F_m,\n\\qquad F_m=\\sum_{j\\ge m}f_j.\n\\]\nBy 7.1,\n\\[\np_m:=\\bigvee_{j\\ge m}g_j\\precsim F_m.\n\\]\nLet \\(p=\\bigwedge_m p_m\\). By (7.2),\n\\[\n1-F_m\\precsim1-p_m\\le1-p.\n\\]\nOrthogonality of the \\(f_n\\) gives \\(F_m\\downarrow0\\) strongly, hence \\(1-F_m\\uparrow1\\). Applying 7.1 again gives \\(1\\precsim1-p\\). Finiteness of \\(1\\) forces \\(p=0\\): a subprojection of \\(1-p\\) equivalent to \\(1\\) must equal \\(1\\). Therefore \\(p_m\\downarrow0\\) strongly. Since \\(g_m\\le p_m\\),\n\\[\n\\|g_m\\xi\\|\\le\\|p_m\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n**7.3. Weak compactness of a normal state's conjugates.** Choose a normal state \\(\\varphi\\) on a nonzero finite algebra \\(P\\), for example a unit-vector state in a faithful concrete representation. For every orthogonal sequence \\((f_n)\\subseteq\\mathcal P(P)\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{u\\in\\mathcal U(P)}\\varphi(u^*f_nu)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nOtherwise there are a subsequence \\(f_{n_k}\\), unitaries \\(u_k\\), and \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) for which \\(\\varphi(u_k^*f_{n_k}u_k)\\ge\\varepsilon\\). The projections \\(g_k=u_k^*f_{n_k}u_k\\) are equivalent to the orthogonal projections \\(f_{n_k}\\); (7.3) makes them converge strongly to zero. Their norms are bounded, so [OT, Lemma 8.5](../../compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-09) gives weak-* convergence to zero, contradicting normality of \\(\\varphi\\).\n\nLet \\(K\\) be the weak closure in \\(P_*\\) of the convex hull of\n\\[\n\\{\\varphi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\]\nThe convex hull is norm bounded and satisfies (7.4) uniformly. Therefore [PW, Theorem 10.2, implication (7)\\(\\Rightarrow\\)(1)](../../polar-decomposition-of-functionals-and-weak-compactness-in-preduals.html#OA-FND-PD-16) makes \\(K\\) weakly compact. Positivity and value \\(1\\) at the identity are weakly closed conditions, so every element of \\(K\\) is a normal state.\n\nThe weak topology on the real self-adjoint part \\((P_*)_h\\) is its real Banach-space weak topology, by Hahn–Banach extension and complexification of real functionals. Conjugation acts on this real Banach space by surjective linear isometries preserving \\(K\\). The lemma in Step 6 gives a normal state \\(\\tau\\in K\\) invariant under all conjugations. For \\(u\\) unitary and \\(x\\in P\\), invariance applied to \\(xu\\) gives\n\\[\n\\tau(ux)=\\tau\\bigl(u(xu)u^*\\bigr)=\\tau(xu).\n\\]\nThe unitaries linearly span \\(P\\) [CF, Proposition 7.3](../../c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-09), so\n\\[\n\\tau(yx)=\\tau(xy)\\qquad(x,y\\in P).\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nThus every nonzero finite von Neumann algebra has a nonzero normal finite trace, normalized here to be a state. The trace construction uses the orbit idea of [L25, Propositions 3 and 6]; 7.1–7.2 give all comparison and limiting details needed for its Proposition 7.\n\n**7.4. Central supports and faithfulness.** The support \\(s(\\tau)\\) exists and \\(\\tau\\) is faithful on \\(s(\\tau)Ps(\\tau)\\) [UE, Lemma 11.1](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18). Unitary invariance and uniqueness of the support give\n\\[\nus(\\tau)u^*=s(\\tau)\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\]\nSince unitaries span \\(P\\), \\(s(\\tau)\\) is central.\n\nChoose a maximal family of normal tracial states whose nonzero central supports \\(z_i\\) are pairwise orthogonal. If \\(1-\\sum_i z_i\\ne0\\), apply 7.3 to that finite central summand and extend its trace by zero to \\(P\\). Its nonzero support is orthogonal to every \\(z_i\\), contradicting maximality. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_i z_i=1,\n\\]\nand each \\(Pz_i\\) has a faithful normal tracial state.\n\nIf \\(P\\) is countably decomposable, this family is countable. Choose strictly positive scalar weights \\(\\lambda_i\\) with \\(\\sum_i\\lambda_i=1\\). The norm-convergent sum\n\\[\n\\tau_0=\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\tau_i\\in P_*\n\\]\nis a normal tracial state. It is faithful: for \\(x\\ge0\\), \\(\\tau_0(x)=0\\) forces \\(\\tau_i(x)=0\\) for every \\(i\\), hence \\(xz_i=0\\) by faithfulness on \\(Pz_i\\), and then \\(x=0\\). Consequently every countably decomposable finite von Neumann algebra has a faithful normal tracial state.\n\n### 8. Replacing a spatial implementer when the commutant is finite\n\nWe prove a statement about normalizing operators. Let \\(P\\subseteq B(H)\\) be finite and countably decomposable, and let \\(h=h^*\\in B(H)\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\|h\\|\\le C,\\qquad uhu^*-h\\in P\\quad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nThen there exists \\(k=k^*\\in P'\\) such that\n\\[\nk-h\\in P,\\qquad \\|k\\|\\le C.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\nTake a faithful normal tracial state \\(\\tau\\) from Step 7. On the real vector space \\(P_h\\), set\n\\[\n\\langle x,y\\rangle_2=\\tau(xy),\\qquad \\|x\\|_2=\\tau(x^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe trace identity makes this a real symmetric bilinear form: \\(\\overline{\\tau(xy)}=\\tau(yx)=\\tau(xy)\\). Faithfulness makes it positive definite. Let \\(L^2(P,\\tau)_h\\) denote its real Hilbert completion, and \\(j:P_h\\to L^2(P,\\tau)_h\\) the injective inclusion.\n\nPut\n\\[\nc(u)=uhu^*-h\\in P_h,\n\\qquad T_u(y)=c(u)+uyu^*.\n\\]\nConjugation preserves \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\), by the trace property. Also\n\\[\nc(uv)=c(u)+uc(v)u^*.\n\\]\nThus the \\(T_u\\) extend to an action by affine isometries of the real Hilbert space. The orbit of zero is \\(\\{j(c(u))\\}\\), and it is bounded, since \\(\\|c(u)\\|_2\\le\\|c(u)\\|\\le2C\\). Its Hilbert-norm closed convex hull \\(S\\) is nonempty, bounded, and invariant under every \\(T_u\\).\n\n**8.1. A Hilbert-space circumcentre.** For \\(y\\in S\\), define\n\\[\nR(y)=\\sup_{s\\in S}\\|y-s\\|_2,\\qquad r=\\inf_{y\\in S}R(y).\n\\]\nFor \\(y,z\\in S\\), the parallelogram identity gives\n\\[\nR\\!\\left(\\frac{y+z}{2}\\right)^2\n\\le\\frac{R(y)^2+R(z)^2}{2}-\\frac{\\|y-z\\|_2^2}{4}.\n\\]\nSince the midpoint belongs to \\(S\\),\n\\[\n\\|y-z\\|_2^2\\le2R(y)^2+2R(z)^2-4r^2.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nA sequence with \\(R(y_n)\\to r\\) is therefore Cauchy. Its limit lies in \\(S\\), and the inequality \\(|R(y)-R(z)|\\le\\|y-z\\|_2\\) shows that it attains \\(r\\). Equation (8.3) proves uniqueness. As each \\(T_u\\) is an isometry carrying \\(S\\) onto itself, it preserves \\(R\\) and hence fixes this unique point, say \\(y_0\\).\n\n**8.2. Recovering the operator norm bound.** Let\n\\[\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\,w^*}\n\\{uhu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}\\subseteq B(H)_h.\n\\]\nIt is weak-* compact and contained in the operator-norm ball of radius \\(C\\). From (8.1),\n\\[\nK-h\\subseteq P_h,\\qquad \\|t\\|\\le2C\\quad(t\\in K-h).\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]\nThe weak-* topology of \\(P\\) is the subspace \\(\\sigma\\)-weak topology from \\(B(H)\\), by [OT, Theorem 9.4](../../compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-10). On a norm-bounded subset of \\(P_h\\), the map \\(j\\) is continuous from weak-* to Hilbert-space weak topology. For a test vector \\(j(z)\\), \\(z\\in P_h\\), this follows because\n\\[\n\\langle j(t),j(z)\\rangle_2=\\tau(tz)\n\\]\nis a normal functional of \\(t\\). For an arbitrary Hilbert-space test vector, approximate it in \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\) by such \\(j(z)\\); the approximation is uniform over (8.4), since \\(\\|j(t)\\|_2\\le2C\\).\n\nIt follows that \\(j(K-h)\\) is weakly compact and convex in the Hilbert space, and hence norm closed. It contains the orbit of zero, so it contains \\(S\\). In particular \\(y_0=j(t)\\) for some \\(t\\in K-h\\). Since \\(j\\) is injective, the fixed-point equations for \\(y_0\\) give\n\\[\nc(u)+utu^*=t\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\]\nThus \\(k=h+t\\in K\\) satisfies \\(uku^*=k\\) for all unitaries of \\(P\\). Their linear span is \\(P\\), so \\(k\\in P'\\), and \\(\\|k\\|\\le C\\). This proves (8.2).\n\nApplied to \\(P=M'\\), using (5.4), this replaces the spatial implementer by a self-adjoint element of \\(M\\), without increasing its norm, whenever \\(M'\\) is finite and countably decomposable.\n\n### 9. Averaging in a countably decomposable type III algebra\n\nWe prove the exact averaging assertion needed for the type III case.\n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(P\\) be a countably decomposable type III von Neumann algebra, and let \\(x=x^*\\in P\\). There is a least self-adjoint central majorant \\(b=b(x)\\) of \\(x\\), and\n\\[\n\\|b\\|\\le\\|x\\|,\\qquad\nb\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|}\n\\{uxu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(x=0\\), take \\(b=0\\). Otherwise let \\(R=\\|x\\|\\) and consider\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_x=\\{c\\in Z(P)_h:x\\le c\\le R1\\}.\n\\]\nIt is nonempty. If \\(c,d\\in\\mathcal C_x\\), the central projection\n\\[\ne=1_{(-\\infty,0]}(c-d)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\min(c,d)=ec+(1-e)d\\ge x.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_x\\) is downward directed, and its elements lie between \\(-R1\\) and \\(R1\\). The bounded decreasing net of these elements has a strong limit \\(b\\), by [ST, Theorem 6.1](../../the-spectral-theorem-for-bounded-self-adjoint-operators.html#OA-FND-ST-06). The centre is strongly closed, so \\(b\\in Z(P)_h\\). Passing to the strong limit in the inequalities gives \\(x\\le b\\le R1\\), and \\(b\\le c\\) for every \\(c\\in\\mathcal C_x\\). If \\(c\\) is any other central self-adjoint majorant of \\(x\\), then \\(\\min(c,R1)\\in\\mathcal C_x\\), so \\(b\\le c\\). This proves leastness and \\(\\|b\\|\\le R\\).\n\nFor \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), set\n\\[\np=1_{[0,\\varepsilon)}(b-x),\\qquad q=1-p.\n\\]\nThe central support of \\(p\\) is \\(1\\). Otherwise \\(z=1-c(p)\\ne0\\) is central and annihilates \\(p\\). Spectral calculus then gives\n\\[\nz(b-x)\\ge\\varepsilon z.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(b-\\varepsilon z\\) is a central majorant of \\(x\\) strictly smaller than \\(b\\), a contradiction.\n\nPut \\(w=c(q)\\). If \\(w\\ne0\\), both \\(wp\\) and \\(q\\) have central support \\(w\\) in \\(Pw\\). Every nonzero projection of a type III algebra is properly infinite [PT, Lemma 6.2(3) and Definition 7.1](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-09). The algebra \\(Pw\\) is countably decomposable. Therefore [PT, Proposition 15.2(4)](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-19) gives\n\\[\nwp\\sim w\\sim q.\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge2\\), repeated halving [PT, Proposition 13.4](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-17) partitions \\(w\\) into orthogonal projections\n\\[\nw=q_1+\\cdots+q_n,\\qquad q_j\\sim w.\n\\]\nFor every \\(j\\), \\(w-q_j\\sim w\\), since it contains one of the other \\(q_k\\sim w\\) and is at most \\(w\\); use projection Schröder–Bernstein [PT, Proposition 5.1](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-03). By (9.2),\n\\[\nq\\sim q_j,\\qquad wp=w-q\\sim w-q_j.\n\\]\nAdd partial isometries implementing these two equivalences. Their orthogonal initial and final projections make their sum a unitary of \\(Pw\\) sending \\(q\\) to \\(q_j\\). Extend it by \\(1-w\\) to a unitary \\(u_j\\in P\\).\n\nFunctional calculus gives\n\\[\n0\\le b-x\\le\\varepsilon1+2R q.\n\\]\nEach \\(u_j\\) commutes with the central element \\(b\\), so\n\\[\n0\\le b-\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^nu_jxu_j^*\n\\le\\varepsilon1+\\frac{2R}{n}\\sum_{j=1}^nq_j\n=\\varepsilon1+\\frac{2R}{n}w.\n\\tag{9.3}\n\\]\nIf \\(w=0\\), then \\(q=0\\) and \\(0\\le b-x\\le\\varepsilon1\\), which gives the needed approximation with the identity unitary. For any prescribed error, choose \\(\\varepsilon\\) small and then \\(n\\) large in (9.3). This proves (9.1).\n\n### 10. Replacing a spatial implementer when the commutant is type III\n\nLet \\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\), let \\(N=M'\\) be countably decomposable and type III, and let \\(h=h^*\\), \\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\), be the spatial implementer from Step 5. We construct\n\\[\nh_0=h_0^*\\in M,\\qquad \\|h_0\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad h_0-h\\in N.\n\\tag{10.1}\n\\]\nFor \\(C=0\\), take \\(h_0=0\\). Suppose \\(C>0\\), put \\(b=h+C1\\), and define\n\\[\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\,w^*}\n\\{ubu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(N)\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nK\\subseteq[0,2C1]\\cap(b+N)_h.\n\\tag{10.2}\n\\]\nAlso every element of \\(K\\) commutes with \\(Z(N)=Z(M)\\), by (5.3). The set \\(K\\) is nonempty, weak-* compact, convex, and invariant under conjugation by \\(\\mathcal U(N)\\).\n\nChoose an inclusion-minimal nonempty weak-* compact convex invariant subset \\(L\\subseteq K\\). Zorn's lemma applies because an inclusion-decreasing chain has nonempty intersection by compactness of \\(K\\), and that intersection remains compact, convex, and invariant.\n\nFor a central projection \\(z\\in N\\) and \\(k\\in L\\), the set\n\\[\n\\{l\\in L:\\|lz\\|\\le\\|kz\\|\\}\n\\]\nis nonempty, weak-* compact, convex, and invariant. Weak-* closedness follows from lower semicontinuity of the norm and normality of multiplication by \\(z\\); invariance follows because the conjugating unitaries commute with \\(z\\). Minimality forces this set to be all of \\(L\\). Interchanging \\(k,l\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\|kz\\|=\\|lz\\|\\qquad(k,l\\in L,\\ z\\in\\mathcal P(Z(N))).\n\\tag{10.3}\n\\]\n\nThe difference set\n\\[\nV=L-L\n\\]\nis a weak-* compact convex symmetric invariant subset of \\(N_h\\), by (10.2). Suppose \\(d\\in V\\) is nonzero. Replacing \\(d\\) by \\(-d\\) if necessary, arrange that its positive part is nonzero. Apply Step 9 inside \\(N\\). The central majorant \\(b_N(d)\\) belongs to the norm-closed convex hull of the unitary orbit of \\(d\\), and hence to \\(V\\). Since \\(b_N(d)\\ge d\\), it cannot be nonpositive. There are therefore \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) and a nonzero central spectral projection \\(z\\) with\n\\[\nb_N(d)z\\ge\\varepsilon z.\n\\]\nWrite \\(b_N(d)=k-l\\) with \\(k,l\\in L\\). The operators \\(k,l\\) are positive and commute with \\(z\\), so\n\\[\nkz\\ge lz+\\varepsilon z.\n\\]\nOn the nonzero Hilbert space \\(zH\\), positivity implies\n\\[\n\\|kz\\|\\ge\\|lz+\\varepsilon z\\|=\\|lz\\|+\\varepsilon,\n\\]\ncontradicting (10.3). Thus \\(V=\\{0\\}\\), and \\(L\\) is a singleton, say \\(\\{k\\}\\).\n\nIts invariance makes \\(k\\) commute with every unitary of \\(N\\), hence with \\(N\\), so \\(k\\in N'=M\\). By (10.2), \\(0\\le k\\le2C1\\) and \\(k-b\\in N\\). Set \\(h_0=k-C1\\). Then \\(-C1\\le h_0\\le C1\\), and (10.1) follows.\n\nThe minimal-invariant-set argument is the part of [K66, Addendum, Theorem 15, p. 292] needed here. Its averaging input has been proved explicitly in Step 9.\n\n### 11. Finite, properly infinite, and semifinite commutants\n\nWe now prove innerness, with the same bound \\(C\\), for every concrete algebra whose commutant is countably decomposable.\n\n**11.1. A full finite corner in a semifinite algebra.** Let \\(N\\) be semifinite. Every nonzero central summand of \\(N\\) contains a nonzero finite projection. Indeed, by [PT, Definition 7.1 and Theorem 7.2](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-10), a nonzero type I part contains an abelian projection, which is finite by Lemma 6.2; a nonzero type II part contains a nonzero finite projection by the definition of type II. There is no type III central part in a semifinite algebra.\n\nChoose a maximal family of nonzero finite projections \\(q_j\\in N\\) whose central supports \\(z_j=c_N(q_j)\\) are pairwise orthogonal. If \\(z=1-\\sum_jz_j\\ne0\\), a nonzero finite projection in \\(Nz\\) would enlarge the family. Hence \\(\\sum_jz_j=1\\). The strong sum\n\\[\nq=\\sum_jq_j\n\\]\nis finite by [PT, Lemma 6.3](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-09), which proves finiteness of centrally orthogonal sums of finite projections. Moreover,\n\\[\nc_N(q)=1.\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]\nFor if a central projection majorizes \\(q\\), it majorizes every \\(q_j\\), and therefore every \\(z_j\\).\n\n**11.2. Compression of a spatial implementer.** Let \\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\), \\(N=M'\\), and suppose now that \\(N\\) is countably decomposable and semifinite. Let \\(D\\) be a *-derivation of \\(M\\) with \\(\\|D\\|\\le C\\), and take a spatial implementer \\(h=h^*\\), \\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\), from Step 5. Choose \\(q\\in N\\) as in 11.1.\n\nBy [DC, Theorem 5.8; PT, Proposition 3.5(3)](../../the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04), the map\n\\[\n\\sigma:M\\longrightarrow B(qH),\\qquad \\sigma(x)=xq|_{qH},\n\\]\nhas a von Neumann algebra as its image, with commutant \\(qNq\\), and its kernel is \\(M(1-c_N(q))=0\\). It is therefore an isometric *-isomorphism onto its image, and is normal in both directions by [UE, Corollary 11.4](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18). Transport \\(D\\) to \\(\\sigma(M)\\), writing\n\\[\nD_q(\\sigma(x))=\\sigma(D(x)).\n\\]\nFor \\(h_q=qhq|_{qH}\\), direct multiplication, using \\(xq=qx\\), gives\n\\[\ni[h_q,\\sigma(x)]=iq[h,x]q|_{qH}\n=\\sigma(D(x)).\n\\tag{11.2}\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\|h_q\\|\\le C\\).\n\nThe algebra \\(qNq\\) is finite, because \\(q\\) is a finite projection, and is countably decomposable, because any orthogonal family in it is an orthogonal family in \\(N\\). From (11.2), the commutator calculation following (5.3), now in \\(B(qH)\\), gives\n\\[\nvh_qv^*-h_q\\in qNq\\qquad(v\\in\\mathcal U(qNq)).\n\\]\nStep 8 therefore provides a self-adjoint\n\\[\nk_q\\in(qNq)'=\\sigma(M),\\qquad \\|k_q\\|\\le C,\n\\]\nwith \\(k_q-h_q\\in qNq\\). It implements \\(D_q\\). Its inverse image \\(k=\\sigma^{-1}(k_q)\\) is self-adjoint, has norm at most \\(C\\), and satisfies\n\\[\nD(x)=i[k,x]\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]\nby injectivity of \\(\\sigma\\).\n\nThis argument includes properly infinite semifinite algebras, of types I and II. In the finite case one may take \\(q=1\\), reducing directly to Step 8. No trace on the original semifinite algebra is assumed or used; the finite trace is constructed on the finite corner.\n\n**11.3. The complete countably decomposable-commutant case.** For an arbitrary countably decomposable \\(N=M'\\), [PT, Theorem 7.2](../../projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-10) first splits its identity by central projections into a finite part and a properly infinite part. For completeness, if \\(1=e_f+e_p\\) with orthogonal central supports, then \\(c(e_f)=c(e_f)(e_f+e_p)=e_f\\), and similarly \\(e_p\\) is central. The type decomposition then splits the properly infinite part into its semifinite and type III parts. Thus there are pairwise orthogonal central projections\n\\[\nz_f+z_s+z_3=1\n\\]\nwith \\(Nz_f\\) finite, \\(Nz_s\\) properly infinite and semifinite, and \\(Nz_3\\) type III; zero parts are omitted.\n\nThese projections belong to \\(Z(M)\\). On each \\(z_jH\\), the algebras \\(Mz_j\\) and \\(Nz_j\\) are mutual commutants [DC, Theorem 5.8](../../the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04). Each commutant remains countably decomposable. Equation (5.3) makes \\(hz_j\\) a self-adjoint spatial implementer on that subspace, of norm at most \\(C\\). Apply Step 8 on \\(z_f\\), 11.2 on \\(z_s\\), and Step 10 on \\(z_3\\). The resulting self-adjoint implementers in \\(Mz_j\\) all have norm at most \\(C\\). Step 2 combines them into one self-adjoint \\(k\\in M\\) with \\(\\|k\\|\\le C\\).\n\nWe have proved:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ M'\\text{ countably decomposable},\\ D\\text{ a *-derivation}\n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ D=i[k,\\cdot],\\quad k=k^*\\in M,\n\\quad\\|k\\|\\le\\|D\\|.\\ }\n\\tag{11.3}\n\\]\n\n### 12. Arbitrary central sums through faithful cyclic representations\n\nAn arbitrary von Neumann algebra need not be a central sum of countably decomposable algebras. The reduction needed here is instead to faithful cyclic representations of central summands; their **commutants** are countably decomposable. We prove that reduction.\n\nFix a concrete \\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\), and put \\(N=M'\\). For a nonzero vector \\(\\xi\\in H\\), let \\(p_\\xi\\) be the projection onto \\([M\\xi]\\). This subspace reduces \\(M\\), so \\(p_\\xi\\in N\\). Put\n\\[\nz_\\xi=c_N(p_\\xi)\\in Z(N)=Z(M).\n\\]\nThe induction results [DC, Theorem 5.8; PT, Proposition 3.5(3)](../../the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04), together with normality of *-isomorphisms [UE, Corollary 11.4](../../the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18), give a faithful normal isometric representation\n\\[\n\\rho_\\xi:Mz_\\xi\\longrightarrow B(p_\\xi H),\n\\qquad \\rho_\\xi(x)=xp_\\xi|_{p_\\xi H},\n\\tag{12.1}\n\\]\nwhose image is a von Neumann algebra with commutant\n\\[\n\\rho_\\xi(Mz_\\xi)'=p_\\xi Np_\\xi.\n\\tag{12.2}\n\\]\nThe vector \\(\\xi\\) is cyclic for the image in (12.1). It is separating for the algebra in (12.2): if \\(t\\xi=0\\) and \\(t\\in p_\\xi Np_\\xi\\), then\n\\[\nt(x\\xi)=x(t\\xi)=0\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]\nand these vectors have dense span in \\(p_\\xi H\\), so \\(t=0\\).\n\nIt follows directly that \\(p_\\xi Np_\\xi\\) is countably decomposable. Indeed, if \\((e_j)\\) is an orthogonal family of nonzero projections in it, each \\(e_j\\xi\\ne0\\), while\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in F}\\|e_j\\xi\\|^2\\le\\|\\xi\\|^2\n\\]\nfor every finite subfamily \\(F\\). A family of strictly positive real numbers with bounded finite subsums is countable: for each positive integer \\(n\\), only finitely many terms can exceed \\(1/n\\), and every positive term exceeds \\(1/n\\) for some \\(n\\). Thus the family of projections is countable.\n\nChoose, by Zorn's lemma, a maximal family \\((\\xi_i)_{i\\in I}\\) for which the nonzero central projections \\(z_i=z_{\\xi_i}\\) are pairwise orthogonal. Their strong sum is \\(1\\). To see this, if\n\\[\nz=1-\\sum_i z_i\\ne0,\n\\]\nchoose a nonzero \\(\\xi\\in zH\\). Since \\(z\\) is central, \\(M\\xi\\subseteq zH\\); thus \\(p_\\xi\\le z\\) and \\(0\\ne z_\\xi\\le z\\). This contradicts maximality.\n\nLet \\(D\\) be a *-derivation of \\(M\\), and set \\(C=\\|D\\|\\). Step 2 restricts it to each \\(Mz_i\\), with norm at most \\(C\\). Transport that restriction through the isomorphism \\(\\rho_{\\xi_i}\\). Its image algebra has countably decomposable commutant by (12.2), so (11.3) gives a self-adjoint implementer of norm at most \\(C\\) there. Pull it back through \\(\\rho_{\\xi_i}\\), obtaining\n\\[\nh_i=h_i^*\\in Mz_i,\\qquad \\|h_i\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad D|_{Mz_i}=i[h_i,\\cdot].\n\\]\nThe uniform central-sum construction of Step 2 applies to the arbitrary index set \\(I\\). It gives\n\\[\nh=\\sum_i h_i\\in M_h,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad D(x)=i[h,x]\\quad(x\\in M).\n\\tag{12.3}\n\\]\nThis is the central-cyclic reduction in [K66, Addendum, Theorem 15, pp. 291–292], with the countability assertion made on the commutants and the uniform summation estimate given explicitly.\n\n### 13. General derivations, the bound, and uniqueness\n\nLet \\(\\delta\\) now be an arbitrary complex-linear derivation of \\(M\\). It is bounded by Step 1. The *-derivations \\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\) in 1.1 satisfy (1.1). By (12.3), choose\n\\[\nh_j=h_j^*\\in M,\\qquad\n\\delta_j=i[h_j,\\cdot],\\qquad\n\\|h_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\n\\quad(j=1,2).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\delta=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2=[ih_1-h_2,\\cdot].\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\na=ih_1-h_2\\in M,\\qquad\n\\|a\\|\\le\\|h_1\\|+\\|h_2\\|\\le2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\delta\\) itself preserves the involution, (12.3) directly gives \\(a=ih\\), \\(h=h^*\\), \\(\\|h\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\\).\n\nFinally,\n\\[\n[a,x]=[b,x]\\text{ for every }x\\in M\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n[a-b,x]=0\\text{ for every }x\\in M\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-b\\in Z(M).\n\\]\nThis proves uniqueness modulo the centre and its converse. If \\(b\\) is any implementer and \\(a\\) is one of the bounded implementers just constructed, then \\(b-a\\in Z(M)\\), so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,Z(M))\\le\\|a\\|.\n\\]\nAll the stated conclusions follow. \\(\\square\\)\n\n## Closedness under a finite-dimensional extension\n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(E\\) be a normed space, \\(F\\subseteq E\\) a closed linear subspace and \\(V\\subseteq E\\) a finite-dimensional subspace. Then \\(F+V\\) is closed in \\(E\\).\n\n**Proof.** Write \\(q:E\\to E/F\\) for the quotient map. The formula\n\\[\n\\|q(x)\\|=\\inf_{f\\in F}\\|x-f\\|\n\\]\nis independent of the representative, since translating \\(x\\) by an element of \\(F\\) merely permutes the elements over which the infimum is taken. It defines a norm: the triangle inequality follows by adding approximants in \\(F\\), and homogeneity follows by rescaling them; zero norm implies \\(x\\in\\overline F=F\\). Also \\(\\|q(x)\\|\\le\\|x\\|\\), so \\(q\\) is continuous. The image \\(q(V)\\) is finite-dimensional and is therefore closed in the normed, hence Hausdorff, space \\(E/F\\), by [HB, Theorem 7.1](../../hahn-banach-baire-and-the-basic-theorems-on-banach-spaces.html#OA-FND-HB-07)(2). Since \\(F+V=q^{-1}(q(V))\\), the desired sum is closed. No completeness assumption on the quotient is needed. \\(\\square\\)\n\nIn Step 1, faithful representation is isometric by [CF, Corollary 4.6](../../c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-12), so the represented C*-algebra is closed in \\(B(H)\\). Apply the lemma with \\(E=B(H)\\), \\(F=A\\) and \\(V=\\mathbb C1\\).\n\n## Compactness, intersections and convergent subnets\n\n**Lemma.** A family of closed subsets of a compact space has nonempty intersection if every finite subfamily has nonempty intersection. Every net in a compact space has a convergent subnet. Neither assertion requires metrizability or a countable index set.\n\n**Proof.** If the closed sets had empty intersection, their open complements would cover the space. A finite subcover would contradict the finite intersection assumption.\n\nFor a net \\((x_i)_{i\\in I}\\) in a compact space \\(K\\), let \\(F_i\\) be the closure of its tail \\(\\{x_j:j\\ge i\\}\\). These nonempty closed sets have the finite intersection property, because any finite set of indices has a common upper bound. Choose \\(x\\in\\bigcap_iF_i\\). Every open neighbourhood of \\(x\\) meets every tail.\n\nLet \\(D\\) consist of pairs \\((i,U)\\), where \\(U\\) is an open neighbourhood of \\(x\\) and \\(x_i\\in U\\). Order it by \\((i,U)\\preceq(j,V)\\) when \\(i\\le j\\) and \\(V\\subseteq U\\). It is directed: given two pairs, choose an upper bound of their indices and then a later term lying in the intersection of their neighbourhoods. The projection \\(D\\to I\\), \\((i,U)\\mapsto i\\), is order-preserving and cofinal, since \\((i,K)\\in D\\) for every \\(i\\). The resulting subnet converges to \\(x\\): after any pair whose neighbourhood is contained in a prescribed neighbourhood \\(U_0\\), all subsequent terms lie in \\(U_0\\). Such a pair exists because every neighbourhood meets every tail. \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>Statement<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Theorem (automatic continuity and Kadison–Sakai innerness).<\/strong> A derivation of a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*\\)\">\\(C^*\\)<\/span>-algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is a complex-linear map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta:A\\to A\\)\">\\(\\delta:A\\to A\\)<\/span> satisfying\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta(xy)=\\delta(x)y+x\\delta(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in A).\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta(xy)=\\delta(x)y+x\\delta(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in A).\n\\]<\/span>\nNo continuity is included in this definition.<\/p>\n<p>Every derivation of every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*\\)\">\\(C^*\\)<\/span>-algebra is bounded. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is a von Neumann algebra and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta:M\\to M\\)\">\\(\\delta:M\\to M\\)<\/span> is a derivation, then there exists <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M\\)\">\\(a\\in M\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta(x)=ax-xa\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\qquad \\|a\\|\\le 2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta(x)=ax-xa\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\qquad \\|a\\|\\le 2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nTwo implementers in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> differ by an element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(M)\\)\">\\(Z(M)\\)<\/span>, and adding an element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(M)\\)\">\\(Z(M)\\)<\/span> to an implementer does not change the derivation. Consequently every implementer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in M\\)\">\\(b\\in M\\)<\/span> satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,Z(M))\\le 2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,Z(M))\\le 2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nIf, in addition,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta(x^*)=\\delta(x)^*\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta(x^*)=\\delta(x)^*\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]<\/span>\nthen one can choose\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\na=ih,\\qquad h=h^*\\in M,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\na=ih,\\qquad h=h^*\\in M,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nIn this case every implementer in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> has distance at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\delta\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\delta\\|\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(M)\\)\">\\(Z(M)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>There is no separability, countable decomposability, factor, type, or trace hypothesis. These are bounds furnished by the proof; no optimality assertion is made.<\/p>\n<h2>Proof<\/h2>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_h=\\{x\\in A:x=x^*\\}\\)\">\\(A_h=\\{x\\in A:x=x^*\\}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U(A)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U(A)\\)<\/span> for the unitary group of a unital algebra, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([a,x]=ax-xa\\)\">\\([a,x]=ax-xa\\)<\/span>. A derivation preserving the involution is called a <em>-derivation. For a concrete von Neumann algebra, weak-<\/em> means its <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weak topology. Central sums are indexed by arbitrary sets and are limits of finite partial sums. The lesson abbreviations used in citations are expanded, with result numbers, under <strong>Results used<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>The zero algebra is immediate, with implementer zero. All algebras represented on Hilbert spaces below act nondegenerately.<\/p>\n<h3>1. Automatic continuity<\/h3>\n<p>*<em>1.1. Algebraic reduction to <em>-derivations.<\/em><\/em> For an arbitrary derivation define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta^\\#(x)=\\delta(x^*)^*,\\qquad\n\\delta_1=\\frac{\\delta+\\delta^\\#}{2},\\qquad\n\\delta_2=\\frac{\\delta-\\delta^\\#}{2i}.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta^\\#(x)=\\delta(x^*)^*,\\qquad\n\\delta_1=\\frac{\\delta+\\delta^\\#}{2},\\qquad\n\\delta_2=\\frac{\\delta-\\delta^\\#}{2i}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta^\\#\\)\">\\(\\delta^\\#\\)<\/span> is complex-linear. Moreover,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta^\\#(xy)\n=\\bigl(\\delta(y^*)x^*+y^*\\delta(x^*)\\bigr)^*\n=x\\delta^\\#(y)+\\delta^\\#(x)y.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta^\\#(xy)\n=\\bigl(\\delta(y^*)x^*+y^*\\delta(x^*)\\bigr)^*\n=x\\delta^\\#(y)+\\delta^\\#(x)y.\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\)\">\\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\)<\/span> are derivations, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta_1(x^*)=\\frac{\\delta(x^*)+\\delta(x)^*}{2}=\\delta_1(x)^*,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_2(x^*)=\\frac{\\delta(x^*)-\\delta(x)^*}{2i}=\\delta_2(x)^*.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta_1(x^*)=\\frac{\\delta(x^*)+\\delta(x)^*}{2}=\\delta_1(x)^*,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_2(x^*)=\\frac{\\delta(x^*)-\\delta(x)^*}{2i}=\\delta_2(x)^*.\n\\]<\/span>\nMoreover,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2.\n\\]<\/span>\nThese assertions are algebraic and do not assume continuity. Once boundedness is established, they also give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|\\delta^\\#\\|=\\|\\delta\\|,\n\\qquad \\|\\delta_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\\quad(j=1,2).\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|\\delta^\\#\\|=\\|\\delta\\|,\n\\qquad \\|\\delta_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\\quad(j=1,2).\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>It suffices to treat a unital algebra. Indeed, represent a nonunital <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> faithfully, using <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.html#faithful-representations-of-c--algebras\">GNS, Theorem 7.2<\/a>. The algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A+\\mathbb C1\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(A+\\mathbb C1\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span> is closed: a finite-dimensional extension of a closed subspace of a Banach space is closed. The <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#OA-FND-INNER-CLOSEDNESS\">closedness bridge below<\/a> proves this implication explicitly. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\notin A\\)\">\\(1\\notin A\\)<\/span>, the expression <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x+\\lambda1\\)\">\\(x+\\lambda1\\)<\/span> is unique, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\widetilde\\delta(x+\\lambda1)=\\delta(x)\n\\]\">\\[\n\\widetilde\\delta(x+\\lambda1)=\\delta(x)\n\\]<\/span>\ndefines a derivation: both sides of its product rule at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x+\\lambda1,y+\\mu1\\)\">\\(x+\\lambda1,y+\\mu1\\)<\/span> equal\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta(x)y+x\\delta(y)+\\lambda\\delta(y)+\\mu\\delta(x).\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta(x)y+x\\delta(y)+\\lambda\\delta(y)+\\mu\\delta(x).\n\\]<\/span>\nIt preserves the involution when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span> does, since\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde\\delta((x+\\lambda1)^*)=\\delta(x^*)=\\delta(x)^*\\)\">\\(\\widetilde\\delta((x+\\lambda1)^*)=\\delta(x^*)=\\delta(x)^*\\)<\/span>.\nBoundedness of this extension implies boundedness on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. In a unital algebra every derivation satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta(1)=0\\)\">\\(\\delta(1)=0\\)<\/span>, by applying the product rule to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\cdot1\\)\">\\(1\\cdot1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>1.2. A state calculation.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> be any derivation of a unital <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*\\)\">\\(C^*\\)<\/span>-algebra, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=b^*\\)\">\\(b=b^*\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> be a state whose restriction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*(1,b)\\)\">\\(C^*(1,b)\\)<\/span> is a character. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\varphi(D(b))=0.\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\varphi(D(b))=0.\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nTo prove this, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=b-\\varphi(b)1\\)\">\\(c=b-\\varphi(b)1\\)<\/span>. Continuous functional calculus <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-07\">CF, Theorem 5.1<\/a> gives positive elements\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ns_+=(c_+)^{1/2},\\qquad s_-=(c_-)^{1/2},\n\\qquad c=s_+^2-s_-^2\n\\]\">\\[\ns_+=(c_+)^{1/2},\\qquad s_-=(c_-)^{1/2},\n\\qquad c=s_+^2-s_-^2\n\\]<\/span>\nin <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*(1,b)\\)\">\\(C^*(1,b)\\)<\/span>. The character takes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> to zero and therefore takes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_+^2,s_-^2\\)\">\\(s_+^2,s_-^2\\)<\/span> to zero. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(s_\\pm^2)=0\\)\">\\(\\varphi(s_\\pm^2)=0\\)<\/span>. The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality for a positive functional <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.html#OA-FND-GN-03\">GNS, Proposition 3.2<\/a>, in the form\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n|\\varphi(rs)|^2\\le\\varphi(rr^*)\\varphi(s^*s),\n\\]\">\\[\n|\\varphi(rs)|^2\\le\\varphi(rr^*)\\varphi(s^*s),\n\\]<\/span>\nshows that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\varphi(D(s_\\pm)s_\\pm)=\\varphi(s_\\pm D(s_\\pm))=0.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\varphi(D(s_\\pm)s_\\pm)=\\varphi(s_\\pm D(s_\\pm))=0.\n\\]<\/span>\nApplying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=s_+^2-s_-^2\\)\">\\(c=s_+^2-s_-^2\\)<\/span>, and using <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(1)=0\\)\">\\(D(1)=0\\)<\/span>, proves (1.2).<\/p>\n<p><strong>1.3. Closed graph.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> be a *-derivation. Suppose that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx_n\\in A_h,\\qquad x_n\\longrightarrow0,\\qquad D(x_n)\\longrightarrow b\\in A_h\n\\]\">\\[\nx_n\\in A_h,\\qquad x_n\\longrightarrow0,\\qquad D(x_n)\\longrightarrow b\\in A_h\n\\]<\/span>\nin norm. We prove <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=0\\)\">\\(b=0\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\ne0\\)\">\\(b\\ne0\\)<\/span>, its spectrum contains a nonzero real number <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span>. Replacing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_n,b\\)\">\\(x_n,b\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_n/\\lambda,b/\\lambda\\)\">\\(x_n/\\lambda,b/\\lambda\\)<\/span>, we can assume <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\in\\sigma(b)\\)\">\\(1\\in\\sigma(b)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a continuous real function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma(b)\\)\">\\(\\sigma(b)\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n0\\le f\\le1,\\qquad f(1)=1,\\qquad f(t)=0\\text{ for }t\\le\\tfrac12.\n\\]\">\\[\n0\\le f\\le1,\\qquad f(1)=1,\\qquad f(t)=0\\text{ for }t\\le\\tfrac12.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=f(b)\\)\">\\(v=f(b)\\)<\/span>, functional calculus gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n0\\le v\\le1,\\qquad \\|v\\|=1,\\qquad vbv\\ge\\tfrac12v^2.\n\\]\">\\[\n0\\le v\\le1,\\qquad \\|v\\|=1,\\qquad vbv\\ge\\tfrac12v^2.\n\\]<\/span>\nAfter discarding finitely many terms, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_n=\\|x_n\\|&gt;0\\)\">\\(r_n=\\|x_n\\|&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ny_n=x_n+3r_n1,\\qquad b_n=vy_nv,\\qquad t_n=\\frac{b_n}{4r_n}.\n\\]\">\\[\ny_n=x_n+3r_n1,\\qquad b_n=vy_nv,\\qquad t_n=\\frac{b_n}{4r_n}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThen\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n2r_n1\\le y_n\\le4r_n1,\n\\qquad \\tfrac12v^2\\le t_n\\le v^2,\n\\qquad \\|t_n\\|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\tag{1.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n2r_n1\\le y_n\\le4r_n1,\n\\qquad \\tfrac12v^2\\le t_n\\le v^2,\n\\qquad \\|t_n\\|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\tag{1.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe product rule, without any continuity assumption on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(b_n)=D(v)y_nv+vD(x_n)v+vy_nD(v)\\longrightarrow vbv.\n\\tag{1.4}\n\\]\">\\[\nD(b_n)=D(v)y_nv+vD(x_n)v+vy_nD(v)\\longrightarrow vbv.\n\\tag{1.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(v)\\)\">\\(D(v)\\)<\/span> is one fixed element, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|y_n\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|y_n\\|\\to0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Evaluation at the spectral point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|b_n\\|\\)\">\\(\\|b_n\\|\\)<\/span> is a character of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*(1,b_n)\\)\">\\(C^*(1,b_n)\\)<\/span>. Extend it to a state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi_n\\)\">\\(\\varphi_n\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, using <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.html#faithful-representations-of-c--algebras\">GNS, Proposition 7.1a<\/a>. Equation (1.2) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi_n(D(b_n))=0\\)\">\\(\\varphi_n(D(b_n))=0\\)<\/span>, while\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\varphi_n(t_n)=\\|t_n\\|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\varphi_n(t_n)=\\|t_n\\|\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently, for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\frac14\n\\le\\frac12\\varphi_n(t_n)\n\\le\\varphi_n(vbv)\n=\\varphi_n(vbv-D(b_n))\n\\le\\|vbv-D(b_n)\\|\n&lt;\\frac18,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\frac14\n\\le\\frac12\\varphi_n(t_n)\n\\le\\varphi_n(vbv)\n=\\varphi_n(vbv-D(b_n))\n\\le\\|vbv-D(b_n)\\|\n&lt;\\frac18,\n\\]<\/span>\na contradiction.<\/p>\n<p>The graph of the real-linear map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D:A_h\\to A_h\\)\">\\(D:A_h\\to A_h\\)<\/span> is therefore closed: a convergent graph sequence is reduced to the preceding case by subtracting its limiting first coordinate. The real Closed Graph Theorem makes this restriction bounded. Every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in A\\)\">\\(x\\in A\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=h+ik\\)\">\\(x=h+ik\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h,k\\in A_h\\)\">\\(h,k\\in A_h\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h\\|,\\|k\\|\\le\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|h\\|,\\|k\\|\\le\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>; hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is bounded on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. Applying this to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\)\">\\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\)<\/span> proves automatic continuity for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The spectral-cut argument above is the automatic-continuity argument of [S60, unnumbered theorem, pp. 31–32], with the algebraic *-splitting and the nonunital reduction included explicitly.<\/p>\n<h3>2. The centre and uniformly bounded central sums<\/h3>\n<p>For a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> and any derivation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(z)=D(z)z+zD(z)=2zD(z).\n\\]\">\\[\nD(z)=D(z)z+zD(z)=2zD(z).\n\\]<\/span>\nMultiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> first gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(zD(z)=0\\)\">\\(zD(z)=0\\)<\/span>, and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(z)=0\\)\">\\(D(z)=0\\)<\/span>. For a bounded derivation on a von Neumann algebra, spectral approximation of central self-adjoint elements by finite linear combinations of central projections gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(c)=0\\quad(c\\in Z(M)),\\qquad D(zx)=zD(x).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\">\\[\nD(c)=0\\quad(c\\in Z(M)),\\qquad D(zx)=zD(x).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe approximation follows from <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-spectral-theorem-for-bounded-self-adjoint-operators.html#OA-FND-ST-03\">ST, Theorem 3.1 and Proposition 5.1<\/a>. In particular, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> restricts to each central summand <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Mz\\)\">\\(Mz\\)<\/span>, with norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D\\|\\)\">\\(\\|D\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>We record the precise summation statement. Suppose that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((z_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((z_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> are pairwise orthogonal central projections with strong sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, and that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\na_i\\in Mz_i,\\qquad \\|a_i\\|\\le C,\\qquad\nD(x)=[a_i,x]\\quad(x\\in Mz_i).\n\\]\">\\[\na_i\\in Mz_i,\\qquad \\|a_i\\|\\le C,\\qquad\nD(x)=[a_i,x]\\quad(x\\in Mz_i).\n\\]<\/span>\nFor finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\subset I\\)\">\\(F\\subset I\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i\\)\">\\(a_F=\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i\\)<\/span>. Since the ranges of the summands are in orthogonal subspaces,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|a_F\\xi\\|^2\n=\\sum_{i\\in F}\\|a_i z_i\\xi\\|^2\n\\le C^2\\sum_{i\\in F}\\|z_i\\xi\\|^2\n\\le C^2\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|a_F\\xi\\|^2\n=\\sum_{i\\in F}\\|a_i z_i\\xi\\|^2\n\\le C^2\\sum_{i\\in F}\\|z_i\\xi\\|^2\n\\le C^2\\|\\xi\\|^2.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe same estimate for differences of partial sums proves strong convergence to an operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. Strong closedness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M\\)\">\\(a\\in M\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|a\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(az_i=a_i\\)\">\\(az_i=a_i\\)<\/span>. If all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_i\\)\">\\(a_i\\)<\/span> are self-adjoint, or all are skew-adjoint, the same property holds for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>, since the adjoint relation passes to matrix coefficients.<\/p>\n<p>For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in M\\)\">\\(x\\in M\\)<\/span>, (2.1) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\bigl(D(x)-[a,x]\\bigr)z_i\n=D(xz_i)-[a_i,xz_i]=0.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\bigl(D(x)-[a,x]\\bigr)z_i\n=D(xz_i)-[a_i,xz_i]=0.\n\\]<\/span>\nTaking the strong sum of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_i\\)\">\\(z_i\\)<\/span> yields <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(x)=[a,x]\\)\">\\(D(x)=[a,x]\\)<\/span>. This argument never applies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> to a strong limit.<\/p>\n<p>A *-isomorphism transports derivations and their implementers, preserves their norms, and preserves self-adjointness. Isometry of *-isomorphisms is <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-12\">CF, Corollary 4.6<\/a>. Between von Neumann algebras such an isomorphism is also normal, by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18\">UE, Corollary 11.4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h3>3. Normal extension without a continuity assumption on the representation<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(A\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span> be a nondegenerate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*\\)\">\\(C^*\\)<\/span>-algebra and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D:A\\to A\\)\">\\(D:A\\to A\\)<\/span> a bounded derivation. We show that it has a unique normal extension\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\overline D:A&#x27;&#x27;\\longrightarrow A&#x27;&#x27;,\\qquad\n\\|\\overline D\\|=\\|D\\|.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\overline D:A&#x27;&#x27;\\longrightarrow A&#x27;&#x27;,\\qquad\n\\|\\overline D\\|=\\|D\\|.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is a *-derivation, so is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline D\\)\">\\(\\overline D\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Identify <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^{**}\\)\">\\(A^{**}\\)<\/span> with its enveloping von Neumann algebra by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-03\">UE, Theorem 3.3<\/a>, and write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j:A\\to A^{**}\\)\">\\(j:A\\to A^{**}\\)<\/span> for the canonical embedding. The second adjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D^{**}\\)\">\\(D^{**}\\)<\/span> is weak-* continuous and has norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D\\|\\)\">\\(\\|D\\|\\)<\/span> <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-05\">WT, Proposition 5.3<\/a>. It is a derivation. Here is the required verification of the product rule.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\in A^{**}\\)\">\\(Y\\in A^{**}\\)<\/span>, choose a net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_\\beta\\in A\\)\">\\(b_\\beta\\in A\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(b_\\beta)\\to Y\\)\">\\(j(b_\\beta)\\to Y\\)<\/span> weak-<em>, using <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-05\">WT, Theorem 5.2<\/a>. Passing to the limit in the product rule for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ab_\\beta\\)\">\\(ab_\\beta\\)<\/span>, by separate weak-<\/em> continuity of multiplication, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD^{**}(j(a)Y)=j(D(a))Y+j(a)D^{**}(Y).\n\\]\">\\[\nD^{**}(j(a)Y)=j(D(a))Y+j(a)D^{**}(Y).\n\\]<\/span>\nNow choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(a_\\alpha)\\to X\\)\">\\(j(a_\\alpha)\\to X\\)<\/span> weak-* and pass to the limit in this identity, keeping <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> fixed. It follows that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD^{**}(XY)=D^{**}(X)Y+XD^{**}(Y).\n\\]\">\\[\nD^{**}(XY)=D^{**}(X)Y+XD^{**}(Y).\n\\]<\/span>\nOnly one variable is allowed to tend to its limit at each passage. The involution is weak-* continuous, so the same density argument preserves the *-condition.<\/p>\n<p>By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-02\">UE, Lemma 2.1(2)–(4)<\/a>, the given representation extends to a normal surjection\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nq:A^{**}\\longrightarrow A&#x27;&#x27;\n\\]\">\\[\nq:A^{**}\\longrightarrow A&#x27;&#x27;\n\\]<\/span>\nwhose kernel is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^{**}(1-z)\\)\">\\(A^{**}(1-z)\\)<\/span> for a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>. Its restriction\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\theta:A^{**}z\\longrightarrow A&#x27;&#x27;\n\\]\">\\[\n\\theta:A^{**}z\\longrightarrow A&#x27;&#x27;\n\\]<\/span>\nis an isometric <em>-isomorphism, normal in both directions. Step 2's algebraic calculation gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D^{**}(z)=0\\)\">\\(D^{**}(z)=0\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D^{**}\\)\">\\(D^{**}\\)<\/span> preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^{**}z\\)\">\\(A^{**}z\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\overline D=\\theta\\circ\\bigl(D^{**}|_{A^{**}z}\\bigr)\\circ\\theta^{-1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\overline D=\\theta\\circ\\bigl(D^{**}|_{A^{**}z}\\bigr)\\circ\\theta^{-1}\n\\]<\/span>\nis a normal derivation of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D\\|\\)\">\\(\\|D\\|\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in A\\)\">\\(a\\in A\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\theta^{-1}(a)=j(a)z,\n\\qquad D^{**}(j(a)z)=j(D(a))z,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\theta^{-1}(a)=j(a)z,\n\\qquad D^{**}(j(a)z)=j(D(a))z,\n\\]<\/span>\nso <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline D(a)=D(a)\\)\">\\(\\overline D(a)=D(a)\\)<\/span>. This also proves the reverse norm inequality. Uniqueness follows from weak-<\/em> density of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(A&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-06\">DC, Theorem 4.4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>In particular, on taking <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=M=M&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(A=M=M&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, every bounded derivation of a von Neumann algebra is normal. The topology facts used here are <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-09\">OT, Proposition 8.6 and Theorem 9.4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h3>4. Type I algebras: finite averaging with a uniform bound<\/h3>\n<p><strong>4.1. Finite-dimensional averaging.<\/strong> Every finite-dimensional unital <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*\\)\">\\(C^*\\)<\/span>-algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> has a finite subgroup of its unitary group whose complex linear span is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>After a faithful unital representation, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> is a von Neumann algebra: a finite-dimensional subspace is closed for the Hausdorff <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weak topology. By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-13\">UE, Lemma 6.3<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> is therefore a finite direct sum of full matrix algebras. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^n\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^n\\)<\/span>, with indices modulo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nS e_j=e_{j+1},\\qquad T e_j=\\zeta^j e_j,\n\\qquad \\zeta=e^{2\\pi i/n}.\n\\]\">\\[\nS e_j=e_{j+1},\\qquad T e_j=\\zeta^j e_j,\n\\qquad \\zeta=e^{2\\pi i/n}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe group generated by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S,T,-1\\)\">\\(S,T,-1\\)<\/span> is finite, because every word has the form <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pm\\zeta^kS^rT^s\\)\">\\(\\pm\\zeta^kS^rT^s\\)<\/span>, with exponents taken modulo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. Its span contains\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nE_{jj}=\\frac1n\\sum_{s=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{-js}T^s,\n\\qquad E_{ij}=S^{i-j}E_{jj},\n\\]\">\\[\nE_{jj}=\\frac1n\\sum_{s=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{-js}T^s,\n\\qquad E_{ij}=S^{i-j}E_{jj},\n\\]<\/span>\nand thus all matrices. For a direct sum, take the product of these groups. Its span is an algebra and contains the central projection of each block: this projection is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((1-g)/2\\)\">\\((1-g)/2\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-1\\)\">\\(-1\\)<\/span> on that block and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> elsewhere. Multiplying by these projections isolates the matrix spans of all blocks.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\subseteq P\\)\">\\(F\\subseteq P\\)<\/span> be a finite-dimensional unital subalgebra of a von Neumann algebra, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> be a bounded derivation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. Choose such a finite group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\subseteq\\mathcal U(F)\\)\">\\(G\\subseteq\\mathcal U(F)\\)<\/span>, and set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nb(u)=D(u)u^*,\\qquad a_G=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_{u\\in G}b(u).\n\\]\">\\[\nb(u)=D(u)u^*,\\qquad a_G=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_{u\\in G}b(u).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe product rule gives the cocycle identity\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nb(vu)=b(v)+vb(u)v^*.\n\\]\">\\[\nb(vu)=b(v)+vb(u)v^*.\n\\]<\/span>\nAveraging over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in G\\)\">\\(u\\in G\\)<\/span> yields\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\na_G=b(v)+va_Gv^*,\\qquad D(v)=a_Gv-va_G\\quad(v\\in G).\n\\]\">\\[\na_G=b(v)+va_Gv^*,\\qquad D(v)=a_Gv-va_G\\quad(v\\in G).\n\\]<\/span>\nSince <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> spans <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD|_F=[a_G,\\cdot]|_F,\n\\qquad \\|a_G\\|\\le\\|D\\|.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\">\\[\nD|_F=[a_G,\\cdot]|_F,\n\\qquad \\|a_G\\|\\le\\|D\\|.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor a *-derivation,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nb(u)^*=uD(u^*)=-D(u)u^*=-b(u),\n\\]\">\\[\nb(u)^*=uD(u^*)=-D(u)u^*=-b(u),\n\\]<\/span>\nso <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_G\\)\">\\(a_G\\)<\/span> is skew-adjoint.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4.2. Homogeneous type I algebras.<\/strong> Suppose first that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> is homogeneous of type I. By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-14\">PT, Theorem 10.3, Proposition 8.4 and Proposition 9.1<\/a>, it has the form\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nP\\cong B\\,\\bar\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2(I)),\\qquad B\\text{ abelian},\n\\]\">\\[\nP\\cong B\\,\\bar\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2(I)),\\qquad B\\text{ abelian},\n\\]<\/span>\nwith matrix units <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_{ij}\\)\">\\(w_{ij}\\)<\/span>. Every finite matrix compression has an expression\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ne_Jxe_J=\\sum_{i,j\\in J}c_{ij}w_{ij},\n\\qquad e_J=\\sum_{i\\in J}w_{ii},\\qquad c_{ij}\\in Z(P),\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\">\\[\ne_Jxe_J=\\sum_{i,j\\in J}c_{ij}w_{ij},\n\\qquad e_J=\\sum_{i\\in J}w_{ii},\\qquad c_{ij}\\in Z(P),\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\subset I\\)\">\\(J\\subset I\\)<\/span> is finite and nonempty.<\/p>\n<p>The finite-dimensional unital algebras\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nF_J=\\operatorname{span}\\{w_{ij}:i,j\\in J\\}+\\mathbb C(1-e_J)\n\\]\">\\[\nF_J=\\operatorname{span}\\{w_{ij}:i,j\\in J\\}+\\mathbb C(1-e_J)\n\\]<\/span>\nform an increasing net; the zero last summand is omitted when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_J=1\\)\">\\(e_J=1\\)<\/span>. By (4.1), for a <em>-derivation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> there is a skew-adjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_J\\in P\\)\">\\(a_J\\in P\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|a_J\\|\\le\\|D\\|,\\qquad D|_{F_J}=[a_J,\\cdot]|_{F_J}.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|a_J\\|\\le\\|D\\|,\\qquad D|_{F_J}=[a_J,\\cdot]|_{F_J}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe weak-<\/em> compact ball has a cluster point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>, still skew-adjoint and of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D\\|\\)\">\\(\\|D\\|\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i,j\\)\">\\(i,j\\)<\/span>, the identity for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w_{ij}\\)\">\\(w_{ij}\\)<\/span> holds eventually along the net, and separate weak-* continuity of multiplication gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(w_{ij})=[a,w_{ij}].\n\\]\">\\[\nD(w_{ij})=[a,w_{ij}].\n\\]<\/span>\nStep 2 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(c)=0\\)\">\\(D(c)=0\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\in Z(P)\\)\">\\(c\\in Z(P)\\)<\/span>. Hence (4.2) implies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(e_Jxe_J)=[a,e_Jxe_J].\n\\]\">\\[\nD(e_Jxe_J)=[a,e_Jxe_J].\n\\]<\/span>\nThe projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_J\\)\">\\(e_J\\)<\/span> increase strongly to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>; consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_Jxe_J\\to x\\)\">\\(e_Jxe_J\\to x\\)<\/span> strongly, with norms bounded by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. Bounded strong convergence implies weak-* convergence <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-09\">OT, Lemma 8.5<\/a>. By Step 3 and separate normality of multiplication, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(x)=[a,x]\\)\">\\(D(x)=[a,x]\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4.3. Arbitrary type I algebras.<\/strong> Decompose a type I algebra into its homogeneous central summands by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-14\">PT, Theorem 10.3<\/a>. Apply 4.2 on each summand and combine the implementers by Step 2. Setting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=-ia\\)\">\\(h=-ia\\)<\/span> for the combined skew-adjoint implementer gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(x)=i[h,x],\\qquad h=h^*\\in P,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le\\|D\\|\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\">\\[\nD(x)=i[h,x],\\qquad h=h^*\\in P,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le\\|D\\|\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nfor every *-derivation of every type I von Neumann algebra. There is no restriction on the cardinalities of the homogeneous summands.<\/p>\n<h3>5. Spatiality with the same norm bound<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D:M\\to M\\)\">\\(D:M\\to M\\)<\/span> be a *-derivation with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|D\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>. We prove that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(x)=i[h,x]\\quad(x\\in M)\n\\]\">\\[\nD(x)=i[h,x]\\quad(x\\in M)\n\\]<\/span>\nfor some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=h^*\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(h=h^*\\in B(H)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a maximal abelian von Neumann subalgebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\subseteq M&#x27;\\)\">\\(B\\subseteq M&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Existence follows from Zorn's lemma: the von Neumann algebra generated by the union of a chain of abelian unital algebras is abelian. Maximality gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nM&#x27;\\cap B&#x27;=B.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\">\\[\nM&#x27;\\cap B&#x27;=B.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed, a self-adjoint element of the left side can be adjoined to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> while preserving commutativity; applying this to the real and imaginary parts proves (5.1).<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A_0\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A_0\\)<\/span> be the linear span of products <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xe\\)\">\\(xe\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in M\\)\">\\(x\\in M\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> a projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. Any finite family of projections in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> can be refined into pairwise orthogonal Boolean atoms whose sum is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. Thus every element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A_0\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A_0\\)<\/span> can be written\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nT=\\sum_{j=1}^n x_je_j,\n\\qquad e_je_k=0\\ (j\\ne k),\\qquad\\sum_j e_j=1.\n\\]\">\\[\nT=\\sum_{j=1}^n x_je_j,\n\\qquad e_je_k=0\\ (j\\ne k),\\qquad\\sum_j e_j=1.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\in\\mathcal P(B)\\)\">\\(e\\in\\mathcal P(B)\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=c_{M&#x27;}(e)\\)\">\\(z=c_{M&#x27;}(e)\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z(M)\\)\">\\(z\\in Z(M)\\)<\/span>, and the induction map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nMz\\longrightarrow Me\\subseteq B(eH),\\qquad xz\\longmapsto xe,\n\\]\">\\[\nMz\\longrightarrow Me\\subseteq B(eH),\\qquad xz\\longmapsto xe,\n\\]<\/span>\nis an isometric *-isomorphism <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04\">DC, Theorem 5.8; PT, Proposition 3.5(3)<\/a>. By (2.1),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|D(x)e\\|=\\|D(x)z\\|=\\|D(xz)\\|\n\\le C\\|xz\\|=C\\|xe\\|.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|D(x)e\\|=\\|D(x)z\\|=\\|D(xz)\\|\n\\le C\\|xz\\|=C\\|xe\\|.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nDefine\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nE(T)=\\sum_{j=1}^nD(x_j)e_j.\n\\]\">\\[\nE(T)=\\sum_{j=1}^nD(x_j)e_j.\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=0\\)\">\\(T=0\\)<\/span>, multiplying by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_j\\)\">\\(e_j\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_je_j=0\\)\">\\(x_je_j=0\\)<\/span>, and (5.2) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(x_j)e_j=0\\)\">\\(D(x_j)e_j=0\\)<\/span>. Refining two expressions to common Boolean atoms therefore proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is well-defined. The orthogonal block decomposition gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|E(T)\\|=\\max_j\\|D(x_j)e_j\\|\n\\le C\\max_j\\|x_je_j\\|=C\\|T\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|E(T)\\|=\\max_j\\|D(x_j)e_j\\|\n\\le C\\max_j\\|x_je_j\\|=C\\|T\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nProducts of generators satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((xe)(yf)=xyef\\)\">\\((xe)(yf)=xyef\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e,f\\)\">\\(e,f\\)<\/span> commute with all elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, including their derivatives. The product rule for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> therefore proves the product rule for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>. The same calculation with adjoints proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is a *-derivation.<\/p>\n<p>Extend <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> by norm continuity to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A=\\overline{\\mathcal A_0}^{\\|\\cdot\\|}\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A=\\overline{\\mathcal A_0}^{\\|\\cdot\\|}\\)<\/span>, then normally to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span> by Step 3. Its norm remains at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Spectral approximation shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A\\)<\/span> contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>; hence\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;=(M\\cup B)&#x27;&#x27;,\\qquad (\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;)&#x27;=M&#x27;\\cap B&#x27;=B.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;=(M\\cup B)&#x27;&#x27;,\\qquad (\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;)&#x27;=M&#x27;\\cap B&#x27;=B.\n\\]<\/span>\nAn algebra with abelian commutant is type I <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-15\">PT, Proposition 11.2<\/a>. Step 4 supplies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=h^*\\in\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(h=h^*\\in\\mathcal A&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>, implementing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, and therefore implementing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. This is the spatial construction underlying [K66, Theorem 4], with its well-definedness and norm estimate supplied by (5.2).<\/p>\n<p>Two consequences will be used repeatedly. First, (2.1) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n[h,z]=0\\qquad(z\\in Z(M)).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n[h,z]=0\\qquad(z\\in Z(M)).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nSecond, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([h,N]\\subseteq N\\)\">\\([h,N]\\subseteq N\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in N,x\\in M\\)\">\\(y\\in N,x\\in M\\)<\/span>, direct expansion gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n[[h,y],x]=[[h,x],y]=0,\n\\]\">\\[\n[[h,y],x]=[[h,x],y]=0,\n\\]<\/span>\nsince <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([h,x]\\in M\\)\">\\([h,x]\\in M\\)<\/span>. Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nuhu^*-h\\in N\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(N)).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\">\\[\nuhu^*-h\\in N\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(N)).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nReplacing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h+t\\)\">\\(h+t\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in N_h\\)\">\\(t\\in N_h\\)<\/span> does not change its commutator with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h3>6. A fixed-point theorem for linear isometries<\/h3>\n<p>We need the following result, including its proof.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a real Banach space, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subseteq E\\)\">\\(K\\subseteq E\\)<\/span> a nonempty weakly compact convex set, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> a group of surjective linear isometries of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gK=K\\)\">\\(gK=K\\)<\/span>. Then some point of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is fixed by every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\in G\\)\">\\(g\\in G\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.1. One bounded linear map.<\/strong> If a bounded linear map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F:E\\to E\\)\">\\(F:E\\to E\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(K)\\subseteq K\\)\">\\(F(K)\\subseteq K\\)<\/span>, fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_0\\in K\\)\">\\(x_0\\in K\\)<\/span> and put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^jx_0.\n\\]\">\\[\nx_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^jx_0.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is norm bounded: weak compactness gives pointwise boundedness on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E^*\\)\">\\(E^*\\)<\/span>, and the Uniform Boundedness Theorem applies to the canonical images in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E^{**}\\)\">\\(E^{**}\\)<\/span>. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(R=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx_n\\in K,\\qquad\n\\|Fx_n-x_n\\|=\\frac1n\\|F^nx_0-x_0\\|\\le\\frac{2R}{n}.\n\\]\">\\[\nx_n\\in K,\\qquad\n\\|Fx_n-x_n\\|=\\frac1n\\|F^nx_0-x_0\\|\\le\\frac{2R}{n}.\n\\]<\/span>\nA weakly convergent subnet has limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in K\\)\">\\(x\\in K\\)<\/span>. Bounded linear maps are weakly continuous, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Fx=x\\)\">\\(Fx=x\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.2. Small caps in a separable space.<\/strong> Suppose that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is separable and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> has more than one point. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>, there is a proper weakly compact convex subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)\">\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(K\\setminus K_0)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(K\\setminus K_0)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere and below closures in this substep are weak closures.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L=\\overline{\\operatorname{Ext}K}\\)\">\\(L=\\overline{\\operatorname{Ext}K}\\)<\/span>. By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06\">WT, Theorem 6.1<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> is nonempty and compact and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}L\\)\">\\(K=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}L\\)<\/span>. Separability provides a countable cover of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> by closed norm balls of radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon/3\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon/3\\)<\/span>. Such balls are weakly closed, by Hahn–Banach separation. The Baire theorem for compact Hausdorff spaces, applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, gives a ball <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_0\\)\">\\(B_0\\)<\/span> for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\cap B_0\\)\">\\(L\\cap B_0\\)<\/span> contains a nonempty relatively weakly open subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\setminus U\\)\">\\(L\\setminus U\\)<\/span> is empty, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subseteq B_0\\)\">\\(K\\subseteq B_0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{diam}K\\le2\\varepsilon/3\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{diam}K\\le2\\varepsilon/3\\)<\/span>. Any singleton in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is a proper subset satisfying (6.1).<\/p>\n<p>Otherwise put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK_1=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\setminus U),\\qquad\nK_2=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\cap B_0).\n\\]\">\\[\nK_1=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\setminus U),\\qquad\nK_2=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\cap B_0).\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth are nonempty compact convex subsets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_2\\subseteq B_0\\)\">\\(K_2\\subseteq B_0\\)<\/span>. The convex join\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1\\}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1\\}\n\\]<\/span>\nis compact, convex, and contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>; it is therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in\\operatorname{Ext}K\\cap U\\)\">\\(y\\in\\operatorname{Ext}K\\cap U\\)<\/span>, which exists because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ext}K\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ext}K\\)<\/span> is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>. We have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\notin K_1\\)\">\\(y\\notin K_1\\)<\/span>. Indeed, otherwise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span> would be extreme in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_1\\)\">\\(K_1\\)<\/span>, and the converse to Krein–Milman <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06\">WT, Theorem 6.2<\/a>, applied to the compact set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\setminus U\\)\">\\(L\\setminus U\\)<\/span>, would give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in L\\setminus U\\)\">\\(y\\in L\\setminus U\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK_\\eta=\\eta K_1+(1-\\eta)K\n=\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ \\eta\\le t\\le1\\}.\n\\]\">\\[\nK_\\eta=\\eta K_1+(1-\\eta)K\n=\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ \\eta\\le t\\le1\\}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe equality follows by substituting the preceding description of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> into the first expression. This set is compact and convex. It does not contain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>: an expression <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=\\eta v+(1-\\eta)k\\)\">\\(y=\\eta v+(1-\\eta)k\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in K_1,k\\in K\\)\">\\(v\\in K_1,k\\in K\\)<\/span>, would force <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=y\\)\">\\(v=y\\)<\/span> by extremality.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,x&#x27;\\notin K_\\eta\\)\">\\(x,x&#x27;\\notin K_\\eta\\)<\/span>, write\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx=tv+(1-t)w,\\qquad x&#x27;=t&#x27;v&#x27;+(1-t&#x27;)w&#x27;\n\\]\">\\[\nx=tv+(1-t)w,\\qquad x&#x27;=t&#x27;v&#x27;+(1-t&#x27;)w&#x27;\n\\]<\/span>\nwith <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v,v&#x27;\\in K_1\\)\">\\(v,v&#x27;\\in K_1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w,w&#x27;\\in K_2\\)\">\\(w,w&#x27;\\in K_2\\)<\/span>. Necessarily <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t,t&#x27;&lt;\\eta\\)\">\\(t,t&#x27;&lt;\\eta\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R=\\sup_K\\|x\\|&gt;0\\)\">\\(R=\\sup_K\\|x\\|&gt;0\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x-x&#x27;\\|\n&amp;\\le t\\|v-w\\|+\\|w-w&#x27;\\|+t&#x27;\\|v&#x27;-w&#x27;\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le2R(t+t&#x27;)+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}\n\\le4R\\eta+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x-x&#x27;\\|\n&amp;\\le t\\|v-w\\|+\\|w-w&#x27;\\|+t&#x27;\\|v&#x27;-w&#x27;\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le2R(t+t&#x27;)+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}\n\\le4R\\eta+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]<\/span>\nChoose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta&lt;\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon/(12R)\\}\\)\">\\(\\eta&lt;\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon/(12R)\\}\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0=K_\\eta\\)\">\\(K_0=K_\\eta\\)<\/span> proves (6.1).<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.3. Finitely many isometries.<\/strong> Given <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\in G\\)\">\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\in G\\)<\/span>, apply 6.1 to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F=n^{-1}\\sum_jg_j\\)\">\\(F=n^{-1}\\sum_jg_j\\)<\/span>, obtaining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in K\\)\">\\(x\\in K\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ng_jx.\n\\]\">\\[\nx=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ng_jx.\n\\]<\/span>\nIf some, but not all, of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_j\\)\">\\(g_j\\)<\/span> fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, cancel their contributions and renumber. To obtain a contradiction, we may thus assume\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^mg_jx,\n\\qquad g_jx\\ne x\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\">\\[\nx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^mg_jx,\n\\qquad g_jx\\ne x\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_0\\)\">\\(G_0\\)<\/span> generated by these finitely many isometries is countable. Replace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> by the weakly closed convex hull of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_0x\\)\">\\(G_0x\\)<\/span>. This hull is a weakly compact subset of the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. It lies in the norm-closed linear span <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0\\)\">\\(E_0\\)<\/span> of the countable orbit: a norm-closed linear subspace is weakly closed by Hahn–Banach. The space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0\\)\">\\(E_0\\)<\/span> is separable, and its weak topology is the subspace weak topology, again by Hahn–Banach extension. Thus 6.2 applies.<\/p>\n<p>Choose\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}\\|g_jx-x\\|\n\\]\">\\[\n0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}\\|g_jx-x\\|\n\\]<\/span>\nand a set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)\">\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)<\/span> satisfying (6.1). Some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(hx\\)\">\\(hx\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in G_0\\)\">\\(h\\in G_0\\)<\/span>, lies outside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\)\">\\(K_0\\)<\/span>, since otherwise the closed convex hull of the orbit would be contained in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\)\">\\(K_0\\)<\/span>. Equation (6.2) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nhx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^m hg_jx.\n\\]\">\\[\nhx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^m hg_jx.\n\\]<\/span>\nConvexity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\)\">\\(K_0\\)<\/span> implies that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(hg_jx\\notin K_0\\)\">\\(hg_jx\\notin K_0\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>. But then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|g_jx-x\\|=\\|hg_jx-hx\\|\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|g_jx-x\\|=\\|hg_jx-hx\\|\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]<\/span>\ncontrary to the choice of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)<\/span>. Therefore the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is fixed by all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\)\">\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.4. An arbitrary group.<\/strong> For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, its fixed-point set in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is weakly closed. Substep 6.3 proves the finite intersection property for these sets. Compactness gives a point in their intersection. This proves the lemma.<\/p>\n<p>The fixed-point argument follows [L26, Theorem 1, Proposition 2 and Lemma 3].<\/p>\n<h3>7. Finite algebras have nonzero normal finite traces<\/h3>\n<p>We first prove the projection estimates needed to construct a trace.<\/p>\n<p><strong>7.1. Increasing comparison in a finite algebra.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> be a finite von Neumann algebra. If\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ne_n\\uparrow e,\\qquad e_n\\precsim f\\quad(n\\ge1),\n\\]\">\\[\ne_n\\uparrow e,\\qquad e_n\\precsim f\\quad(n\\ge1),\n\\]<\/span>\nthen <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\precsim f\\)\">\\(e\\precsim f\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Construct partial isometries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n\\)\">\\(v_n\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nv_n^*v_n=e_n,\\qquad v_nv_n^*\\le f,\\qquad v_{n+1}e_n=v_n.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\">\\[\nv_n^*v_n=e_n,\\qquad v_nv_n^*\\le f,\\qquad v_{n+1}e_n=v_n.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nChoose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_1\\)\">\\(v_1\\)<\/span> using <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_1\\precsim f\\)\">\\(e_1\\precsim f\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n\\)\">\\(v_n\\)<\/span> is chosen, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w^*w=e_{n+1}\\)\">\\(w^*w=e_{n+1}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ww^*\\le f\\)\">\\(ww^*\\le f\\)<\/span>. Put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\np=we_nw^*,\\qquad r=v_nv_n^*,\\qquad t=v_nw^*.\n\\]\">\\[\np=we_nw^*,\\qquad r=v_nv_n^*,\\qquad t=v_nw^*.\n\\]<\/span>\nThen <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t^*t=p\\)\">\\(t^*t=p\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(tt^*=r\\)\">\\(tt^*=r\\)<\/span>. The algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(fPf\\)\">\\(fPf\\)<\/span> is finite <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-09\">PT, Lemma 6.2<\/a>. Complement equivalence in this finite algebra <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-18\">PT, Proposition 14.2<\/a> gives a partial isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_0\\)\">\\(t_0\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f-p\\)\">\\(f-p\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f-r\\)\">\\(f-r\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u=t+t_0\\)\">\\(u=t+t_0\\)<\/span> is a unitary of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(fPf\\)\">\\(fPf\\)<\/span>. Define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_{n+1}=uw\\)\">\\(v_{n+1}=uw\\)<\/span>. It has the required initial and final projections, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nv_{n+1}e_n=tw e_n=v_nw^*we_n=v_n.\n\\]\">\\[\nv_{n+1}e_n=tw e_n=v_nw^*we_n=v_n.\n\\]<\/span>\nThis proves (7.1).<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\ge n\\)\">\\(m\\ge n\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nv_m-v_n=v_m(e_m-e_n),\\qquad\n\\|(v_m-v_n)\\xi\\|=\\|(e_m-e_n)\\xi\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\nv_m-v_n=v_m(e_m-e_n),\\qquad\n\\|(v_m-v_n)\\xi\\|=\\|(e_m-e_n)\\xi\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_n\\)\">\\(v_n\\)<\/span> converges strongly to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in P\\)\">\\(v\\in P\\)<\/span>. Moreover, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|v\\xi\\|=\\|e\\xi\\|\\)\">\\(\\|v\\xi\\|=\\|e\\xi\\|\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*v=e\\)\">\\(v^*v=e\\)<\/span>, while <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(fv=v\\)\">\\(fv=v\\)<\/span>. Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*\\le f\\)\">\\(vv^*\\le f\\)<\/span>, proving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\precsim f\\)\">\\(e\\precsim f\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>We will also use reversal of comparison on complements in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\precsim f\\)\">\\(e\\precsim f\\)<\/span>, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_1\\le f\\)\">\\(e_1\\le f\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_1\\sim e\\)\">\\(e_1\\sim e\\)<\/span>. Proposition 14.2 gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-e_1\\sim1-e\\)\">\\(1-e_1\\sim1-e\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-f\\le1-e_1\\)\">\\(1-f\\le1-e_1\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n1-f\\precsim1-e.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n1-f\\precsim1-e.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>7.2. Equivalent copies of orthogonal projections tend strongly to zero.<\/strong> Suppose that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_n\\in\\mathcal P(P)\\)\">\\(f_n\\in\\mathcal P(P)\\)<\/span> are pairwise orthogonal and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_n\\sim f_n\\)\">\\(g_n\\sim f_n\\)<\/span>. We claim that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_n\\to0\\)\">\\(g_n\\to0\\)<\/span> strongly.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\ge m\\)\">\\(n\\ge m\\)<\/span>, let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nr_{m,n}=\\bigvee_{j=m}^n g_j,\\qquad r_{m,m-1}=0,\n\\qquad d_{m,n}=r_{m,n}-r_{m,n-1}.\n\\]\">\\[\nr_{m,n}=\\bigvee_{j=m}^n g_j,\\qquad r_{m,m-1}=0,\n\\qquad d_{m,n}=r_{m,n}-r_{m,n-1}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe parallelogram law <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-04\">PT, Proposition 4.4<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nd_{m,n}\\sim g_n-(g_n\\wedge r_{m,n-1})\\precsim g_n\\sim f_n.\n\\]\">\\[\nd_{m,n}\\sim g_n-(g_n\\wedge r_{m,n-1})\\precsim g_n\\sim f_n.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>, the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_{m,n}\\)\">\\(d_{m,n}\\)<\/span> are orthogonal. Additivity of subequivalence <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-01\">PT, Lemma 3.3<\/a> yields\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nr_{m,n}\\precsim\\sum_{j=m}^n f_j\\le F_m,\n\\qquad F_m=\\sum_{j\\ge m}f_j.\n\\]\">\\[\nr_{m,n}\\precsim\\sum_{j=m}^n f_j\\le F_m,\n\\qquad F_m=\\sum_{j\\ge m}f_j.\n\\]<\/span>\nBy 7.1,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\np_m:=\\bigvee_{j\\ge m}g_j\\precsim F_m.\n\\]\">\\[\np_m:=\\bigvee_{j\\ge m}g_j\\precsim F_m.\n\\]<\/span>\nLet <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=\\bigwedge_m p_m\\)\">\\(p=\\bigwedge_m p_m\\)<\/span>. By (7.2),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n1-F_m\\precsim1-p_m\\le1-p.\n\\]\">\\[\n1-F_m\\precsim1-p_m\\le1-p.\n\\]<\/span>\nOrthogonality of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_n\\)\">\\(f_n\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_m\\downarrow0\\)\">\\(F_m\\downarrow0\\)<\/span> strongly, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-F_m\\uparrow1\\)\">\\(1-F_m\\uparrow1\\)<\/span>. Applying 7.1 again gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\precsim1-p\\)\">\\(1\\precsim1-p\\)<\/span>. Finiteness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p=0\\)\">\\(p=0\\)<\/span>: a subprojection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-p\\)\">\\(1-p\\)<\/span> equivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> must equal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_m\\downarrow0\\)\">\\(p_m\\downarrow0\\)<\/span> strongly. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_m\\le p_m\\)\">\\(g_m\\le p_m\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|g_m\\xi\\|\\le\\|p_m\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|g_m\\xi\\|\\le\\|p_m\\xi\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>7.3. Weak compactness of a normal state's conjugates.<\/strong> Choose a normal state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> on a nonzero finite algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, for example a unit-vector state in a faithful concrete representation. For every orthogonal sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((f_n)\\subseteq\\mathcal P(P)\\)\">\\((f_n)\\subseteq\\mathcal P(P)\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\sup_{u\\in\\mathcal U(P)}\\varphi(u^*f_nu)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\sup_{u\\in\\mathcal U(P)}\\varphi(u^*f_nu)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nOtherwise there are a subsequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_{n_k}\\)\">\\(f_{n_k}\\)<\/span>, unitaries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_k\\)\">\\(u_k\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span> for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(u_k^*f_{n_k}u_k)\\ge\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(\\varphi(u_k^*f_{n_k}u_k)\\ge\\varepsilon\\)<\/span>. The projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_k=u_k^*f_{n_k}u_k\\)\">\\(g_k=u_k^*f_{n_k}u_k\\)<\/span> are equivalent to the orthogonal projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_{n_k}\\)\">\\(f_{n_k}\\)<\/span>; (7.3) makes them converge strongly to zero. Their norms are bounded, so <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-09\">OT, Lemma 8.5<\/a> gives weak-* convergence to zero, contradicting normality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> be the weak closure in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_*\\)\">\\(P_*\\)<\/span> of the convex hull of\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\{\\varphi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\{\\varphi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe convex hull is norm bounded and satisfies (7.4) uniformly. Therefore <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/polar-decomposition-of-functionals-and-weak-compactness-in-preduals.html#OA-FND-PD-16\">PW, Theorem 10.2, implication (7)<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Rightarrow\\)\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)<\/span>(1)<\/a> makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> weakly compact. Positivity and value <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> at the identity are weakly closed conditions, so every element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is a normal state.<\/p>\n<p>The weak topology on the real self-adjoint part <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((P_*)_h\\)\">\\((P_*)_h\\)<\/span> is its real Banach-space weak topology, by Hahn–Banach extension and complexification of real functionals. Conjugation acts on this real Banach space by surjective linear isometries preserving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. The lemma in Step 6 gives a normal state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\in K\\)\">\\(\\tau\\in K\\)<\/span> invariant under all conjugations. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> unitary and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in P\\)\">\\(x\\in P\\)<\/span>, invariance applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xu\\)\">\\(xu\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\tau(ux)=\\tau\\bigl(u(xu)u^*\\bigr)=\\tau(xu).\n\\]\">\\[\n\\tau(ux)=\\tau\\bigl(u(xu)u^*\\bigr)=\\tau(xu).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe unitaries linearly span <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-09\">CF, Proposition 7.3<\/a>, so\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\tau(yx)=\\tau(xy)\\qquad(x,y\\in P).\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\tau(yx)=\\tau(xy)\\qquad(x,y\\in P).\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus every nonzero finite von Neumann algebra has a nonzero normal finite trace, normalized here to be a state. The trace construction uses the orbit idea of [L25, Propositions 3 and 6]; 7.1–7.2 give all comparison and limiting details needed for its Proposition 7.<\/p>\n<p><strong>7.4. Central supports and faithfulness.<\/strong> The support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\tau)\\)\">\\(s(\\tau)\\)<\/span> exists and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> is faithful on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\tau)Ps(\\tau)\\)\">\\(s(\\tau)Ps(\\tau)\\)<\/span> <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18\">UE, Lemma 11.1<\/a>. Unitary invariance and uniqueness of the support give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nus(\\tau)u^*=s(\\tau)\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\]\">\\[\nus(\\tau)u^*=s(\\tau)\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\]<\/span>\nSince unitaries span <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\tau)\\)\">\\(s(\\tau)\\)<\/span> is central.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a maximal family of normal tracial states whose nonzero central supports <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_i\\)\">\\(z_i\\)<\/span> are pairwise orthogonal. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-\\sum_i z_i\\ne0\\)\">\\(1-\\sum_i z_i\\ne0\\)<\/span>, apply 7.3 to that finite central summand and extend its trace by zero to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. Its nonzero support is orthogonal to every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_i\\)\">\\(z_i\\)<\/span>, contradicting maximality. Thus\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\sum_i z_i=1,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\sum_i z_i=1,\n\\]<\/span>\nand each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Pz_i\\)\">\\(Pz_i\\)<\/span> has a faithful normal tracial state.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> is countably decomposable, this family is countable. Choose strictly positive scalar weights <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_i\\)\">\\(\\lambda_i\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\lambda_i=1\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\lambda_i=1\\)<\/span>. The norm-convergent sum\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\tau_0=\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\tau_i\\in P_*\n\\]\">\\[\n\\tau_0=\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\tau_i\\in P_*\n\\]<\/span>\nis a normal tracial state. It is faithful: for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\ge0\\)\">\\(x\\ge0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_0(x)=0\\)\">\\(\\tau_0(x)=0\\)<\/span> forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_i(x)=0\\)\">\\(\\tau_i(x)=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xz_i=0\\)\">\\(xz_i=0\\)<\/span> by faithfulness on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Pz_i\\)\">\\(Pz_i\\)<\/span>, and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=0\\)\">\\(x=0\\)<\/span>. Consequently every countably decomposable finite von Neumann algebra has a faithful normal tracial state.<\/p>\n<h3>8. Replacing a spatial implementer when the commutant is finite<\/h3>\n<p>We prove a statement about normalizing operators. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(P\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span> be finite and countably decomposable, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=h^*\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(h=h^*\\in B(H)\\)<\/span> satisfy\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|h\\|\\le C,\\qquad uhu^*-h\\in P\\quad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|h\\|\\le C,\\qquad uhu^*-h\\in P\\quad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThen there exists <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=k^*\\in P&#x27;\\)\">\\(k=k^*\\in P&#x27;\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nk-h\\in P,\\qquad \\|k\\|\\le C.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\">\\[\nk-h\\in P,\\qquad \\|k\\|\\le C.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Take a faithful normal tracial state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> from Step 7. On the real vector space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_h\\)\">\\(P_h\\)<\/span>, set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\langle x,y\\rangle_2=\\tau(xy),\\qquad \\|x\\|_2=\\tau(x^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\langle x,y\\rangle_2=\\tau(xy),\\qquad \\|x\\|_2=\\tau(x^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe trace identity makes this a real symmetric bilinear form: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline{\\tau(xy)}=\\tau(yx)=\\tau(xy)\\)\">\\(\\overline{\\tau(xy)}=\\tau(yx)=\\tau(xy)\\)<\/span>. Faithfulness makes it positive definite. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(P,\\tau)_h\\)\">\\(L^2(P,\\tau)_h\\)<\/span> denote its real Hilbert completion, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j:P_h\\to L^2(P,\\tau)_h\\)\">\\(j:P_h\\to L^2(P,\\tau)_h\\)<\/span> the injective inclusion.<\/p>\n<p>Put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nc(u)=uhu^*-h\\in P_h,\n\\qquad T_u(y)=c(u)+uyu^*.\n\\]\">\\[\nc(u)=uhu^*-h\\in P_h,\n\\qquad T_u(y)=c(u)+uyu^*.\n\\]<\/span>\nConjugation preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\)<\/span>, by the trace property. Also\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nc(uv)=c(u)+uc(v)u^*.\n\\]\">\\[\nc(uv)=c(u)+uc(v)u^*.\n\\]<\/span>\nThus the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_u\\)\">\\(T_u\\)<\/span> extend to an action by affine isometries of the real Hilbert space. The orbit of zero is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{j(c(u))\\}\\)\">\\(\\{j(c(u))\\}\\)<\/span>, and it is bounded, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|c(u)\\|_2\\le\\|c(u)\\|\\le2C\\)\">\\(\\|c(u)\\|_2\\le\\|c(u)\\|\\le2C\\)<\/span>. Its Hilbert-norm closed convex hull <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> is nonempty, bounded, and invariant under every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_u\\)\">\\(T_u\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>8.1. A Hilbert-space circumcentre.<\/strong> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in S\\)\">\\(y\\in S\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nR(y)=\\sup_{s\\in S}\\|y-s\\|_2,\\qquad r=\\inf_{y\\in S}R(y).\n\\]\">\\[\nR(y)=\\sup_{s\\in S}\\|y-s\\|_2,\\qquad r=\\inf_{y\\in S}R(y).\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y,z\\in S\\)\">\\(y,z\\in S\\)<\/span>, the parallelogram identity gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nR\\!\\left(\\frac{y+z}{2}\\right)^2\n\\le\\frac{R(y)^2+R(z)^2}{2}-\\frac{\\|y-z\\|_2^2}{4}.\n\\]\">\\[\nR\\!\\left(\\frac{y+z}{2}\\right)^2\n\\le\\frac{R(y)^2+R(z)^2}{2}-\\frac{\\|y-z\\|_2^2}{4}.\n\\]<\/span>\nSince the midpoint belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|y-z\\|_2^2\\le2R(y)^2+2R(z)^2-4r^2.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|y-z\\|_2^2\\le2R(y)^2+2R(z)^2-4r^2.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nA sequence with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R(y_n)\\to r\\)\">\\(R(y_n)\\to r\\)<\/span> is therefore Cauchy. Its limit lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, and the inequality <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|R(y)-R(z)|\\le\\|y-z\\|_2\\)\">\\(|R(y)-R(z)|\\le\\|y-z\\|_2\\)<\/span> shows that it attains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>. Equation (8.3) proves uniqueness. As each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_u\\)\">\\(T_u\\)<\/span> is an isometry carrying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> onto itself, it preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> and hence fixes this unique point, say <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y_0\\)\">\\(y_0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>8.2. Recovering the operator norm bound.<\/strong> Let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\,w^*}\n\\{uhu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}\\subseteq B(H)_h.\n\\]\">\\[\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\,w^*}\n\\{uhu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}\\subseteq B(H)_h.\n\\]<\/span>\nIt is weak-* compact and contained in the operator-norm ball of radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. From (8.1),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK-h\\subseteq P_h,\\qquad \\|t\\|\\le2C\\quad(t\\in K-h).\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]\">\\[\nK-h\\subseteq P_h,\\qquad \\|t\\|\\le2C\\quad(t\\in K-h).\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe weak-* topology of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> is the subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>-weak topology from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span>, by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.html#OA-FND-LT-10\">OT, Theorem 9.4<\/a>. On a norm-bounded subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_h\\)\">\\(P_h\\)<\/span>, the map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> is continuous from weak-* to Hilbert-space weak topology. For a test vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(z)\\)\">\\(j(z)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in P_h\\)\">\\(z\\in P_h\\)<\/span>, this follows because\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\langle j(t),j(z)\\rangle_2=\\tau(tz)\n\\]\">\\[\n\\langle j(t),j(z)\\rangle_2=\\tau(tz)\n\\]<\/span>\nis a normal functional of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>. For an arbitrary Hilbert-space test vector, approximate it in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\)<\/span> by such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(z)\\)\">\\(j(z)\\)<\/span>; the approximation is uniform over (8.4), since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|j(t)\\|_2\\le2C\\)\">\\(\\|j(t)\\|_2\\le2C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>It follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(K-h)\\)\">\\(j(K-h)\\)<\/span> is weakly compact and convex in the Hilbert space, and hence norm closed. It contains the orbit of zero, so it contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y_0=j(t)\\)\">\\(y_0=j(t)\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in K-h\\)\">\\(t\\in K-h\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> is injective, the fixed-point equations for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y_0\\)\">\\(y_0\\)<\/span> give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nc(u)+utu^*=t\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\]\">\\[\nc(u)+utu^*=t\\qquad(u\\in\\mathcal U(P)).\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=h+t\\in K\\)\">\\(k=h+t\\in K\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(uku^*=k\\)\">\\(uku^*=k\\)<\/span> for all unitaries of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. Their linear span is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\in P&#x27;\\)\">\\(k\\in P&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|k\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|k\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>. This proves (8.2).<\/p>\n<p>Applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P=M&#x27;\\)\">\\(P=M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, using (5.4), this replaces the spatial implementer by a self-adjoint element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, without increasing its norm, whenever <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;\\)\">\\(M&#x27;\\)<\/span> is finite and countably decomposable.<\/p>\n<h3>9. Averaging in a countably decomposable type III algebra<\/h3>\n<p>We prove the exact averaging assertion needed for the type III case.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> be a countably decomposable type III von Neumann algebra, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=x^*\\in P\\)\">\\(x=x^*\\in P\\)<\/span>. There is a least self-adjoint central majorant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=b(x)\\)\">\\(b=b(x)\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|b\\|\\le\\|x\\|,\\qquad\nb\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|}\n\\{uxu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|b\\|\\le\\|x\\|,\\qquad\nb\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|}\n\\{uxu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(P)\\}.\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=0\\)\">\\(x=0\\)<\/span>, take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=0\\)\">\\(b=0\\)<\/span>. Otherwise let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R=\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(R=\\|x\\|\\)<\/span> and consider\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\mathcal C_x=\\{c\\in Z(P)_h:x\\le c\\le R1\\}.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\mathcal C_x=\\{c\\in Z(P)_h:x\\le c\\le R1\\}.\n\\]<\/span>\nIt is nonempty. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c,d\\in\\mathcal C_x\\)\">\\(c,d\\in\\mathcal C_x\\)<\/span>, the central projection\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ne=1_{(-\\infty,0]}(c-d)\n\\]\">\\[\ne=1_{(-\\infty,0]}(c-d)\n\\]<\/span>\nsatisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\min(c,d)=ec+(1-e)d\\ge x.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\min(c,d)=ec+(1-e)d\\ge x.\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal C_x\\)\">\\(\\mathcal C_x\\)<\/span> is downward directed, and its elements lie between <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-R1\\)\">\\(-R1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R1\\)\">\\(R1\\)<\/span>. The bounded decreasing net of these elements has a strong limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-spectral-theorem-for-bounded-self-adjoint-operators.html#OA-FND-ST-06\">ST, Theorem 6.1<\/a>. The centre is strongly closed, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in Z(P)_h\\)\">\\(b\\in Z(P)_h\\)<\/span>. Passing to the strong limit in the inequalities gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\le b\\le R1\\)\">\\(x\\le b\\le R1\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\le c\\)\">\\(b\\le c\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\in\\mathcal C_x\\)\">\\(c\\in\\mathcal C_x\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span> is any other central self-adjoint majorant of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\min(c,R1)\\in\\mathcal C_x\\)\">\\(\\min(c,R1)\\in\\mathcal C_x\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\le c\\)\">\\(b\\le c\\)<\/span>. This proves leastness and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|b\\|\\le R\\)\">\\(\\|b\\|\\le R\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>, set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\np=1_{[0,\\varepsilon)}(b-x),\\qquad q=1-p.\n\\]\">\\[\np=1_{[0,\\varepsilon)}(b-x),\\qquad q=1-p.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe central support of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. Otherwise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=1-c(p)\\ne0\\)\">\\(z=1-c(p)\\ne0\\)<\/span> is central and annihilates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. Spectral calculus then gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nz(b-x)\\ge\\varepsilon z.\n\\]\">\\[\nz(b-x)\\ge\\varepsilon z.\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b-\\varepsilon z\\)\">\\(b-\\varepsilon z\\)<\/span> is a central majorant of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> strictly smaller than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, a contradiction.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w=c(q)\\)\">\\(w=c(q)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\ne0\\)\">\\(w\\ne0\\)<\/span>, both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(wp\\)\">\\(wp\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> have central support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Pw\\)\">\\(Pw\\)<\/span>. Every nonzero projection of a type III algebra is properly infinite <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-09\">PT, Lemma 6.2(3) and Definition 7.1<\/a>. The algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Pw\\)\">\\(Pw\\)<\/span> is countably decomposable. Therefore <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-19\">PT, Proposition 15.2(4)<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nwp\\sim w\\sim q.\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\">\\[\nwp\\sim w\\sim q.\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor any integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\ge2\\)\">\\(n\\ge2\\)<\/span>, repeated halving <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-17\">PT, Proposition 13.4<\/a> partitions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span> into orthogonal projections\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nw=q_1+\\cdots+q_n,\\qquad q_j\\sim w.\n\\]\">\\[\nw=q_1+\\cdots+q_n,\\qquad q_j\\sim w.\n\\]<\/span>\nFor every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w-q_j\\sim w\\)\">\\(w-q_j\\sim w\\)<\/span>, since it contains one of the other <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_k\\sim w\\)\">\\(q_k\\sim w\\)<\/span> and is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span>; use projection Schröder–Bernstein <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-03\">PT, Proposition 5.1<\/a>. By (9.2),\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nq\\sim q_j,\\qquad wp=w-q\\sim w-q_j.\n\\]\">\\[\nq\\sim q_j,\\qquad wp=w-q\\sim w-q_j.\n\\]<\/span>\nAdd partial isometries implementing these two equivalences. Their orthogonal initial and final projections make their sum a unitary of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Pw\\)\">\\(Pw\\)<\/span> sending <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_j\\)\">\\(q_j\\)<\/span>. Extend it by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-w\\)\">\\(1-w\\)<\/span> to a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_j\\in P\\)\">\\(u_j\\in P\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Functional calculus gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n0\\le b-x\\le\\varepsilon1+2R q.\n\\]\">\\[\n0\\le b-x\\le\\varepsilon1+2R q.\n\\]<\/span>\nEach <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_j\\)\">\\(u_j\\)<\/span> commutes with the central element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, so\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n0\\le b-\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^nu_jxu_j^*\n\\le\\varepsilon1+\\frac{2R}{n}\\sum_{j=1}^nq_j\n=\\varepsilon1+\\frac{2R}{n}w.\n\\tag{9.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n0\\le b-\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^nu_jxu_j^*\n\\le\\varepsilon1+\\frac{2R}{n}\\sum_{j=1}^nq_j\n=\\varepsilon1+\\frac{2R}{n}w.\n\\tag{9.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w=0\\)\">\\(w=0\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=0\\)\">\\(q=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le b-x\\le\\varepsilon1\\)\">\\(0\\le b-x\\le\\varepsilon1\\)<\/span>, which gives the needed approximation with the identity unitary. For any prescribed error, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)<\/span> small and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> large in (9.3). This proves (9.1).<\/p>\n<h3>10. Replacing a spatial implementer when the commutant is type III<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=M&#x27;\\)<\/span> be countably decomposable and type III, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=h^*\\)\">\\(h=h^*\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>, be the spatial implementer from Step 5. We construct\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nh_0=h_0^*\\in M,\\qquad \\|h_0\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad h_0-h\\in N.\n\\tag{10.1}\n\\]\">\\[\nh_0=h_0^*\\in M,\\qquad \\|h_0\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad h_0-h\\in N.\n\\tag{10.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=0\\)\">\\(C=0\\)<\/span>, take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_0=0\\)\">\\(h_0=0\\)<\/span>. Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C&gt;0\\)\">\\(C&gt;0\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=h+C1\\)\">\\(b=h+C1\\)<\/span>, and define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\,w^*}\n\\{ubu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(N)\\}.\n\\]\">\\[\nK=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}^{\\,w^*}\n\\{ubu^*:u\\in\\mathcal U(N)\\}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThen\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK\\subseteq[0,2C1]\\cap(b+N)_h.\n\\tag{10.2}\n\\]\">\\[\nK\\subseteq[0,2C1]\\cap(b+N)_h.\n\\tag{10.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nAlso every element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)=Z(M)\\)\">\\(Z(N)=Z(M)\\)<\/span>, by (5.3). The set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is nonempty, weak-* compact, convex, and invariant under conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal U(N)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal U(N)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose an inclusion-minimal nonempty weak-* compact convex invariant subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\subseteq K\\)\">\\(L\\subseteq K\\)<\/span>. Zorn's lemma applies because an inclusion-decreasing chain has nonempty intersection by compactness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, and that intersection remains compact, convex, and invariant.<\/p>\n<p>For a central projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in N\\)\">\\(z\\in N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\in L\\)\">\\(k\\in L\\)<\/span>, the set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\{l\\in L:\\|lz\\|\\le\\|kz\\|\\}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\{l\\in L:\\|lz\\|\\le\\|kz\\|\\}\n\\]<\/span>\nis nonempty, weak-* compact, convex, and invariant. Weak-* closedness follows from lower semicontinuity of the norm and normality of multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>; invariance follows because the conjugating unitaries commute with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>. Minimality forces this set to be all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>. Interchanging <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k,l\\)\">\\(k,l\\)<\/span>, we obtain\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|kz\\|=\\|lz\\|\\qquad(k,l\\in L,\\ z\\in\\mathcal P(Z(N))).\n\\tag{10.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|kz\\|=\\|lz\\|\\qquad(k,l\\in L,\\ z\\in\\mathcal P(Z(N))).\n\\tag{10.3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The difference set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nV=L-L\n\\]\">\\[\nV=L-L\n\\]<\/span>\nis a weak-* compact convex symmetric invariant subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_h\\)\">\\(N_h\\)<\/span>, by (10.2). Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\in V\\)\">\\(d\\in V\\)<\/span> is nonzero. Replacing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-d\\)\">\\(-d\\)<\/span> if necessary, arrange that its positive part is nonzero. Apply Step 9 inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. The central majorant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_N(d)\\)\">\\(b_N(d)\\)<\/span> belongs to the norm-closed convex hull of the unitary orbit of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>, and hence to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_N(d)\\ge d\\)\">\\(b_N(d)\\ge d\\)<\/span>, it cannot be nonpositive. There are therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span> and a nonzero central spectral projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nb_N(d)z\\ge\\varepsilon z.\n\\]\">\\[\nb_N(d)z\\ge\\varepsilon z.\n\\]<\/span>\nWrite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_N(d)=k-l\\)\">\\(b_N(d)=k-l\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k,l\\in L\\)\">\\(k,l\\in L\\)<\/span>. The operators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k,l\\)\">\\(k,l\\)<\/span> are positive and commute with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>, so\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nkz\\ge lz+\\varepsilon z.\n\\]\">\\[\nkz\\ge lz+\\varepsilon z.\n\\]<\/span>\nOn the nonzero Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(zH\\)\">\\(zH\\)<\/span>, positivity implies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|kz\\|\\ge\\|lz+\\varepsilon z\\|=\\|lz\\|+\\varepsilon,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|kz\\|\\ge\\|lz+\\varepsilon z\\|=\\|lz\\|+\\varepsilon,\n\\]<\/span>\ncontradicting (10.3). Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(V=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> is a singleton, say <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{k\\}\\)\">\\(\\{k\\}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Its invariance makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> commute with every unitary of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, hence with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\in N&#x27;=M\\)\">\\(k\\in N&#x27;=M\\)<\/span>. By (10.2), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le k\\le2C1\\)\">\\(0\\le k\\le2C1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k-b\\in N\\)\">\\(k-b\\in N\\)<\/span>. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_0=k-C1\\)\">\\(h_0=k-C1\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-C1\\le h_0\\le C1\\)\">\\(-C1\\le h_0\\le C1\\)<\/span>, and (10.1) follows.<\/p>\n<p>The minimal-invariant-set argument is the part of [K66, Addendum, Theorem 15, p. 292] needed here. Its averaging input has been proved explicitly in Step 9.<\/p>\n<h3>11. Finite, properly infinite, and semifinite commutants<\/h3>\n<p>We now prove innerness, with the same bound <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, for every concrete algebra whose commutant is countably decomposable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>11.1. A full finite corner in a semifinite algebra.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> be semifinite. Every nonzero central summand of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> contains a nonzero finite projection. Indeed, by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-10\">PT, Definition 7.1 and Theorem 7.2<\/a>, a nonzero type I part contains an abelian projection, which is finite by Lemma 6.2; a nonzero type II part contains a nonzero finite projection by the definition of type II. There is no type III central part in a semifinite algebra.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a maximal family of nonzero finite projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_j\\in N\\)\">\\(q_j\\in N\\)<\/span> whose central supports <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_j=c_N(q_j)\\)\">\\(z_j=c_N(q_j)\\)<\/span> are pairwise orthogonal. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=1-\\sum_jz_j\\ne0\\)\">\\(z=1-\\sum_jz_j\\ne0\\)<\/span>, a nonzero finite projection in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nz\\)\">\\(Nz\\)<\/span> would enlarge the family. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_jz_j=1\\)\">\\(\\sum_jz_j=1\\)<\/span>. The strong sum\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nq=\\sum_jq_j\n\\]\">\\[\nq=\\sum_jq_j\n\\]<\/span>\nis finite by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-09\">PT, Lemma 6.3<\/a>, which proves finiteness of centrally orthogonal sums of finite projections. Moreover,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nc_N(q)=1.\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]\">\\[\nc_N(q)=1.\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor if a central projection majorizes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>, it majorizes every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_j\\)\">\\(q_j\\)<\/span>, and therefore every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_j\\)\">\\(z_j\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>11.2. Compression of a spatial implementer.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, and suppose now that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is countably decomposable and semifinite. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> be a *-derivation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|D\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>, and take a spatial implementer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=h^*\\)\">\\(h=h^*\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|h\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>, from Step 5. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\in N\\)\">\\(q\\in N\\)<\/span> as in 11.1.<\/p>\n<p>By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04\">DC, Theorem 5.8; PT, Proposition 3.5(3)<\/a>, the map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\sigma:M\\longrightarrow B(qH),\\qquad \\sigma(x)=xq|_{qH},\n\\]\">\\[\n\\sigma:M\\longrightarrow B(qH),\\qquad \\sigma(x)=xq|_{qH},\n\\]<\/span>\nhas a von Neumann algebra as its image, with commutant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(qNq\\)\">\\(qNq\\)<\/span>, and its kernel is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M(1-c_N(q))=0\\)\">\\(M(1-c_N(q))=0\\)<\/span>. It is therefore an isometric *-isomorphism onto its image, and is normal in both directions by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18\">UE, Corollary 11.4<\/a>. Transport <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma(M)\\)\">\\(\\sigma(M)\\)<\/span>, writing\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD_q(\\sigma(x))=\\sigma(D(x)).\n\\]\">\\[\nD_q(\\sigma(x))=\\sigma(D(x)).\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_q=qhq|_{qH}\\)\">\\(h_q=qhq|_{qH}\\)<\/span>, direct multiplication, using <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xq=qx\\)\">\\(xq=qx\\)<\/span>, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\ni[h_q,\\sigma(x)]=iq[h,x]q|_{qH}\n=\\sigma(D(x)).\n\\tag{11.2}\n\\]\">\\[\ni[h_q,\\sigma(x)]=iq[h,x]q|_{qH}\n=\\sigma(D(x)).\n\\tag{11.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nAlso <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h_q\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|h_q\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(qNq\\)\">\\(qNq\\)<\/span> is finite, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> is a finite projection, and is countably decomposable, because any orthogonal family in it is an orthogonal family in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. From (11.2), the commutator calculation following (5.3), now in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(qH)\\)\">\\(B(qH)\\)<\/span>, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nvh_qv^*-h_q\\in qNq\\qquad(v\\in\\mathcal U(qNq)).\n\\]\">\\[\nvh_qv^*-h_q\\in qNq\\qquad(v\\in\\mathcal U(qNq)).\n\\]<\/span>\nStep 8 therefore provides a self-adjoint\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nk_q\\in(qNq)&#x27;=\\sigma(M),\\qquad \\|k_q\\|\\le C,\n\\]\">\\[\nk_q\\in(qNq)&#x27;=\\sigma(M),\\qquad \\|k_q\\|\\le C,\n\\]<\/span>\nwith <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k_q-h_q\\in qNq\\)\">\\(k_q-h_q\\in qNq\\)<\/span>. It implements <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_q\\)\">\\(D_q\\)<\/span>. Its inverse image <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=\\sigma^{-1}(k_q)\\)\">\\(k=\\sigma^{-1}(k_q)\\)<\/span> is self-adjoint, has norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, and satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nD(x)=i[k,x]\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]\">\\[\nD(x)=i[k,x]\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]<\/span>\nby injectivity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>This argument includes properly infinite semifinite algebras, of types I and II. In the finite case one may take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=1\\)\">\\(q=1\\)<\/span>, reducing directly to Step 8. No trace on the original semifinite algebra is assumed or used; the finite trace is constructed on the finite corner.<\/p>\n<p><strong>11.3. The complete countably decomposable-commutant case.<\/strong> For an arbitrary countably decomposable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.html#OA-FND-TY-10\">PT, Theorem 7.2<\/a> first splits its identity by central projections into a finite part and a properly infinite part. For completeness, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1=e_f+e_p\\)\">\\(1=e_f+e_p\\)<\/span> with orthogonal central supports, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c(e_f)=c(e_f)(e_f+e_p)=e_f\\)\">\\(c(e_f)=c(e_f)(e_f+e_p)=e_f\\)<\/span>, and similarly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_p\\)\">\\(e_p\\)<\/span> is central. The type decomposition then splits the properly infinite part into its semifinite and type III parts. Thus there are pairwise orthogonal central projections\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nz_f+z_s+z_3=1\n\\]\">\\[\nz_f+z_s+z_3=1\n\\]<\/span>\nwith <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nz_f\\)\">\\(Nz_f\\)<\/span> finite, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nz_s\\)\">\\(Nz_s\\)<\/span> properly infinite and semifinite, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nz_3\\)\">\\(Nz_3\\)<\/span> type III; zero parts are omitted.<\/p>\n<p>These projections belong to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(M)\\)\">\\(Z(M)\\)<\/span>. On each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_jH\\)\">\\(z_jH\\)<\/span>, the algebras <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Mz_j\\)\">\\(Mz_j\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Nz_j\\)\">\\(Nz_j\\)<\/span> are mutual commutants <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04\">DC, Theorem 5.8<\/a>. Each commutant remains countably decomposable. Equation (5.3) makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(hz_j\\)\">\\(hz_j\\)<\/span> a self-adjoint spatial implementer on that subspace, of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Apply Step 8 on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_f\\)\">\\(z_f\\)<\/span>, 11.2 on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_s\\)\">\\(z_s\\)<\/span>, and Step 10 on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_3\\)\">\\(z_3\\)<\/span>. The resulting self-adjoint implementers in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Mz_j\\)\">\\(Mz_j\\)<\/span> all have norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Step 2 combines them into one self-adjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\in M\\)\">\\(k\\in M\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|k\\|\\le C\\)\">\\(\\|k\\|\\le C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>We have proved:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\boxed{\\ M&#x27;\\text{ countably decomposable},\\ D\\text{ a *-derivation}\n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ D=i[k,\\cdot],\\quad k=k^*\\in M,\n\\quad\\|k\\|\\le\\|D\\|.\\ }\n\\tag{11.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\ M&#x27;\\text{ countably decomposable},\\ D\\text{ a *-derivation}\n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ D=i[k,\\cdot],\\quad k=k^*\\in M,\n\\quad\\|k\\|\\le\\|D\\|.\\ }\n\\tag{11.3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<h3>12. Arbitrary central sums through faithful cyclic representations<\/h3>\n<p>An arbitrary von Neumann algebra need not be a central sum of countably decomposable algebras. The reduction needed here is instead to faithful cyclic representations of central summands; their <strong>commutants<\/strong> are countably decomposable. We prove that reduction.<\/p>\n<p>Fix a concrete <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)\">\\(M\\subseteq B(H)\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M&#x27;\\)\">\\(N=M&#x27;\\)<\/span>. For a nonzero vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\)<\/span> be the projection onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([M\\xi]\\)\">\\([M\\xi]\\)<\/span>. This subspace reduces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\in N\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\in N\\)<\/span>. Put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nz_\\xi=c_N(p_\\xi)\\in Z(N)=Z(M).\n\\]\">\\[\nz_\\xi=c_N(p_\\xi)\\in Z(N)=Z(M).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe induction results <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-double-commutant-theorem.html#OA-FND-BI-04\">DC, Theorem 5.8; PT, Proposition 3.5(3)<\/a>, together with normality of *-isomorphisms <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.html#OA-FND-WA-18\">UE, Corollary 11.4<\/a>, give a faithful normal isometric representation\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\rho_\\xi:Mz_\\xi\\longrightarrow B(p_\\xi H),\n\\qquad \\rho_\\xi(x)=xp_\\xi|_{p_\\xi H},\n\\tag{12.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\rho_\\xi:Mz_\\xi\\longrightarrow B(p_\\xi H),\n\\qquad \\rho_\\xi(x)=xp_\\xi|_{p_\\xi H},\n\\tag{12.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhose image is a von Neumann algebra with commutant\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\rho_\\xi(Mz_\\xi)&#x27;=p_\\xi Np_\\xi.\n\\tag{12.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\rho_\\xi(Mz_\\xi)&#x27;=p_\\xi Np_\\xi.\n\\tag{12.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> is cyclic for the image in (12.1). It is separating for the algebra in (12.2): if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\xi=0\\)\">\\(t\\xi=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in p_\\xi Np_\\xi\\)\">\\(t\\in p_\\xi Np_\\xi\\)<\/span>, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nt(x\\xi)=x(t\\xi)=0\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]\">\\[\nt(x\\xi)=x(t\\xi)=0\\qquad(x\\in M),\n\\]<\/span>\nand these vectors have dense span in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi H\\)\">\\(p_\\xi H\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=0\\)\">\\(t=0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>It follows directly that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi Np_\\xi\\)\">\\(p_\\xi Np_\\xi\\)<\/span> is countably decomposable. Indeed, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_j)\\)\">\\((e_j)\\)<\/span> is an orthogonal family of nonzero projections in it, each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_j\\xi\\ne0\\)\">\\(e_j\\xi\\ne0\\)<\/span>, while\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in F}\\|e_j\\xi\\|^2\\le\\|\\xi\\|^2\n\\]\">\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in F}\\|e_j\\xi\\|^2\\le\\|\\xi\\|^2\n\\]<\/span>\nfor every finite subfamily <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>. A family of strictly positive real numbers with bounded finite subsums is countable: for each positive integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, only finitely many terms can exceed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span>, and every positive term exceeds <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. Thus the family of projections is countable.<\/p>\n<p>Choose, by Zorn's lemma, a maximal family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((\\xi_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> for which the nonzero central projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_i=z_{\\xi_i}\\)\">\\(z_i=z_{\\xi_i}\\)<\/span> are pairwise orthogonal. Their strong sum is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. To see this, if\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nz=1-\\sum_i z_i\\ne0,\n\\]\">\\[\nz=1-\\sum_i z_i\\ne0,\n\\]<\/span>\nchoose a nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in zH\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in zH\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> is central, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\xi\\subseteq zH\\)\">\\(M\\xi\\subseteq zH\\)<\/span>; thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\le z\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\le z\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\ne z_\\xi\\le z\\)\">\\(0\\ne z_\\xi\\le z\\)<\/span>. This contradicts maximality.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> be a *-derivation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, and set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=\\|D\\|\\)\">\\(C=\\|D\\|\\)<\/span>. Step 2 restricts it to each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Mz_i\\)\">\\(Mz_i\\)<\/span>, with norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Transport that restriction through the isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_{\\xi_i}\\)\">\\(\\rho_{\\xi_i}\\)<\/span>. Its image algebra has countably decomposable commutant by (12.2), so (11.3) gives a self-adjoint implementer of norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> there. Pull it back through <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho_{\\xi_i}\\)\">\\(\\rho_{\\xi_i}\\)<\/span>, obtaining\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nh_i=h_i^*\\in Mz_i,\\qquad \\|h_i\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad D|_{Mz_i}=i[h_i,\\cdot].\n\\]\">\\[\nh_i=h_i^*\\in Mz_i,\\qquad \\|h_i\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad D|_{Mz_i}=i[h_i,\\cdot].\n\\]<\/span>\nThe uniform central-sum construction of Step 2 applies to the arbitrary index set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>. It gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nh=\\sum_i h_i\\in M_h,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad D(x)=i[h,x]\\quad(x\\in M).\n\\tag{12.3}\n\\]\">\\[\nh=\\sum_i h_i\\in M_h,\\qquad \\|h\\|\\le C,\n\\qquad D(x)=i[h,x]\\quad(x\\in M).\n\\tag{12.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is the central-cyclic reduction in [K66, Addendum, Theorem 15, pp. 291–292], with the countability assertion made on the commutants and the uniform summation estimate given explicitly.<\/p>\n<h3>13. General derivations, the bound, and uniqueness<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span> now be an arbitrary complex-linear derivation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. It is bounded by Step 1. The *-derivations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\)\">\\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\)<\/span> in 1.1 satisfy (1.1). By (12.3), choose\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nh_j=h_j^*\\in M,\\qquad\n\\delta_j=i[h_j,\\cdot],\\qquad\n\\|h_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\n\\quad(j=1,2).\n\\]\">\\[\nh_j=h_j^*\\in M,\\qquad\n\\delta_j=i[h_j,\\cdot],\\qquad\n\\|h_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta_j\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\n\\quad(j=1,2).\n\\]<\/span>\nThen\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\delta=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2=[ih_1-h_2,\\cdot].\n\\]\">\\[\n\\delta=\\delta_1+i\\delta_2=[ih_1-h_2,\\cdot].\n\\]<\/span>\nThus\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\na=ih_1-h_2\\in M,\\qquad\n\\|a\\|\\le\\|h_1\\|+\\|h_2\\|\\le2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\na=ih_1-h_2\\in M,\\qquad\n\\|a\\|\\le\\|h_1\\|+\\|h_2\\|\\le2\\|\\delta\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\)\">\\(\\delta\\)<\/span> itself preserves the involution, (12.3) directly gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=ih\\)\">\\(a=ih\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=h^*\\)\">\\(h=h^*\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|h\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\\)\">\\(\\|h\\|\\le\\|\\delta\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Finally,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n[a,x]=[b,x]\\text{ for every }x\\in M\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n[a-b,x]=0\\text{ for every }x\\in M\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-b\\in Z(M).\n\\]\">\\[\n[a,x]=[b,x]\\text{ for every }x\\in M\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n[a-b,x]=0\\text{ for every }x\\in M\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-b\\in Z(M).\n\\]<\/span>\nThis proves uniqueness modulo the centre and its converse. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> is any implementer and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> is one of the bounded implementers just constructed, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b-a\\in Z(M)\\)\">\\(b-a\\in Z(M)\\)<\/span>, so\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,Z(M))\\le\\|a\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,Z(M))\\le\\|a\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nAll the stated conclusions follow. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<h2>Closedness under a finite-dimensional extension<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Lemma.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a normed space, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\subseteq E\\)\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)<\/span> a closed linear subspace and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\subseteq E\\)\">\\(V\\subseteq E\\)<\/span> a finite-dimensional subspace. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F+V\\)\">\\(F+V\\)<\/span> is closed in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q:E\\to E/F\\)\">\\(q:E\\to E/F\\)<\/span> for the quotient map. The formula\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|q(x)\\|=\\inf_{f\\in F}\\|x-f\\|\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|q(x)\\|=\\inf_{f\\in F}\\|x-f\\|\n\\]<\/span>\nis independent of the representative, since translating <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> by an element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> merely permutes the elements over which the infimum is taken. It defines a norm: the triangle inequality follows by adding approximants in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, and homogeneity follows by rescaling them; zero norm implies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\overline F=F\\)\">\\(x\\in\\overline F=F\\)<\/span>. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|q(x)\\|\\le\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|q(x)\\|\\le\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> is continuous. The image <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(V)\\)\">\\(q(V)\\)<\/span> is finite-dimensional and is therefore closed in the normed, hence Hausdorff, space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E/F\\)\">\\(E/F\\)<\/span>, by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/hahn-banach-baire-and-the-basic-theorems-on-banach-spaces.html#OA-FND-HB-07\">HB, Theorem 7.1<\/a>(2). Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F+V=q^{-1}(q(V))\\)\">\\(F+V=q^{-1}(q(V))\\)<\/span>, the desired sum is closed. No completeness assumption on the quotient is needed. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In Step 1, faithful representation is isometric by <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#OA-FND-CF-12\">CF, Corollary 4.6<\/a>, so the represented C*-algebra is closed in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span>. Apply the lemma with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=B(H)\\)\">\\(E=B(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F=A\\)\">\\(F=A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(V=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h2>Compactness, intersections and convergent subnets<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Lemma.<\/strong> A family of closed subsets of a compact space has nonempty intersection if every finite subfamily has nonempty intersection. Every net in a compact space has a convergent subnet. Neither assertion requires metrizability or a countable index set.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> If the closed sets had empty intersection, their open complements would cover the space. A finite subcover would contradict the finite intersection assumption.<\/p>\n<p>For a net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((x_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((x_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> in a compact space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_i\\)\">\\(F_i\\)<\/span> be the closure of its tail <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{x_j:j\\ge i\\}\\)\">\\(\\{x_j:j\\ge i\\}\\)<\/span>. These nonempty closed sets have the finite intersection property, because any finite set of indices has a common upper bound. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\bigcap_iF_i\\)\">\\(x\\in\\bigcap_iF_i\\)<\/span>. Every open neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> meets every tail.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> consist of pairs <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((i,U)\\)\">\\((i,U)\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> is an open neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_i\\in U\\)\">\\(x_i\\in U\\)<\/span>. Order it by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((i,U)\\preceq(j,V)\\)\">\\((i,U)\\preceq(j,V)\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\le j\\)\">\\(i\\le j\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\subseteq U\\)\">\\(V\\subseteq U\\)<\/span>. It is directed: given two pairs, choose an upper bound of their indices and then a later term lying in the intersection of their neighbourhoods. The projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\to I\\)\">\\(D\\to I\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((i,U)\\mapsto i\\)\">\\((i,U)\\mapsto i\\)<\/span>, is order-preserving and cofinal, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((i,K)\\in D\\)\">\\((i,K)\\in D\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span>. The resulting subnet converges to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>: after any pair whose neighbourhood is contained in a prescribed neighbourhood <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_0\\)\">\\(U_0\\)<\/span>, all subsequent terms lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_0\\)\">\\(U_0\\)<\/span>. Such a pair exists because every neighbourhood meets every tail. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/support/src/derivations-of-von-neumann-algebras-alternative.md","start_offset":645,"end_offset":51869,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
